INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_23_m_23_1_46',
  'Which component of an EC2 Auto Scaling group specifies the instance type, AMI, and other configuration details for new instances launched by the group?',
  'mcq',
  '["Scaling policy","Target tracking configuration","Launch template","Desired capacity"]'::json,
  '{2}'::int[],
  'Option C (Launch template) is the correct choice. A launch template is used by an EC2 Auto Scaling group to define the configuration parameters for launching EC2 instances, including the Amazon Machine Image (AMI) ID, instance type, key pair, security groups, and user data. Option A (Scaling policy) defines when and how the Auto Scaling group should scale. Option B (Target tracking configuration) is a type of scaling policy that adjusts capacity to maintain a specific metric at a target value. Option D (Desired capacity) is a setting within the Auto Scaling group that specifies the current number of instances the group should maintain.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain23_5',
  NOW(),
  69,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_23_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_23_m_23_1_47',
  'A development team uses Amazon DynamoDB for their application''s data store. The application experiences unpredictable traffic patterns with occasional sharp spikes. The team wants to ensure the database can handle these fluctuations with the LEAST operational overhead for capacity management. Which DynamoDB capacity mode should they choose?',
  'mcq',
  '["Provisioned capacity mode with manual adjustments","On-demand capacity mode","Provisioned capacity mode with Auto Scaling","Reserved capacity mode"]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (On-demand capacity mode) is the correct choice. DynamoDB On-demand capacity mode automatically adjusts read and write capacity to meet the application''s needs, making it ideal for unpredictable workloads with the least operational overhead. You only pay for the reads and writes your application performs. Option A (Provisioned capacity mode with manual adjustments) requires constant monitoring and manual intervention to adjust capacity, which is high operational overhead. Option C (Provisioned capacity mode with Auto Scaling) automatically adjusts capacity based on defined utilization targets, which reduces operational overhead compared to manual adjustments, but On-demand is even lower as it requires no configuration of scaling policies. Option D (Reserved capacity mode) is not a standard DynamoDB capacity mode; reserved capacity is typically a pricing model for other services like EC2.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain23_5',
  NOW(),
  70,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_23_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_23_m_23_1_48',
  'A web application running on Amazon EC2 instances behind an Elastic Load Balancer needs to scale based on CPU utilization to maintain a consistent user experience. The SysOps Administrator wants the scaling to be smooth and automatically adjust capacity to keep CPU utilization around 50%. Which EC2 Auto Scaling policy type is MOST appropriate for this requirement?',
  'mcq',
  '["Simple scaling policy","Step scaling policy","Scheduled scaling policy","Target tracking scaling policy"]'::json,
  '{3}'::int[],
  'Option D (Target tracking scaling policy) is the correct choice. Target tracking scaling policies are designed to maintain a specified metric (like CPU utilization) at a target value. Auto Scaling automatically adjusts the group''s capacity to keep the metric as close to the target as possible, providing smoother scaling and consistent performance with minimal configuration. Option A (Simple scaling policy) requires manual tuning of thresholds and creates step-function behavior, making it less ideal for maintaining a specific metric target smoothly. Option B (Step scaling policy) also uses thresholds and defines specific steps for scaling, which can be less smooth than target tracking. Option C (Scheduled scaling policy) is used for scaling based on predictable load changes over time, not real-time metric-based scaling.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain23_5',
  NOW(),
  71,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_23_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_23_m_23_1_49',
  'An Amazon RDS for MySQL database is experiencing storage capacity issues. The SysOps Administrator wants to ensure the database automatically scales its storage when needed, without manual intervention. Which feature should be enabled?',
  'mcq',
  '["Multi-AZ deployment","Read Replicas","Storage auto-scaling","Manual storage modification"]'::json,
  '{2}'::int[],
  'Option C (Storage auto-scaling) is the correct choice. Amazon RDS storage auto-scaling automatically scales up the storage capacity for your database instance when it detects that free storage is running low. This prevents storage-related outages and eliminates the need for manual intervention. Option A (Multi-AZ deployment) provides high availability and automatic failover, but it does not automatically scale storage. Option B (Read Replicas) are used to improve read performance and offload queries, not for automatic storage scaling. Option D (Manual storage modification) requires manual intervention, which goes against the requirement for automatic scaling.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain23_5',
  NOW(),
  72,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_23_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_23_m_23_1_50',
  'Which AWS service can automatically adjust the capacity of non-EC2 resources, such as Amazon DynamoDB tables, Amazon ECS services, or Amazon Aurora Replicas, based on demand?',
  'mcq',
  '["Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling","AWS Application Auto Scaling","AWS CloudFormation","Amazon CloudWatch"]'::json,
  '{1}'::int[],
  'Option B (AWS Application Auto Scaling) is the correct choice. AWS Application Auto Scaling is a web service that enables you to automatically scale various AWS resources beyond Amazon EC2 instances, including Amazon DynamoDB tables, Amazon ECS services, Amazon Aurora Replicas, and others. Option A (Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling) is specifically for scaling Amazon EC2 instances. Option C (AWS CloudFormation) is an infrastructure as code service for provisioning resources, not for dynamic scaling. Option D (Amazon CloudWatch) is a monitoring service that can provide metrics used by Auto Scaling, but it does not perform the scaling itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain23_5',
  NOW(),
  73,
