INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_24_m_24_1_51',
  'Which AWS service allows you to define your infrastructure as code using templates, enabling consistent provisioning and updating of resources?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudFormation","AWS Systems Manager","AWS Service Catalog","AWS CodeDeploy"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is AWS CloudFormation, which enables you to model, provision, and manage AWS and third-party resources by treating infrastructure as code. It uses templates to define the desired state of your resources, ensuring consistent deployments. Option B (AWS Systems Manager) is used for operational insights and automation, not primarily for infrastructure provisioning as code. Option C (AWS Service Catalog) allows organizations to create and manage catalogs of IT services that are approved for use on AWS, but it relies on underlying provisioning mechanisms like CloudFormation. Option D (AWS CodeDeploy) automates code deployments to various compute services, not infrastructure provisioning.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain24_5',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_24_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_24_m_24_1_52',
  'What is the primary purpose of an Amazon Machine Image (AMI) in AWS?',
  'mcq',
  '["To store application code for serverless functions.","To provide a template for launching Amazon EC2 instances.","To manage user permissions and access control for AWS resources.","To define network configurations for a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) states that an Amazon Machine Image (AMI) provides a template for launching Amazon EC2 instances. An AMI includes the operating system, application server, and applications required to launch an instance. Option A (To store application code for serverless functions) describes a use case for services like AWS Lambda, not AMIs. Option C (To manage user permissions and access control for AWS resources) is the function of AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM). Option D (To define network configurations for a Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)) is handled by VPC configurations, subnets, and route tables, not AMIs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain24_5',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_24_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_24_m_24_1_53',
  'A company wants to allow its developers to provision approved AWS resources without granting them full administrative access. Which AWS service should be used to create and manage a catalog of standardized, pre-approved resources?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudFormation","AWS Systems Manager","AWS Service Catalog","AWS OpsWorks"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is AWS Service Catalog, which enables organizations to create and manage catalogs of IT services that are approved for use on AWS. This allows users to quickly deploy only the approved services they need, while adhering to organizational standards. Option A (AWS CloudFormation) is used to provision and manage AWS resources as infrastructure as code, but it doesn''t provide a self-service catalog for end-users with restricted permissions. Option B (AWS Systems Manager) is for operational management and automation, not for creating a self-service catalog of provisionable resources. Option D (AWS OpsWorks) is a configuration management service that uses Chef or Puppet, primarily for automating server configuration and application deployment, not for a self-service catalog.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain24_5',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_24_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_24_m_24_1_54',
  'A company needs to standardize the build process for its Amazon EC2 instances, ensuring all new instances include a specific operating system, software packages, and security configurations. Which approach offers the LEAST operational overhead for creating these standardized builds?',
  'mcq',
  '["Use Amazon Machine Images (AMIs) created with EC2 Image Builder.","Manually install software on each EC2 instance after launch.","Write a custom script to install software during instance bootstrap.","Use AWS OpsWorks to manage software installation on instances."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) suggests using Amazon Machine Images (AMIs) created with EC2 Image Builder. EC2 Image Builder automates the creation, management, and deployment of customized, secure, and up-to-date server images, significantly reducing operational overhead compared to manual methods. Option B (Manually install software on each EC2 instance after launch) is highly manual, error-prone, and has high operational overhead. Option C (Write a custom script to install software during instance bootstrap) can automate some tasks but requires ongoing script maintenance and doesn''t provide a pre-baked, immutable image, increasing management complexity. Option D (Use AWS OpsWorks to manage software installation on instances) is a configuration management service, but EC2 Image Builder is specifically designed for automating image creation, which is a more direct and lower-overhead solution for standardized builds.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain24_5',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_24_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_24_m_24_1_55',
  'A SysOps team manages infrastructure using AWS CloudFormation. They observe that some resources are being modified manually outside of CloudFormation, leading to inconsistencies between the deployed infrastructure and the CloudFormation template. What is the BEST way to address this anti-pattern?',
  'mcq',
  '["Implement CloudFormation change sets for all future updates.","Use AWS Systems Manager Session Manager for all EC2 access.","Apply AWS Service Catalog to restrict resource provisioning.","Manually update the CloudFormation template to reflect the current state of the resources."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is the best way to address the anti-pattern of manual changes causing CloudFormation drift. By implementing CloudFormation change sets, all modifications to the infrastructure are previewed and applied through CloudFormation, ensuring the template remains consistent with the actual infrastructure. Option B (Use AWS Systems Manager Session Manager for all EC2 access) is a best practice for secure EC2 access but does not directly prevent or resolve CloudFormation drift. Option C (Apply AWS Service Catalog to restrict resource provisioning) helps standardize resource provisioning but does not prevent manual modifications to already deployed CloudFormation-managed resources. Option D (Manually update the CloudFormation template to reflect the current state of the resources) is a reactive measure that doesn''t prevent future drift and can be time-consuming and error-prone.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain24_5',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_24_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_24_m_24_1_56',
  'Which AWS service allows you to deploy and manage CloudFormation stacks across multiple AWS accounts and AWS Regions from a single operation?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudFormation","AWS Systems Manager State Manager","AWS CloudFormation StackSets","AWS CodeDeploy"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is AWS CloudFormation StackSets, which extends the functionality of CloudFormation by enabling you to provision, update, or delete stacks across multiple accounts and Regions with a single operation. Option A (AWS CloudFormation) manages stacks within a single account and Region. Option B (AWS Systems Manager State Manager) is for configuring and maintaining the state of EC2 instances and on-premises servers, not for multi-account/multi-Region CloudFormation deployments. Option D (AWS CodeDeploy) is for automating application deployments, not for managing CloudFormation stacks across accounts/Regions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain24_5',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_24_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_24_m_24_1_57',
  'Which AWS service is used to create infrastructure as code by writing code in familiar programming languages like Python or TypeScript, which then synthesizes into CloudFormation templates?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CDK","AWS CloudFormation","AWS Lambda","AWS CodeDeploy"]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) is AWS CDK (Cloud Development Kit). AWS CDK allows developers to define cloud infrastructure using familiar programming languages, which then synthesizes into AWS CloudFormation templates for deployment. Option B (AWS CloudFormation) uses declarative JSON or YAML templates directly, not programming languages. Option C (AWS Lambda) is a serverless compute service, not an infrastructure as code tool. Option D (AWS CodeDeploy) is for automating application deployments, not for defining infrastructure as code.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain24_5',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_24_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_24_m_24_1_58',
  'A development team needs a consistent and repeatable way to provision a specific set of development resources, including EC2 instances and an Amazon RDS database, without manual configuration. They want to ensure these resources adhere to company standards. Which AWS service should they use?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Systems Manager Run Command","AWS Service Catalog","AWS CodeDeploy","AWS Lambda"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS Service Catalog. It allows organizations to create and manage catalogs of IT services that are approved for use on AWS. This enables developers to provision pre-approved stacks of resources (like EC2 and RDS) consistently and repeatedly, adhering to company standards. Option A (AWS Systems Manager Run Command) is for executing commands on EC2 instances, not for provisioning infrastructure. Option C (AWS CodeDeploy) is for automating application deployments, not for provisioning infrastructure. Option D (AWS Lambda) is a serverless compute service used for running code in response to events, not for defining and provisioning infrastructure stacks.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain24_5',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_24_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_24_m_24_1_59',
  'What is the main benefit of using AWS CloudFormation for provisioning AWS resources?',
  'mcq',
  '["It allows for manual, granular control over each resource''s configuration.","It enables defining infrastructure as code, ensuring consistent and repeatable deployments.","It provides a graphical user interface for drag-and-drop resource creation.","It automatically optimizes resource costs based on usage patterns."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) highlights the primary benefit of AWS CloudFormation: it allows you to define your infrastructure as code using templates, ensuring consistent, repeatable, and version-controlled deployments across environments. Option A describes a manual approach, which CloudFormation aims to automate. Option C is not a core feature of CloudFormation; while some tools might offer visual builders, CloudFormation itself is template-driven. Option D describes services like AWS Cost Explorer or AWS Budgets, not CloudFormation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain24_5',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_24_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_24_m_24_1_60',
  'Which AWS service allows organizations to create and manage a portfolio of approved IT services, enabling end-users to self-provision these services while adhering to governance rules?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudFormation","AWS Service Catalog","AWS Systems Manager","AWS OpsWorks"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS Service Catalog, which enables organizations to create and manage catalogs of IT services that are approved for use on AWS. These services can include everything from virtual machine images, servers, software, databases, to complete multi-tier application architectures. Option A, AWS CloudFormation, is for provisioning infrastructure as code. Option C, AWS Systems Manager, is a suite of operational tools. Option D, AWS OpsWorks, is a configuration management service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain24_5',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_24_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_24_m_24_1_61',
  'What is the primary use case for Amazon Machine Images (AMIs) in AWS?',
  'mcq',
  '["To store application logs for auditing purposes.","To serve as a template for launching EC2 instances with a standardized configuration.","To provide a managed relational database service.","To automate the deployment of application code."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) describes the primary use case for Amazon Machine Images (AMIs). An AMI provides the information required to launch an EC2 instance, including the operating system, application server, and applications, ensuring standardization and consistency across instances. Option A describes services like Amazon CloudWatch Logs or AWS CloudTrail. Option C describes Amazon RDS. Option D describes AWS CodeDeploy.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain24_5',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_24_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_24_m_24_1_62',
  'What is the purpose of a CloudFormation Change Set?',
  'mcq',
  '["To manually modify resources outside of CloudFormation control.","To preview the changes that CloudFormation will make to your stack before executing them.","To revert a stack to a previous state after a failed deployment.","To detect and report configuration drift in a CloudFormation stack after changes are applied."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) accurately describes the purpose of a CloudFormation Change Set. It allows you to see how proposed changes to your stack template will affect your running resources before you implement them, helping to prevent unintended modifications. Option A is an anti-pattern (ap-3.1-001) that leads to stack drift. Option C describes stack rollback functionality. Option D describes CloudFormation drift detection, which identifies changes made outside of CloudFormation, but a Change Set is for *previewing* intended changes.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain24_5',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_24_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_24_m_24_1_63',
  'A company needs to provision a standardized set of AWS resources, such as a VPC, subnets, and security groups, consistently across multiple AWS accounts and Regions. Which AWS CloudFormation feature is designed for this purpose?',
  'mcq',
  '["CloudFormation Change Sets","CloudFormation Drift Detection","CloudFormation StackSets","AWS Service Catalog"]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) is CloudFormation StackSets, which extends the functionality of stacks by enabling you to provision common AWS resources across multiple accounts and Regions with a single CloudFormation template. Option A, CloudFormation Change Sets, are for previewing changes to a single stack. Option B, CloudFormation Drift Detection, is for identifying manual changes to resources. Option D, AWS Service Catalog, allows end-users to self-provision approved IT services, but StackSets is specifically for multi-account/multi-Region provisioning of CloudFormation templates.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain24_5',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_24_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_24_m_24_1_64',
  'What is the primary difference between an EC2 Launch Template and an EC2 Launch Configuration?',
  'mcq',
  '["Launch Configurations support multiple instance types, while Launch Templates support only one.","Launch Templates are used for manual instance launches, while Launch Configurations are for Auto Scaling Groups.","Launch Templates support multiple versions and advanced features, while Launch Configurations are an older, immutable option primarily for Auto Scaling Groups.","Launch Configurations allow specifying Spot Instance requests, while Launch Templates do not."]'::json,
  '{2}',
  'Option C (correct) accurately describes the primary difference. EC2 Launch Templates are the newer, preferred method for defining instance launch parameters. They support versioning, mixed instance types within an Auto Scaling Group, and advanced features like Spot Instance requests and dedicated hosts. EC2 Launch Configurations are an older, immutable option primarily used with Auto Scaling Groups and lack many of the advanced features of Launch Templates. Option A is incorrect; Launch Templates support mixed instance types. Option B is incorrect; both can be used with Auto Scaling Groups, and Launch Templates can also be used for manual launches. Option D is incorrect; Launch Templates fully support Spot Instance requests.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain24_5',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_24_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_24_m_24_1_65',
  'A development team frequently provisions new testing environments that require a consistent set of AWS resources. They want to empower developers to self-provision these environments while ensuring compliance with organizational standards and cost controls. Which service should be used?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudFormation directly, allowing developers to write their own templates.","AWS Service Catalog, providing pre-approved CloudFormation templates for self-provisioning.","AWS Systems Manager Run Command, to execute scripts for environment setup.","Manually provision resources via the AWS Management Console to ensure control."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS Service Catalog. It allows the central IT team to create and manage a catalog of approved AWS resources (products), often defined by CloudFormation templates. Developers can then self-provision these pre-approved environments, ensuring compliance and cost controls while reducing operational overhead for the central team. Option A, allowing developers to write their own CloudFormation templates, might lead to inconsistent environments and lack of governance. Option C, AWS Systems Manager Run Command, is for executing commands on existing instances, not for provisioning entire environments. Option D, manual provisioning, is inefficient, error-prone, and does not ensure compliance or cost control.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain24_5',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_24_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_24_m_24_1_66',
  'Which AWS service enables you to define your cloud infrastructure as code using familiar programming languages like Python, TypeScript, Java, and C#?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CloudFormation","AWS CDK (Cloud Development Kit)","AWS CodeDeploy","AWS OpsWorks"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is the AWS Cloud Development Kit (CDK). The AWS CDK is an open-source software development framework to define your cloud application resources using familiar programming languages. It synthesizes into CloudFormation templates for deployment. Option A, AWS CloudFormation, uses declarative JSON or YAML templates. Option C, AWS CodeDeploy, is for application deployment. Option D, AWS OpsWorks, is a configuration management service that uses Chef or Puppet.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain24_5',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_24_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_24_m_24_1_67',
  'A SysOps team needs to ensure that all newly launched EC2 instances conform to a specific baseline configuration, including the operating system, pre-installed applications, and security settings. Which AWS resource is fundamental for achieving this standardization?',
  'mcq',
  '["An EC2 Key Pair","An Amazon Machine Image (AMI)","An Amazon EBS Snapshot","An EC2 Security Group"]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is an Amazon Machine Image (AMI). An AMI provides the information required to launch an EC2 instance, including the root volume template (operating system, application server, and applications) and launch permissions, making it fundamental for standardizing EC2 instance configurations. Option A, an EC2 Key Pair, is for secure SSH access. Option C, an Amazon EBS Snapshot, is a point-in-time backup of an EBS volume, not a launch template for an instance. Option D, an EC2 Security Group, acts as a virtual firewall for instances, controlling inbound and outbound traffic, but does not define the instance''s baseline software configuration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain24_5',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_24_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_24_m_24_1_68',
  'Which AWS service allows you to define, provision, and update infrastructure resources in a declarative way, treating infrastructure as code?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeDeploy for automating application deployments.","AWS Service Catalog for creating and managing catalogs of IT services.","AWS Systems Manager for operational tasks and automation.","AWS CloudFormation."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) is AWS CloudFormation, which enables you to model your entire AWS infrastructure in text files (templates) and provision it in an automated, secure, and repeatable manner. This approach treats infrastructure as code. Option A (AWS CodeDeploy) is for application deployment. Option B (AWS Service Catalog) is for self-service provisioning of approved resources. Option C (AWS Systems Manager) is for operational management and automation of instances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain24_5',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_24_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_24_m_24_1_69',
  'What is the primary benefit of using AWS CloudFormation change sets?',
  'mcq',
  '["To roll back a stack to a previous state in case of deployment failures.","To deploy applications to Amazon EC2 instances automatically.","To detect configuration drift in a stack by comparing it to the template.","To preview proposed changes to a stack before applying them, allowing you to see how your changes might impact running resources."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) describes the primary purpose of AWS CloudFormation change sets. A change set allows you to preview the changes that CloudFormation will make to your stack before you execute them, helping you understand the potential impact of your updates. Option A describes stack rollback, which is a different CloudFormation feature. Option B describes AWS CodeDeploy. Option C describes CloudFormation drift detection.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain24_5',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_24_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_24_m_24_1_70',
  'A SysOps team wants to provide developers with a self-service portal to provision pre-approved AWS resources, such as specific Amazon EC2 instance types with a standardized Amazon Machine Image (AMI), while ensuring governance and compliance. Which AWS service should they use?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS CodeDeploy for automating application deployments.","AWS Service Catalog.","AWS CloudFormation for defining infrastructure as code templates.","AWS Systems Manager for operational tasks."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS Service Catalog, which allows organizations to create and manage catalogs of IT services that are approved for use on AWS. These services can include everything from virtual machine images, servers, software, and databases to complete multi-tier application architectures. It enables self-service provisioning while enforcing governance. Option A (AWS CodeDeploy) is for application deployment. Option C (AWS CloudFormation) is used to define the underlying templates for Service Catalog products, but Service Catalog provides the self-service portal and governance layer. Option D (AWS Systems Manager) is for operational management, not self-service provisioning.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain24_5',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_24_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_24_m_24_1_71',
  'A SysOps Administrator discovers that manual modifications have been made to an Amazon EC2 instance that was originally provisioned using AWS CloudFormation, causing its configuration to deviate from the template. Which CloudFormation feature helps identify this discrepancy?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Systems Manager State Manager for maintaining desired configurations.","AWS CloudFormation drift detection.","AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager for automated OS updates.","AWS CloudFormation change sets for previewing updates."]'::json,
  '{1}',
  'Option B (correct) is AWS CloudFormation drift detection. This feature allows you to identify when the actual configuration of stack resources deviates from their expected configuration in the CloudFormation template. This addresses the anti-pattern (ap-3.1-001) of making manual changes to CloudFormation-managed resources. Option A (AWS Systems Manager State Manager) is for configuration compliance on instances but is not a CloudFormation feature for stack-level drift. Option C (AWS Systems Manager Patch Manager) is for patching. Option D (AWS CloudFormation change sets) are for previewing planned updates, not detecting unplanned changes.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain24_5',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_24_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_24_m_24_1_72',
  'Which AWS service is primarily used for automating application deployments to various compute services like Amazon EC2, AWS Lambda, and Amazon ECS, handling tasks such as rolling updates and rollbacks?',
  'mcq',
  '["AWS Service Catalog for standardized resource provisioning.","AWS CloudFormation for infrastructure as code.","AWS Systems Manager Run Command for executing scripts on instances.","AWS CodeDeploy."]'::json,
  '{3}',
  'Option D (correct) is AWS CodeDeploy, a service that automates code deployments to any instance, including Amazon EC2 instances and instances running on-premises. It can deploy applications to various compute services, manage rolling updates, and facilitate rollbacks. Option A (AWS Service Catalog) is for provisioning IT services. Option B (AWS CloudFormation) is for provisioning infrastructure. Option C (AWS Systems Manager Run Command) is for executing commands on instances, not primarily for full application lifecycle deployments with strategies like rolling updates.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain24_5',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_24_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_24_m_24_1_73',
  'What is the main advantage of using EC2 Launch Templates over EC2 Launch Configurations for launching Amazon EC2 instances?',
  'mcq',
  '["Launch Templates support more instance types, purchasing options (e.g., Spot and On-Demand in a single Auto Scaling group), and advanced features like T2/T3 Unlimited.","Launch Templates are simpler to create and manage for basic Auto Scaling groups.","Launch Templates integrate directly with AWS Service Catalog but Launch Configurations do not.","Launch Templates only support On-Demand instances, unlike Launch Configurations which support Spot Instances."]'::json,
  '{0}',
  'Option A (correct) highlights the key advantages of EC2 Launch Templates. They offer more features and flexibility compared to Launch Configurations, including support for multiple instance types, purchasing options (On-Demand, Spot, RIs) within a single Auto Scaling group, and advanced features like T2/T3 Unlimited. Option B is incorrect; while both are relatively simple, Launch Templates offer more configuration options. Option C is incorrect; both can be used with AWS Service Catalog. Option D is incorrect as Launch Templates explicitly support a wider range of purchasing options, including Spot Instances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain24_5',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_24_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

