INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_2_78',
  'Which of the following are types of hosted zones supported by Amazon Route 53? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Public Hosted Zone","Private Hosted Zone","Shared Hosted Zone","Global Hosted Zone","Local Hosted Zone"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'Amazon Route 53 supports two types of hosted zones: Public Hosted Zones (Option A) for managing DNS for public domains, and Private Hosted Zones (Option B) for managing DNS for domains within one or more Amazon VPCs. Option C, Option D, and Option E are not standard Route 53 hosted zone types.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  1,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_2_79',
  'Which of these benefits are primarily provided by Amazon CloudFront for content delivery? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Reduced latency for end-users","Improved availability of content","Automated database backups","Serverless compute execution","Managed message queuing"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'Amazon CloudFront is a content delivery network (CDN) service that caches content at edge locations worldwide. This significantly reduces latency for end-users (Option A) by serving content from a location closer to them. It also improves the availability of content (Option B) by distributing it across multiple edge locations. Option C (Automated database backups) is a feature of services like Amazon RDS, Option D (Serverless compute execution) is for AWS Lambda, and Option E (Managed message queuing) is for Amazon SQS, none of which are primary benefits of CloudFront.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  2,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_2_80',
  'Which AWS services are primarily used to provision and manage SSL/TLS certificates for use with other AWS services, and which service commonly integrates with them to deploy certificates for secure web traffic? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Certificate Manager","Elastic Load Balancing","Amazon S3","Amazon Route 53","AWS Key Management Service"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) (Option A) is the primary service for provisioning, managing, and deploying SSL/TLS certificates for use with AWS services. Elastic Load Balancing (ELB) (Option B) commonly integrates with ACM to deploy these certificates to its listeners, enabling secure HTTPS traffic for applications behind the load balancer. Amazon S3 (Option C) is object storage, Amazon Route 53 (Option D) is a DNS service, and AWS Key Management Service (Option E) is for managing encryption keys, none of which directly provision or deploy SSL/TLS certificates in this context.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  3,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_2_81',
  'Which of the following are common routing policies available in Amazon Route 53 for directing traffic to different resources? (Select THREE.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Simple routing","Weighted routing","Latency routing","Request-based routing","Path-based routing"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2}'::int[],
  'Amazon Route 53 offers various routing policies to determine how DNS queries are answered. Simple routing (Option A) is the default, Weighted routing (Option B) distributes traffic based on specified weights, and Latency routing (Option C) directs traffic to the resource that provides the lowest latency for the user. Request-based routing (Option D) and Path-based routing (Option E) are not standard Route 53 routing policies; these concepts are more relevant to application load balancers or API gateways.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  4,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_2_82',
  'Which of the following can serve as an origin for an Amazon CloudFront distribution? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon S3 bucket","Elastic Load Balancer","Amazon DynamoDB table","AWS Lambda function","Amazon Redshift cluster"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'An Amazon CloudFront distribution fetches content from an origin server. Common origins include an Amazon S3 bucket (Option A) for static website content or an Elastic Load Balancer (Option B) for dynamic web applications hosted on EC2 instances. Amazon DynamoDB (Option C) is a NoSQL database, AWS Lambda (Option D) is a serverless compute service, and Amazon Redshift (Option E) is a data warehouse service; none of these directly serve as CloudFront origins.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  5,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_2_83',
  'Which methods can be used with Amazon CloudFront to restrict access to private content? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Signed URLs","Signed Cookies","Security Groups","Network ACLs","AWS WAF"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'Amazon CloudFront provides mechanisms to restrict access to private content. Signed URLs (Option A) allow you to provide access to individual files for a limited time, while Signed Cookies (Option B) allow you to provide access to multiple files or an entire directory for a limited time. Security Groups (Option C) and Network ACLs (Option D) are VPC network firewalls, and AWS WAF (Option E) is a web application firewall, none of which are directly used by CloudFront for content access restriction in this manner.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  6,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_2_84',
  'Which of the following are benefits of using Amazon Route 53 Alias records instead of CNAME records for AWS resources? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Can be used for the zone apex (root domain)","Do not incur additional DNS query charges","Support all AWS resource types","Provide faster DNS resolution globally","Automatically renew SSL/TLS certificates"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'Amazon Route 53 Alias records offer specific advantages over CNAME records for AWS resources. Alias records can be used for the zone apex (root domain, e.g., example.com) (Option A), which CNAME records cannot. Additionally, Alias queries to AWS resources do not incur additional DNS query charges (Option B), unlike CNAMEs which may incur charges for resolving the target. Option C is incorrect as Alias records only support specific AWS resources. Option D (faster resolution) is generally true for Route 53 but not a unique benefit of Alias over CNAME. Option E (SSL/TLS certificate renewal) is handled by AWS Certificate Manager, not Route 53.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  7,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_2_85',
  'Which of the following are features provided by Amazon CloudFront to optimize content delivery? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Edge caching of content","SSL/TLS termination at the edge","Server-side encryption for S3 buckets","Automated database failover","Managed message filtering"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'Amazon CloudFront optimizes content delivery through several features. Edge caching of content (Option A) stores copies of your content at geographically dispersed edge locations, reducing latency. CloudFront also supports SSL/TLS termination at the edge (Option B), offloading encryption/decryption from your origin server and improving performance. Server-side encryption for S3 buckets (Option C) is an S3 feature. Automated database failover (Option D) is for database services like Amazon RDS. Managed message filtering (Option E) is not a CloudFront feature.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  8,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_2_86',
  'Which of the following are standard DNS record types that can be configured within an Amazon Route 53 hosted zone? (Select THREE.)',
  'multiple',
  '["A record","CNAME record","MX record","VPC record","EC2 record"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2}'::int[],
  'Amazon Route 53 supports various standard DNS record types. An A record (Option A) maps a domain name to an IPv4 address. A CNAME record (Option B) maps an alias name to another canonical domain name. An MX record (Option C) specifies mail servers for a domain. VPC record (Option D) and EC2 record (Option E) are not standard DNS record types; these are AWS service concepts, not DNS record types.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  9,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_2_87',
  'Which of the following are key features provided by Amazon CloudFront for content delivery? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Edge caching","Managed relational database hosting","Custom SSL/TLS certificates","Serverless compute execution","VPC peering management"]'::json,
  '{0,2}'::int[],
  'Option A (Edge caching) is a primary feature of Amazon CloudFront, which caches content at edge locations closer to users, reducing latency and improving performance. Option C (Custom SSL/TLS certificates) is also a key feature, allowing users to associate their own SSL/TLS certificates (managed by AWS Certificate Manager) with CloudFront distributions for secure content delivery over HTTPS. Option B (Managed relational database hosting) is provided by Amazon RDS. Option D (Serverless compute execution) is a function of AWS Lambda. Option E (VPC peering management) is related to Amazon VPC and AWS Transit Gateway, not CloudFront.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  10,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_2_88',
  'An organization wants to distribute content globally with low latency and ensure secure, controlled access to premium media files. Which Amazon CloudFront features should they implement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Origin Access Control (OAC)","VPC Endpoints","Signed URLs or Signed Cookies","AWS Direct Connect","Amazon Route 53 health checks"]'::json,
  '{0,2}'::int[],
  'Option A (Origin Access Control (OAC)) is a recommended CloudFront feature to restrict direct access to your Amazon S3 origin content, ensuring that users can only access files through CloudFront URLs. This enhances security by preventing bypass of CloudFront''s features. Option C (Signed URLs or Signed Cookies) are crucial for controlling access to premium content, allowing you to specify who can access your content, when they can access it, and from where. Option B (VPC Endpoints) are used for private connectivity to AWS services from within a VPC, not for global content distribution or access control for CloudFront. Option D (AWS Direct Connect) provides a dedicated network connection from on-premises to AWS, which is not directly a CloudFront feature for content access control. Option E (Amazon Route 53 health checks) are used for DNS routing based on health, not for securing content access via CloudFront.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  11,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_2_89',
  'Which Amazon Route 53 routing policies allow you to route traffic based on the geographic location of your users? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Failover routing policy","Geolocation routing policy","Weighted routing policy","Latency routing policy","Geoproximity routing policy"]'::json,
  '{1,4}'::int[],
  'Option B (Geolocation routing policy) lets you choose the resources that serve your traffic based on the geographic location of your users, specifying locations by continent, country, or by state/province in the United States. Option E (Geoproximity routing policy) routes traffic based on the geographic location of your users and your resources. You can also optionally route more traffic to resources in one location by specifying a bias. Option A (Failover routing policy) is used for active-passive failover configurations. Option C (Weighted routing policy) distributes traffic among multiple resources based on weights you assign. Option D (Latency routing policy) routes requests to the region that provides the lowest latency for the user.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  12,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_2_90',
  'An administrator needs to secure a web application hosted on Amazon EC2 instances behind an Elastic Load Balancer with SSL/TLS. They also want to use HTTPS for their Amazon CloudFront distribution. Which AWS services are essential for provisioning and managing these certificates? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)","AWS Key Management Service (KMS)","AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)","AWS Secrets Manager","Amazon Route 53"]'::json,
  '{0,4}'::int[],
  'Option A (AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)) is the primary service for provisioning, managing, and deploying SSL/TLS certificates for use with AWS services like Elastic Load Balancing and Amazon CloudFront. ACM simplifies the process of securing network communications. Option E (Amazon Route 53) is essential for DNS validation of certificates issued by ACM, as it can automatically create the necessary CNAME records for domain validation. Option B (AWS Key Management Service (KMS)) is used for managing encryption keys, not for provisioning SSL/TLS certificates. Option C (AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM)) manages access to AWS resources but doesn''t provision certificates. Option D (AWS Secrets Manager) is for managing secrets like database credentials, not SSL/TLS certificates.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_2_91',
  'Which of the following are valid components that can be configured within an Amazon CloudFront distribution? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Origins","Behaviors","VPC Endpoints","Subnets","Route Tables"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'Option A (Origins) are the locations where CloudFront fetches content that it then distributes to users. An origin can be an Amazon S3 bucket, an HTTP server (like an EC2 instance or an Elastic Load Balancer), or a MediaStore container. Option B (Behaviors) define how CloudFront processes requests for specific file types or paths, including caching policies, viewer protocols, and allowed HTTP methods. Option C (VPC Endpoints), Option D (Subnets), and Option E (Route Tables) are all components of an Amazon VPC, which is a networking service, and are not directly configured within a CloudFront distribution.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_2_92',
  'A media company needs to deliver high-quality video content to a global audience with minimal latency and ensure that only authorized users can access specific content. Which combination of AWS services and features should be used? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon CloudFront with signed URLs","AWS Direct Connect with a dedicated network","Amazon S3 with public read access","Amazon CloudFront with Origin Access Control (OAC)","Amazon Route 53 with simple routing"]'::json,
  '{0,3}'::int[],
  'Option A (Amazon CloudFront with signed URLs) is ideal for delivering content globally with low latency due to its edge caching, and signed URLs provide a mechanism to control access to specific content by authorized users for a limited time. Option D (Amazon CloudFront with Origin Access Control (OAC)) is essential for securing the Amazon S3 bucket (or other origin) that stores the video content, ensuring that users can only access the content through CloudFront and not directly from S3, which prevents bypassing the access controls. Option B (AWS Direct Connect with a dedicated network) is for hybrid cloud connectivity and doesn''t directly address global content delivery or access control for end-users. Option C (Amazon S3 with public read access) would make the content publicly available to anyone, which contradicts the requirement for ''only authorized users''. Option E (Amazon Route 53 with simple routing) provides basic DNS resolution but doesn''t offer content delivery optimization or access control features.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_2_93',
  'Which of the following are valid methods to restrict access to content served through an Amazon CloudFront distribution? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Configuring a Network ACL on the CloudFront distribution","Using CloudFront Signed URLs","Applying Security Groups to the CloudFront distribution","Implementing CloudFront Signed Cookies","Setting up a VPC endpoint for CloudFront"]'::json,
  '{1,3}'::int[],
  'Option B (Using CloudFront Signed URLs) allows you to provide access to specific content for a limited time, which is ideal for individual files or specific user access. Option D (Implementing CloudFront Signed Cookies) allows you to provide access to multiple restricted files for a limited time, which is useful for granting access to a subscriber section of a website. Option A (Configuring a Network ACL on the CloudFront distribution) and Option C (Applying Security Groups to the CloudFront distribution) are incorrect because Network ACLs and Security Groups are networking constructs used with Amazon VPC and EC2 instances, not directly with CloudFront distributions. Option E (Setting up a VPC endpoint for CloudFront) is not a valid concept; CloudFront is a global service and does not reside within a VPC in a way that would use VPC endpoints for access control.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_2_94',
  'A media company hosts a popular static website on Amazon S3 and serves content globally via Amazon CloudFront. They need to ensure that specific premium content is only accessible to authenticated subscribers for a limited time, and they want to prevent direct access to the S3 origin. Which two mechanisms should the company implement to meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Configure an Origin Access Control (OAC) for the CloudFront distribution to restrict S3 bucket access.","Implement CloudFront signed URLs or signed cookies for the premium content.","Use Amazon Route 53 DNSSEC to prevent unauthorized DNS resolution for the content.","Set up an AWS WAF Web ACL on the CloudFront distribution to block direct S3 requests.","Encrypt the S3 bucket using server-side encryption with AWS KMS keys."]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'Option A (correct) is to configure an Origin Access Control (OAC) for the CloudFront distribution. OAC is the recommended way to restrict access to Amazon S3 origins, ensuring that users can only access content through CloudFront and not directly from the S3 bucket. Option B (correct) is to implement CloudFront signed URLs or signed cookies. These mechanisms allow the company to provide temporary, time-limited access to private content, which is essential for authenticated subscribers. Signed URLs are suitable for individual files, while signed cookies are better for access to multiple restricted files.
Option C (incorrect) is to use Amazon Route 53 DNSSEC. While DNSSEC enhances DNS security by preventing DNS spoofing, it does not control access to the content itself or restrict direct S3 bucket access. Option D (incorrect) is to set up an AWS WAF Web ACL. AWS WAF can protect against common web exploits and control traffic based on IP addresses or request headers, but it is not the primary mechanism for restricting direct S3 origin access or implementing time-limited access for subscribers. Option E (incorrect) is to encrypt the S3 bucket. While important for data security at rest, server-side encryption does not control who can access the content or how it is delivered; it only protects the data once it''s stored.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_2_95',
  'A company is using Amazon CloudFront to distribute its web application content globally. They observe that updates to their origin content are not immediately reflected at the edge locations, leading to users seeing stale content. They also want to ensure that specific sensitive content is not cached at all. Which two CloudFront features can address these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Configure a custom error page for the distribution to redirect stale content requests.","Set appropriate Cache-Control headers on the origin content to control caching behavior.","Create a CloudFront invalidation for the specific paths of updated content.","Enable CloudFront geo-restriction to limit content access to specific countries.","Use CloudFront field-level encryption for sensitive data."]'::json,
  '{1,2}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) is to set appropriate Cache-Control headers on the origin content. By setting `Cache-Control: no-store` or `Cache-Control: no-cache` for sensitive content, you can prevent CloudFront from caching it. For other content, `max-age` and `s-maxage` directives can control how long content is cached. Option C (correct) is to create a CloudFront invalidation. When origin content is updated and you need it to be reflected immediately at edge locations, you can create an invalidation request for the specific paths. This forces CloudFront edge locations to fetch the latest version from the origin for subsequent requests.
Option A (incorrect) is to configure a custom error page. Custom error pages are used to display branded error messages to users when an error occurs (e.g., 404 Not Found, 503 Service Unavailable), but they do not control caching behavior or force content updates. Option D (incorrect) is to enable CloudFront geo-restriction. Geo-restriction controls which geographic locations can access your content, but it has no impact on caching or content freshness. Option E (incorrect) is to use CloudFront field-level encryption. Field-level encryption protects specific sensitive data fields throughout the application stack, but it is not related to controlling caching or invalidating stale content.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_2_96',
  'A company needs to secure its public-facing web applications with SSL/TLS certificates. They require a solution that automatically renews certificates and integrates seamlessly with AWS services like Elastic Load Balancing and Amazon CloudFront. Which two AWS services are best suited for provisioning and managing these certificates? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) for certificate storage.","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) for provisioning and automatic renewal.","AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) for uploading third-party certificates.","Amazon Route 53 for DNS validation of certificates.","AWS Secrets Manager for storing certificate private keys."]'::json,
  '{1,3}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) is AWS Certificate Manager (ACM). ACM allows you to easily provision, manage, and deploy public and private SSL/TLS certificates for use with AWS services. It handles certificate renewals automatically, which is a key requirement for minimizing operational overhead. Option D (correct) is Amazon Route 53 for DNS validation. When requesting a certificate from ACM, you need to validate domain ownership. DNS validation using Route 53 is a common and automated method, where ACM provides a CNAME record that you add to your Route 53 hosted zone to prove ownership.
Option A (incorrect) is AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS). While KMS is used for managing encryption keys, it is not used for provisioning or storing SSL/TLS certificates themselves. ACM handles the certificate management. Option C (incorrect) is AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM). IAM can be used to upload third-party SSL/TLS certificates for use with some AWS services (like CloudFront), but it does not provide automatic renewal and is generally less preferred than ACM for public certificates due to the manual renewal process. Option E (incorrect) is AWS Secrets Manager. Secrets Manager is used for storing and rotating sensitive information like database credentials, API keys, and other secrets, but not for the lifecycle management of SSL/TLS certificates.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_2_97',
  'A global company hosts its main application on Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer (ALB) in a VPC. They want to ensure that users from different geographic regions are routed to the closest available ALB for optimal latency and performance. Additionally, they need to implement a weighted routing policy for A/B testing new features in specific regions. Which two Amazon Route 53 routing policies can achieve these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Simple routing policy for each ALB endpoint.","Geolocation routing policy to direct traffic based on user location.","Latency routing policy to route users to the region with the lowest latency.","Multivalue answer routing policy for multiple healthy endpoints.","Weighted routing policy to distribute traffic based on specified weights."]'::json,
  '{2,4}'::int[],
  'Option C (correct) is a Latency routing policy. This policy routes users to the AWS region that provides the lowest latency for the user, which directly addresses the requirement for optimal latency and performance by directing users to the closest available ALB. Option E (correct) is a Weighted routing policy. This policy allows you to distribute traffic to multiple resources based on specified weights, making it ideal for A/B testing scenarios where a small percentage of traffic needs to be directed to a new feature in specific regions.
Option A (incorrect) is a Simple routing policy. Simple routing is used for a single resource that performs a given function for your domain and does not support routing based on latency, location, or weights. Option B (incorrect) is a Geolocation routing policy. While geolocation routing can direct traffic based on the user''s geographic location, the primary requirement here is lowest latency, which is specifically handled by the latency routing policy. Geolocation is more about content localization or compliance. Option D (incorrect) is a Multivalue answer routing policy. This policy returns up to eight healthy records, allowing clients to choose one, which can be useful for random load balancing or distributing traffic across multiple healthy resources, but it doesn''t directly address latency optimization or weighted A/B testing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_2_98',
  'A company is deploying a new application that requires a custom domain name and needs to be highly available across multiple AWS regions. They plan to use Amazon Route 53 for DNS management and want to ensure that users are directed to the closest healthy endpoint. Which two Amazon Route 53 features should be configured to meet these requirements? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["A simple routing policy with a single A record.","Health checks associated with each endpoint to monitor their status.","A failover routing policy to switch between primary and secondary regions.","A geolocation routing policy to direct traffic based on user location.","An Alias record pointing to a non-AWS resource."]'::json,
  '{1,3}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) is to configure health checks associated with each endpoint. Route 53 health checks continuously monitor the health and availability of your application endpoints. This information is then used by routing policies to ensure that traffic is only directed to healthy resources, which is critical for high availability. Option D (correct) is a geolocation routing policy. This policy allows you to route traffic to resources based on the geographic location of your users. By configuring records for different regions, users will be directed to the closest endpoint, optimizing latency and performance.
Option A (incorrect) is a simple routing policy. A simple routing policy is used for a single resource and does not provide high availability across multiple regions or intelligent routing based on health or location. Option C (incorrect) is a failover routing policy. While failover routing is used for high availability, it typically involves a primary and a secondary endpoint. For routing users to the *closest* healthy endpoint across multiple regions, a geolocation or latency-based policy combined with health checks is more appropriate than a simple active-passive failover. Option E (incorrect) is an Alias record pointing to a non-AWS resource. Alias records are primarily designed to point to specific AWS resources (like ELBs, CloudFront distributions, S3 buckets) and do not support pointing to arbitrary non-AWS resources.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_2_99',
  'A SysOps Administrator needs to secure a web application hosted on Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer (ALB) with SSL/TLS. The company requires a cost-effective solution that simplifies certificate management and ensures automatic renewal. Which two services are essential for implementing this solution? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Secrets Manager for storing the SSL/TLS private key.","AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) to provision and manage the certificate.","Amazon Route 53 for DNS validation of the domain.","AWS Key Management Service (KMS) for encrypting the certificate.","AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) to upload a self-signed certificate."]'::json,
  '{1,2}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) is AWS Certificate Manager (ACM). ACM provides free SSL/TLS certificates that integrate seamlessly with ALBs and automatically renew, significantly reducing operational overhead and cost. This directly addresses the requirements for cost-effectiveness, simplified management, and automatic renewal. Option C (correct) is Amazon Route 53 for DNS validation. When requesting a certificate from ACM, domain ownership must be validated. DNS validation, often performed by adding a CNAME record to a Route 53 hosted zone, is the most common and automated method, ensuring the certificate can be issued and renewed without manual intervention.
Option A (incorrect) is AWS Secrets Manager. Secrets Manager is used for managing database credentials, API keys, and other secrets, but not for the lifecycle management of SSL/TLS certificates. ACM handles the certificates directly. Option D (incorrect) is AWS Key Management Service (KMS). KMS is used for managing encryption keys for various AWS services, but it does not provision or manage SSL/TLS certificates themselves. ACM handles the certificates. Option E (incorrect) is AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM). While IAM can store certificates, it does not offer automatic renewal for certificates uploaded manually, which goes against the requirement for simplified and automatic renewal.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_26_1
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_69',
  'Which of the following are fundamental components of an Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Subnets","Route Tables","Amazon S3 buckets","AWS Lambda functions","Amazon DynamoDB tables"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'An Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) is a logically isolated section of the AWS Cloud where you can launch AWS resources. Fundamental components of a VPC include Subnets (Option A), which are ranges of IP addresses within the VPC, and Route Tables (Option B), which contain rules that determine where network traffic from your subnets is directed. Amazon S3 buckets (Option C), AWS Lambda functions (Option D), and Amazon DynamoDB tables (Option E) are AWS services that can be used within or accessed from a VPC, but they are not fundamental components of the VPC itself.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_70',
  'Which AWS services enable private connectivity from your VPC to other AWS services without traversing the public internet? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["VPC Endpoints","AWS PrivateLink","Internet Gateway","NAT Gateway","AWS Direct Connect"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'VPC Endpoints (Option A) and AWS PrivateLink (Option B) are designed to establish private connectivity from your VPC to supported AWS services and VPC endpoint services hosted by other AWS accounts or AWS Marketplace partners, without requiring traffic to traverse the public internet. An Internet Gateway (Option C) provides public internet access, a NAT Gateway (Option D) allows private subnets to access the internet, and AWS Direct Connect (Option E) connects your on-premises network to AWS, but not directly for private VPC-to-AWS service connectivity in the same manner as endpoints.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_71',
  'Which AWS services are designed to establish hybrid cloud connectivity between your on-premises data center and your AWS VPC? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Site-to-Site VPN","AWS Direct Connect","VPC Peering","AWS Transit Gateway","Amazon CloudFront"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'To establish hybrid cloud connectivity between an on-premises data center and an AWS VPC, AWS Site-to-Site VPN (Option A) creates an encrypted connection over the public internet, while AWS Direct Connect (Option B) provides a dedicated private network connection. VPC Peering (Option C) connects two VPCs, AWS Transit Gateway (Option D) connects multiple VPCs and on-premises networks (but Direct Connect/VPN are the direct hybrid links), and Amazon CloudFront (Option E) is a content delivery network.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_72',
  'Which of the following are primary functions of an AWS NAT Gateway within a VPC? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Allow instances in private subnets to initiate outbound internet connections","Prevent inbound internet connections to instances in private subnets","Enable direct inbound internet access to private instances","Route traffic between VPCs","Provide DNS resolution for private hosted zones"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'An AWS NAT Gateway allows instances in a private subnet to connect to the internet (Option A) or other AWS services, but prevents the internet from initiating connections to those instances (Option B). It translates the private IP addresses of instances to the NAT Gateway''s public IP address for outbound traffic. Option C is incorrect as NAT Gateways prevent inbound internet access. Option D (routing traffic between VPCs) is for VPC peering or Transit Gateway. Option E (DNS resolution) is for Amazon Route 53.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_73',
  'Which of the following are components within a VPC that act as firewalls to control traffic to and from instances? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Security Groups","Network ACLs","Route Tables","Internet Gateway","VPC Flow Logs"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'Within an Amazon VPC, Security Groups (Option A) and Network ACLs (NACLs) (Option B) both act as virtual firewalls. Security Groups operate at the instance level and are stateful, while NACLs operate at the subnet level and are stateless. Route Tables (Option C) determine traffic routing, an Internet Gateway (Option D) enables internet connectivity, and VPC Flow Logs (Option E) capture network traffic information, but none of these directly filter traffic in the same way as Security Groups and NACLs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_74',
  'For an Amazon EC2 instance in a public subnet to have direct internet access, which of the following are essential VPC components? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Internet Gateway","Route Table entry to the Internet Gateway","NAT Gateway","VPC Endpoint","AWS Direct Connect"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'For an Amazon EC2 instance in a public subnet to have direct internet access, an Internet Gateway (Option A) must be attached to the VPC. Additionally, the subnet''s route table must contain a route (Option B) that directs internet-bound traffic (0.0.0.0/0) to the Internet Gateway. A NAT Gateway (Option C) is for private subnets. A VPC Endpoint (Option D) is for private access to AWS services. AWS Direct Connect (Option E) is for hybrid connectivity to on-premises networks.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_75',
  'Which of the following are key benefits of using AWS Transit Gateway for network connectivity? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Simplifies network topology for many VPCs","Enables centralized routing management","Provides direct internet access for private subnets","Automatically encrypts all data at rest","Manages SSL/TLS certificates"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'AWS Transit Gateway is a network transit hub that simplifies network topology for many VPCs (Option A) by connecting them in a hub-and-spoke model, eliminating the need for complex VPC peering meshes. It also enables centralized routing management (Option B) for all connected VPCs and on-premises networks. Option C (direct internet access for private subnets) is a function of a NAT Gateway. Option D (data encryption at rest) is a feature of storage services, and Option E (SSL/TLS certificate management) is handled by AWS Certificate Manager.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_76',
  'Which of the following are types of VPC Endpoints available for connecting your VPC to AWS services privately? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Gateway Endpoint","Interface Endpoint","Direct Connect Endpoint","VPN Endpoint","Peering Endpoint"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'AWS offers two types of VPC Endpoints to connect your VPC to AWS services privately. A Gateway Endpoint (Option A) provides reliable connectivity to Amazon S3 and DynamoDB. An Interface Endpoint (Option B) is powered by AWS PrivateLink and provides private connectivity to a wide range of AWS services and endpoint services. Direct Connect Endpoint (Option C), VPN Endpoint (Option D), and Peering Endpoint (Option E) are not standard VPC Endpoint types.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_77',
  'Which AWS resources can an Elastic IP address be associated with to provide a static public IPv4 address? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["EC2 instance","Network Interface","Amazon S3 bucket","Amazon RDS instance","AWS Lambda function"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'An Elastic IP address is a static, public IPv4 address designed for dynamic cloud computing. It can be associated with an Amazon EC2 instance (Option A) or a Network Interface (Option B) to mask instance failures by quickly remapping the address to another instance or network interface in your account. Elastic IPs cannot be directly associated with an Amazon S3 bucket (Option C), Amazon RDS instance (Option D), or an AWS Lambda function (Option E).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_78',
  'Which AWS services provide private, dedicated network connectivity between an on-premises data center and AWS VPCs? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Site-to-Site VPN","Amazon Connect","AWS Direct Connect","AWS Client VPN","Amazon VPC Peering"]'::json,
  '{0,2}'::int[],
  'Option A (AWS Site-to-Site VPN) establishes an encrypted connection over the public internet between your on-premises network and your Amazon VPC. Option C (AWS Direct Connect) provides a dedicated, private network connection from your on-premises data center directly to AWS, bypassing the public internet entirely for consistent network performance. Option B (Amazon Connect) is a cloud contact center service. Option D (AWS Client VPN) allows individual users to securely access AWS resources from their client devices. Option E (Amazon VPC Peering) connects two VPCs within AWS, not an on-premises data center to AWS.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  32,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_79',
  'An administrator needs to ensure that an Amazon EC2 instance maintains a consistent public IP address even if the instance is stopped and restarted, or replaced. Which AWS networking components facilitate this requirement? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Elastic IP address","Public IP address","Elastic Network Interface (ENI)","Private IP address","Internet Gateway"]'::json,
  '{0,2}'::int[],
  'Option A (Elastic IP address) is a static, public IPv4 address designed for dynamic cloud computing. You can associate an Elastic IP address with an EC2 instance, and it remains associated with your AWS account until you explicitly release it, even if the instance is stopped, started, or replaced. Option C (Elastic Network Interface (ENI)) is a virtual network card that can be attached to an EC2 instance. An Elastic IP address can be associated with an ENI, and the ENI itself can be moved between instances, allowing the public IP to follow the application. Option B (Public IP address) is assigned automatically to instances in a public subnet and changes upon stop/start. Option D (Private IP address) is for internal communication within the VPC. Option E (Internet Gateway) provides internet connectivity for a VPC but doesn''t manage static public IPs for individual instances.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_80',
  'Which of the following are valid methods for establishing private network connectivity between your on-premises data center and your AWS VPCs? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["VPC Peering","AWS Site-to-Site VPN","AWS Direct Connect","Internet Gateway","VPC Endpoints"]'::json,
  '{1,2}'::int[],
  'Option B (AWS Site-to-Site VPN) creates an encrypted tunnel over the public internet to connect your on-premises network to your AWS VPC. Option C (AWS Direct Connect) provides a dedicated, private network connection from your on-premises data center to AWS, offering more consistent network performance than an internet-based VPN. Option A (VPC Peering) connects two VPCs within AWS, not an on-premises network. Option D (Internet Gateway) provides internet access to a VPC but does not establish private connectivity to an on-premises network. Option E (VPC Endpoints) enable private access to AWS services from within a VPC, not for connecting an on-premises network to a VPC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_81',
  'An architect is designing a network for an application that requires high throughput and low latency communication between several Amazon EC2 instances located in different subnets within the same Amazon VPC. Which networking constructs are fundamental for this design? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Subnets","Internet Gateway","Route Tables","VPC Peering","NAT Gateway"]'::json,
  '{0,2}'::int[],
  'Option A (Subnets) are fundamental for segmenting the VPC into smaller networks, allowing for logical isolation and organization of resources, which is a core part of any VPC design. Option C (Route Tables) are essential for defining how traffic flows between subnets, to the internet, or to other VPCs/networks. For instances in different subnets to communicate, the route tables must correctly direct traffic. Option B (Internet Gateway) provides internet access, which is not required for communication between instances within the same VPC. Option D (VPC Peering) is for connecting different VPCs, not for communication within the same VPC. Option E (NAT Gateway) allows instances in private subnets to initiate outbound internet connections, which is not directly relevant for inter-subnet communication within the same VPC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_82',
  'Which of the following are valid uses for Elastic Network Interfaces (ENIs) in an Amazon VPC? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Creating a dedicated network connection to an on-premises data center","Implementing a management network for an EC2 instance","Attaching multiple IP addresses to a single EC2 instance","Providing internet connectivity to a private subnet","Establishing a VPN connection to another VPC"]'::json,
  '{1,2}'::int[],
  'Option B (Implementing a management network for an EC2 instance) is a common use case for ENIs, allowing you to attach a separate network interface to an instance for management traffic, isolating it from application traffic. Option C (Attaching multiple IP addresses to a single EC2 instance) is also a key capability of ENIs, as each ENI can have multiple private IP addresses, and you can attach multiple ENIs to a single instance, effectively giving the instance multiple IP addresses. Option A (Creating a dedicated network connection to an on-premises data center) is the purpose of AWS Direct Connect. Option D (Providing internet connectivity to a private subnet) is the function of a NAT Gateway. Option E (Establishing a VPN connection to another VPC) is typically done via AWS Site-to-Site VPN or AWS Transit Gateway, not directly with ENIs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_83',
  'Which AWS networking services allow you to establish private connectivity to AWS services from within your VPC without requiring an Internet Gateway, NAT device, VPN connection, or AWS Direct Connect connection? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["VPC Peering","VPC Gateway Endpoints","AWS Transit Gateway","VPC Interface Endpoints","Elastic Load Balancing"]'::json,
  '{1,3}'::int[],
  'Option B (VPC Gateway Endpoints) provide private connectivity to Amazon S3 and Amazon DynamoDB from within your VPC. They are implemented as a target in your route table. Option D (VPC Interface Endpoints), powered by AWS PrivateLink, provide private connectivity to a wide range of AWS services (including S3 and DynamoDB as an alternative to Gateway Endpoints, or other services like EC2, SQS, SNS) and services hosted by other AWS customers or partners. Option A (VPC Peering) connects two VPCs. Option C (AWS Transit Gateway) connects multiple VPCs and on-premises networks. Option E (Elastic Load Balancing) distributes incoming application traffic across multiple targets, such as EC2 instances, but does not provide private access to AWS services themselves.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_84',
  'A company is designing a new multi-tier application within a single VPC. The application''s web servers are in public subnets, while the database servers are in private subnets. The web servers need to access the internet for software updates, but the database servers must remain isolated from the internet. Which two networking components are essential for this design? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["An Internet Gateway attached to the VPC.","A NAT Gateway deployed in a private subnet.","A Virtual Private Gateway for VPN connectivity.","An Elastic IP address associated with each web server.","A NAT Gateway deployed in a public subnet."]'::json,
  '{0,4}'::int[],
  'Option A (correct) is an Internet Gateway attached to the VPC. An Internet Gateway is necessary to allow resources in public subnets (like the web servers) to communicate with the internet. It provides the target for internet-bound routes in the public subnet''s route table. Option E (correct) is a NAT Gateway deployed in a public subnet. A NAT Gateway allows instances in private subnets (like the database servers) to initiate outbound connections to the internet for updates or patches while preventing unsolicited inbound connections from the internet. It must be placed in a public subnet to have internet connectivity itself.
Option B (incorrect) is a NAT Gateway deployed in a private subnet. A NAT Gateway must be deployed in a public subnet to function correctly, as it needs a public IP address and a route to the Internet Gateway. Deploying it in a private subnet would prevent it from reaching the internet. Option C (incorrect) is a Virtual Private Gateway. A Virtual Private Gateway is used for establishing AWS Site-to-Site VPN connections between your VPC and your on-premises network, not for providing internet access within a VPC. Option D (incorrect) is an Elastic IP address associated with each web server. While web servers in public subnets need public IP addresses to be directly accessible from the internet, associating an Elastic IP with *each* web server is not strictly essential for the core design of internet access for public subnets and outbound access for private subnets. An Internet Gateway and public IP addresses (either Elastic IPs or public IPv4 addresses assigned automatically) are needed, but the question asks for components essential for the *design* of internet access for public and outbound for private. A NAT Gateway handles the private subnet''s outbound needs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_85',
  'A large enterprise has multiple VPCs in the same AWS region, each owned by a different department. They need to establish secure and scalable network connectivity between these VPCs for shared services, avoiding the complexity of managing numerous individual peering connections. Which two AWS networking solutions are most appropriate for this scenario? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Establish a full mesh of VPC peering connections between all departmental VPCs.","Implement AWS Transit Gateway to centralize inter-VPC routing.","Configure AWS Direct Connect for each VPC to establish private connectivity.","Use VPC endpoints to allow private access to shared services across VPCs.","Deploy a hub-and-spoke model using AWS Transit Gateway."]'::json,
  '{1,4}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) is to implement AWS Transit Gateway. AWS Transit Gateway acts as a network transit hub, allowing you to connect multiple VPCs and on-premises networks to a single gateway. This simplifies network architecture and management, especially in scenarios with many VPCs, by centralizing routing. Option E (correct) is to deploy a hub-and-spoke model using AWS Transit Gateway. This is the recommended architectural pattern for connecting many VPCs. The Transit Gateway acts as the ''hub,'' and each VPC connects as a ''spoke,'' providing scalable and centralized inter-VPC routing and management, directly addressing the anti-pattern of using VPC peering for many VPCs (ap-5.1-001).
Option A (incorrect) is to establish a full mesh of VPC peering connections. VPC peering connections are non-transitive and require N*(N-1)/2 connections for a full mesh, which becomes unmanageable and complex with many VPCs. This is a common anti-pattern (ap-5.1-001). Option C (incorrect) is to configure AWS Direct Connect for each VPC. AWS Direct Connect provides a dedicated private connection from your on-premises data center to AWS. While it offers private connectivity, it''s not designed for inter-VPC connectivity within AWS and would be overly complex and expensive for this use case. Option D (incorrect) is to use VPC endpoints. VPC endpoints (Interface and Gateway) allow private access to specific AWS services (like S3 or EC2 APIs) from within your VPC, but they do not provide general inter-VPC connectivity for custom applications.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_86',
  'A company hosts a web application in a private subnet within a VPC. The application needs to securely access Amazon S3 buckets to store and retrieve user-generated content, without routing traffic through the public internet. Which two types of VPC endpoints can be used to achieve this private connectivity to Amazon S3? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["An Interface VPC Endpoint for Amazon S3.","A Gateway VPC Endpoint for Amazon S3.","An Interface VPC Endpoint for Amazon EC2.","A Gateway Load Balancer endpoint.","An AWS PrivateLink endpoint."]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'Option A (correct) is an Interface VPC Endpoint for Amazon S3. Interface endpoints are powered by AWS PrivateLink and provide private connectivity to S3 via an Elastic Network Interface (ENI) with private IP addresses in your subnet. This allows S3 traffic to stay entirely within the AWS network. Option B (correct) is a Gateway VPC Endpoint for Amazon S3. Gateway endpoints are a specific type of VPC endpoint that provides reliable connectivity to Amazon S3 (and DynamoDB) from your VPC without requiring an internet gateway, NAT device, VPN connection, or AWS Direct Connect connection. It works by adding an entry to your route table.
Option C (incorrect) is an Interface VPC Endpoint for Amazon EC2. While Interface endpoints are valid, an endpoint for EC2 would provide private access to the EC2 API, not to Amazon S3. Option D (incorrect) is a Gateway Load Balancer endpoint. Gateway Load Balancer endpoints are used for deploying, scaling, and managing third-party virtual appliances (like firewalls or intrusion detection systems) in a VPC, not for connecting directly to AWS services like S3. Option E (incorrect) is an AWS PrivateLink endpoint. AWS PrivateLink is the technology that powers Interface VPC Endpoints, but ''AWS PrivateLink endpoint'' is not a distinct type of VPC endpoint you configure directly; you configure an Interface VPC Endpoint which uses PrivateLink.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_87',
  'A SysOps Administrator is configuring a new Amazon EC2 instance that needs to serve web traffic and also connect to a backend database in a different private subnet. The instance must have a static public IP address for external access and a private IP address for internal communication. Which two networking components are essential for configuring this EC2 instance? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["A Network Access Control List (NACL) to control inbound traffic to the instance.","An Elastic IP address associated with the instance''s primary network interface.","A Security Group attached to the instance to control traffic.","A Virtual Private Gateway for VPN connectivity.","Multiple Elastic Network Interfaces (ENIs) for separate public and private IPs."]'::json,
  '{1,2}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) is an Elastic IP address associated with the instance''s primary network interface. An Elastic IP (EIP) is a static, public IPv4 address that you can associate with an EC2 instance. This meets the requirement for a static public IP address for external access. Option C (correct) is a Security Group attached to the instance. Security Groups act as a virtual firewall for your EC2 instances, controlling both inbound and outbound traffic at the instance level. It is essential for securing the web server and allowing necessary communication to the database.
Option A (incorrect) is a Network Access Control List (NACL). NACLs operate at the subnet level and are stateless. While NACLs are part of VPC security, a Security Group is the primary and essential component for controlling traffic directly to an EC2 instance. Option D (incorrect) is a Virtual Private Gateway. A Virtual Private Gateway is used for establishing AWS Site-to-Site VPN connections to on-premises networks, not for assigning static public IPs or managing internal communication within a VPC. Option E (incorrect) is Multiple Elastic Network Interfaces (ENIs). While ENIs can provide multiple network interfaces, the requirement for a static public IP and a private IP can be met with a single primary ENI. An EIP is associated with an ENI, and the ENI already has a private IP. Multiple ENIs would be used for more complex scenarios like network appliance deployments or multi-homing, not for this basic requirement.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_88',
  'A SysOps Administrator needs to configure a highly available and resilient NAT solution for private subnets in a VPC. The solution must allow instances in private subnets to initiate outbound connections to the internet for software updates and patches, without exposing them to inbound internet traffic. Which two statements correctly describe the best practices for deploying a NAT Gateway to achieve this? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Deploy a single NAT Gateway in a private subnet to save costs.","Deploy a NAT Gateway in each public subnet in different Availability Zones.","Associate an Elastic IP address with each NAT Gateway.","Configure the route tables of private subnets to route internet-bound traffic to the Internet Gateway.","Attach a Security Group to the NAT Gateway to control outbound traffic."]'::json,
  '{1,2}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) is to deploy a NAT Gateway in each public subnet in different Availability Zones. For high availability and resiliency, it is a best practice to deploy a NAT Gateway in each public subnet that serves private subnets in that Availability Zone. This ensures that if one AZ experiences issues, the NAT Gateway in another AZ can still provide outbound internet access. Option C (correct) is to associate an Elastic IP address with each NAT Gateway. Each NAT Gateway requires an Elastic IP address, which is a static public IPv4 address, to enable outbound internet connectivity.
Option A (incorrect) is to deploy a single NAT Gateway in a private subnet. A NAT Gateway must be deployed in a public subnet to function correctly, as it needs a public IP and a route to the Internet Gateway. Deploying a single NAT Gateway also creates a single point of failure, failing the high availability requirement. Option D (incorrect) is to configure the route tables of private subnets to route internet-bound traffic to the Internet Gateway. Private subnets should have their internet-bound traffic (0.0.0.0/0) routed to the NAT Gateway, not directly to the Internet Gateway. The NAT Gateway then routes traffic to the Internet Gateway. Option E (incorrect) is to attach a Security Group to the NAT Gateway. You cannot associate security groups with a NAT Gateway. Instead, you control traffic to and from instances in private subnets using security groups on the instances themselves, and NACLs on the subnets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_89',
  'A company requires its Amazon EC2 instances to have multiple network interfaces for different purposes, such as separating management traffic from application traffic, or for deploying network appliances. Each interface needs its own IP addresses, security groups, and potentially different subnets. Which two components are fundamental for configuring such a setup? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Multiple Internet Gateways attached to the VPC.","Multiple Elastic Network Interfaces (ENIs) attached to the EC2 instance.","Multiple Security Groups associated with the EC2 instance.","Multiple Route Tables associated with the EC2 instance.","Multiple Network Access Control Lists (NACLs) for the subnets."]'::json,
  '{1,2}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) is multiple Elastic Network Interfaces (ENIs) attached to the EC2 instance. ENIs are logical networking components that can be attached to an EC2 instance. Each ENI can have its own private IP addresses, Elastic IP addresses, and security groups, allowing for multiple network interfaces on a single instance. Option C (correct) is multiple Security Groups associated with the EC2 instance. While an ENI can have its own security groups, an EC2 instance itself can also have multiple security groups associated with it, or different ENIs can have different security groups, allowing for granular control over traffic for each interface or purpose.
Option A (incorrect) is multiple Internet Gateways. A VPC can only have one Internet Gateway, which provides internet connectivity for the entire VPC. You cannot attach multiple Internet Gateways. Option D (incorrect) is multiple Route Tables associated with the EC2 instance. Route tables are associated with subnets, not directly with EC2 instances. While different ENIs might be in different subnets with different route tables, the route tables themselves are not directly associated with the instance. Option E (incorrect) is multiple Network Access Control Lists (NACLs). NACLs are stateless firewalls at the subnet level. While different subnets might have different NACLs, they are not directly configured on the EC2 instance itself to manage its multiple interfaces.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_26_3
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_56',
  'Which AWS services or features provide logs for analyzing network traffic within a VPC? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["VPC Flow Logs","Elastic Load Balancing Access Logs","Amazon CloudWatch Logs","AWS CloudTrail","AWS Config"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'VPC Flow Logs (Option A) capture information about IP traffic going to and from network interfaces in your VPC, providing detailed records for analysis. Elastic Load Balancing Access Logs (Option B) capture detailed information about requests sent to your load balancer, including client IP, request latency, and server responses. Amazon CloudWatch Logs (Option C) is a general logging service that can store logs from various sources, but VPC Flow Logs and ELB Access Logs are the specific features for network traffic. AWS CloudTrail (Option D) logs API activity, and AWS Config (Option E) assesses resource configurations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_57',
  'Which AWS services or features are primarily used to analyze network connectivity paths and troubleshoot reachability issues between resources? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Reachability Analyzer","Amazon CloudWatch","VPC Flow Logs","AWS Config","Amazon Route 53"]'::json,
  '{0,2}'::int[],
  'AWS Reachability Analyzer (Option A) is a network diagnostics tool that enables you to analyze and troubleshoot network reachability between specified resources in your VPCs. VPC Flow Logs (Option C) record information about the IP traffic going to and from network interfaces in your VPC, which is crucial for identifying if traffic is reaching its destination or being dropped, thereby aiding in troubleshooting reachability. Amazon CloudWatch (Option B) provides monitoring but not direct path analysis. AWS Config (Option D) assesses resource configurations. Amazon Route 53 (Option E) is a DNS service.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_58',
  'When troubleshooting network connectivity issues for an Amazon EC2 instance, which VPC components should be primarily reviewed for correct configuration? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Security Groups","Network ACLs","Amazon S3 bucket policies","AWS Lambda permissions","Amazon RDS instance settings"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'When troubleshooting network connectivity for an Amazon EC2 instance, the primary VPC components to review are Security Groups (Option A), which act as a virtual firewall for the instance, and Network ACLs (NACLs) (Option B), which act as a stateless firewall for the subnet. Both control inbound and outbound traffic. Amazon S3 bucket policies (Option C), AWS Lambda permissions (Option D), and Amazon RDS instance settings (Option E) are unrelated to the network connectivity of an EC2 instance within a VPC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_59',
  'Which types of information are typically captured and recorded in VPC Flow Logs? (Select THREE.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Source IP address","Destination IP address","Action (ACCEPT/REJECT)","EC2 instance CPU utilization","S3 bucket access events"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2}'::int[],
  'VPC Flow Logs capture detailed information about IP traffic. This includes the Source IP address (Option A) and Destination IP address (Option B) of the traffic, as well as the Action (ACCEPT/REJECT) (Option C) indicating whether the traffic was allowed or denied by security groups or network ACLs. EC2 instance CPU utilization (Option D) is a metric captured by Amazon CloudWatch, and S3 bucket access events (Option E) are logged by S3 access logs or CloudTrail, not VPC Flow Logs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_60',
  'When troubleshooting network connectivity, which of the following statements are true about Network ACLs (NACLs) compared to Security Groups? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["NACLs are stateless","NACLs require explicit rules for both inbound and outbound traffic","NACLs operate at the instance level","NACLs are stateful","NACLs only allow outbound traffic by default"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'Network ACLs (NACLs) are stateless (Option A), meaning they do not track the state of connections. Consequently, NACLs require explicit rules for both inbound and outbound traffic (Option B), including return traffic, to allow full communication. In contrast, Security Groups are stateful. Option C is incorrect; NACLs operate at the subnet level, while Security Groups operate at the instance level. Option D is incorrect as NACLs are stateless. Option E is incorrect; NACLs have default rules that allow all inbound and outbound traffic unless modified.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_61',
  'Which features of Elastic Load Balancing (ELB) are useful for operational monitoring and graceful instance termination? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Access Logs","Connection Draining","VPC Flow Logs","AWS CloudTrail","Amazon S3 bucket policies"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'Elastic Load Balancing (ELB) provides features for operational monitoring and managing instance lifecycle. Access Logs (Option A) capture detailed information about requests sent to your load balancer, which is useful for monitoring and troubleshooting. Connection Draining (Option B) ensures that the load balancer stops sending new requests to a deregistering or unhealthy instance while allowing existing requests to complete, facilitating graceful instance termination. VPC Flow Logs (Option C) are for VPC network traffic, AWS CloudTrail (Option D) logs API activity, and Amazon S3 bucket policies (Option E) control S3 access.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_62',
  'To identify why specific network traffic is being blocked to or from an Amazon EC2 instance within a VPC, which of the following should be examined? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Security Group rules","Network ACL rules","Amazon Route 53 record sets","AWS Certificate Manager certificates","Elastic Load Balancer listener rules"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'When troubleshooting blocked network traffic for an Amazon EC2 instance, you should examine the Security Group rules (Option A) associated with the instance, as they control traffic at the instance level. You should also review the Network ACL rules (Option B) associated with the subnet where the instance resides, as they control traffic at the subnet level. Amazon Route 53 record sets (Option C) are for DNS resolution, AWS Certificate Manager certificates (Option D) are for SSL/TLS, and Elastic Load Balancer listener rules (Option E) are for load balancer traffic routing, none of which directly block traffic to/from an EC2 instance in this context.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_63',
  'When troubleshooting an EC2 instance''s inability to communicate with resources in another subnet or the internet, which VPC networking components are crucial to review? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Route Tables","Security Groups","Amazon S3 bucket policies","AWS Lambda configurations","Amazon CloudFront distributions"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'When an EC2 instance cannot communicate with other subnets or the internet, it''s crucial to review the Route Tables (Option A) associated with the instance''s subnet to ensure traffic is directed to the correct destinations (e.g., local subnet, NAT Gateway, Internet Gateway). Additionally, Security Groups (Option B) attached to the EC2 instance must be checked to ensure they allow the necessary inbound and outbound traffic. Amazon S3 bucket policies (Option C), AWS Lambda configurations (Option D), and Amazon CloudFront distributions (Option E) are unrelated to an EC2 instance''s basic network routing and access within a VPC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_64',
  'Which of the following actions can be performed on an Amazon CloudFront distribution to manage cached content? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Invalidate cached objects","Update origin server settings","Modify DNS record types","Configure VPC peering","Create new hosted zones"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'To manage cached content on an Amazon CloudFront distribution, you can Invalidate cached objects (Option A) to force CloudFront to fetch the latest version from the origin for subsequent requests. You can also Update origin server settings (Option B), which can affect how CloudFront retrieves and caches content going forward. Modifying DNS record types (Option C) and creating new hosted zones (Option E) are functions of Amazon Route 53. Configuring VPC peering (Option D) is a VPC networking feature.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_65',
  'An administrator needs to analyze all IP traffic flowing in and out of the network interfaces within an Amazon VPC to identify potential security threats and troubleshoot connectivity issues. Which AWS services can capture and store this network traffic data? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["VPC Flow Logs","AWS CloudTrail","Amazon CloudWatch Logs","AWS Config","AWS WAF"]'::json,
  '{0,2}'::int[],
  'Option A (VPC Flow Logs) is specifically designed to capture information about the IP traffic going to and from network interfaces in your VPC. This data can be published to Amazon CloudWatch Logs or Amazon S3. Option C (Amazon CloudWatch Logs) is a destination where VPC Flow Logs can publish their data, allowing for storage, monitoring, and analysis of the captured network traffic. Option B (AWS CloudTrail) records API calls made within your AWS account, not network traffic data. Option D (AWS Config) provides a detailed inventory of your AWS resources and their configurations, and records configuration changes. Option E (AWS WAF) is a web application firewall that helps protect web applications from common web exploits, but it does not capture all IP traffic at the VPC level.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_66',
  'When troubleshooting network connectivity to an Amazon EC2 instance, an administrator must consider which of the following network controls that operate at the subnet level? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Security Groups","Network Access Control Lists (NACLs)","Route Tables","Elastic Network Interfaces (ENIs)","Internet Gateway"]'::json,
  '{1,2}'::int[],
  'Option B (Network Access Control Lists (NACLs)) operate at the subnet level and act as a stateless firewall, allowing or denying traffic to and from subnets. Option C (Route Tables) are also associated with subnets and dictate where network traffic from the subnet is directed. Option A (Security Groups) operate at the instance level and are stateful firewalls. Option D (Elastic Network Interfaces (ENIs)) are attached to instances, not directly a subnet-level control. Option E (Internet Gateway) is attached to the VPC, not directly to individual subnets, though it provides connectivity for public subnets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_67',
  'An application on an Amazon EC2 instance in a private subnet cannot connect to a public API on the internet. The security group for the instance allows all outbound traffic. Which other networking components should the administrator investigate for potential issues? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Network ACLs","VPC Peering connections","Route Tables","Elastic Load Balancer configuration","AWS Direct Connect settings"]'::json,
  '{0,2}'::int[],
  'Option A (Network ACLs) are stateless and operate at the subnet level. Even if a security group allows outbound traffic, a NACL might explicitly deny outbound traffic or inbound response traffic (ap-5.3-001), preventing the connection. Option C (Route Tables) are crucial for directing traffic from the private subnet to a NAT Gateway (which then routes to an Internet Gateway) for internet access. An incorrect or missing route table entry would prevent the instance from reaching the internet. Option B (VPC Peering connections) are for inter-VPC communication, not internet access. Option D (Elastic Load Balancer configuration) is irrelevant as the instance is trying to connect to a public API, not acting as a target for an ELB. Option E (AWS Direct Connect settings) are for on-premises connectivity, not for an EC2 instance in a private subnet accessing the public internet.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_68',
  'An administrator is troubleshooting intermittent 5xx errors from an application behind an Elastic Load Balancer (ELB). Which of the following sources provide valuable information for diagnosing these issues? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["ELB Access Logs","VPC Flow Logs","AWS CloudTrail logs","Amazon S3 access logs","AWS Config history"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'Option A (ELB Access Logs) provide detailed information about requests sent to your load balancer, including client IP addresses, latencies, request and response headers, and HTTP status codes, which are essential for diagnosing application errors like 5xx. Option B (VPC Flow Logs) capture information about IP traffic going to and from network interfaces in your VPC, including those associated with the ELB and backend instances. This can help identify network connectivity issues between the ELB and its targets, or between the ELB and clients. Option C (AWS CloudTrail logs) record API calls made to AWS services, which is useful for auditing configuration changes but not for diagnosing application-level errors or network traffic. Option D (Amazon S3 access logs) are for S3 bucket access, not for an ELB. Option E (AWS Config history) tracks resource configuration changes, which is useful for auditing but not for real-time traffic analysis.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_69',
  'Which AWS services can assist in diagnosing and resolving network reachability problems between two resources within your AWS environment, such as an EC2 instance and a VPC endpoint? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["AWS Reachability Analyzer","VPC Flow Logs","AWS CloudFormation","Amazon Route 53 Resolver","AWS WAF"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'Option A (AWS Reachability Analyzer) is a network diagnostics tool that enables you to analyze and troubleshoot network reachability between specified source and destination resources in your VPCs. It provides path analysis and identifies potential blocking components like security groups or route tables. Option B (VPC Flow Logs) capture information about IP traffic, which can be reviewed to see if traffic is actually reaching the destination or being dropped, providing crucial data for troubleshooting. Option C (AWS CloudFormation) is an infrastructure as code service, not a troubleshooting tool. Option D (Amazon Route 53 Resolver) is for DNS resolution, not for analyzing network path reachability. Option E (AWS WAF) is a web application firewall, not a general network reachability analyzer.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_70',
  'An administrator is troubleshooting why an Amazon EC2 instance cannot be reached via SSH from a bastion host in the same VPC. Which of the following are common network configuration areas to check? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Security Groups","Network ACLs","AWS Direct Connect status","VPC Peering connections","Amazon CloudFront distribution settings"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'Option A (Security Groups) are stateful firewalls that control inbound and outbound traffic for an EC2 instance. The security group attached to the target EC2 instance must allow inbound SSH traffic from the bastion host''s IP address or security group. Option B (Network ACLs) are stateless firewalls that control traffic at the subnet level. The NACL for the subnet containing the target EC2 instance must allow both inbound SSH traffic and outbound ephemeral port traffic for the SSH response (ap-5.3-001). Option C (AWS Direct Connect status) is for on-premises connectivity and irrelevant for inter-instance communication within a VPC. Option D (VPC Peering connections) are for communication between different VPCs, not within the same VPC. Option E (Amazon CloudFront distribution settings) are for content delivery and unrelated to EC2 instance SSH access.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_71',
  'A user reports that a website served via Amazon CloudFront is displaying outdated content. Which actions can the administrator take to resolve this issue? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Invalidate the CloudFront cache","Update the DNS records in Amazon Route 53","Modify the CloudFront distribution''s cache behaviors","Restart the origin EC2 instance","Change the S3 bucket policy"]'::json,
  '{0,2}'::int[],
  'Option A (Invalidate the CloudFront cache) is a direct way to force CloudFront to remove cached content from its edge locations, ensuring that subsequent requests fetch the latest content from the origin. Option C (Modify the CloudFront distribution''s cache behaviors) allows you to adjust the Time To Live (TTL) settings for specific paths or file types, which can prevent future caching of outdated content by setting appropriate expiration times. Option B (Update the DNS records in Amazon Route 53) is for routing traffic to different origins, not for refreshing cached content. Option D (Restart the origin EC2 instance) might refresh content on the origin, but it won''t clear the CloudFront cache. Option E (Change the S3 bucket policy) affects access to the origin content but doesn''t directly control CloudFront''s caching behavior.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_72',
  'Which of the following statements accurately describe the differences between Security Groups and Network Access Control Lists (NACLs) in an Amazon VPC? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Security Groups are stateless, while NACLs are stateful.","Security Groups operate at the instance level, while NACLs operate at the subnet level.","Security Groups support allow rules only, while NACLs support both allow and deny rules.","Security Groups apply to all instances in a subnet, while NACLs apply to specific instances.","Security Groups are evaluated before NACLs for inbound traffic."]'::json,
  '{1,2}'::int[],
  'Option B (Security Groups operate at the instance level, while NACLs operate at the subnet level) is correct. Security Groups act as a virtual firewall for individual EC2 instances, whereas NACLs act as a firewall for subnets. Option C (Security Groups support allow rules only, while NACLs support both allow and deny rules) is also correct. Security Groups are permissive (you can only specify allow rules), while NACLs allow you to explicitly define both allow and deny rules. Option A (Security Groups are stateless, while NACLs are stateful) is incorrect; it''s the opposite. Security Groups are stateful (return traffic is automatically allowed), while NACLs are stateless (both inbound and outbound rules must be explicitly defined for return traffic, ap-5.3-001). Option D (Security Groups apply to all instances in a subnet, while NACLs apply to specific instances) is incorrect; Security Groups are associated with instances, and NACLs with subnets. Option E (Security Groups are evaluated before NACLs for inbound traffic) is incorrect; NACLs are evaluated first for inbound traffic, then Security Groups.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_73',
  'An administrator wants to monitor the health and performance of an Elastic Load Balancer (ELB) and its registered targets. Which metrics and logs are most relevant for this task? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Target connection error count","VPC Flow Logs","HealthyHostCount metric","AWS CloudTrail events","Amazon S3 storage metrics"]'::json,
  '{0,2}'::int[],
  'Option A (Target connection error count) is a crucial metric provided by ELB that indicates issues with the load balancer connecting to its registered targets, helping to diagnose backend server problems. Option C (HealthyHostCount metric) directly shows the number of healthy targets registered with the load balancer, which is a primary indicator of the application''s availability and performance. Option B (VPC Flow Logs) capture network traffic at the ENI level, which can be useful for deep network troubleshooting but is not the primary source for ELB health and performance metrics. Option D (AWS CloudTrail events) track API calls and management events, not runtime performance or health. Option E (Amazon S3 storage metrics) are for S3 buckets and unrelated to ELB monitoring.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_74',
  'A company is experiencing intermittent network latency when accessing an Amazon EC2 instance. The instance''s security group allows the necessary inbound and outbound traffic. Which additional network configurations should be examined to troubleshoot the latency? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Route Tables","VPC Flow Logs","AWS Certificate Manager settings","Amazon Route 53 record types","Elastic Load Balancer listener rules"]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'Option A (Route Tables) are critical for directing traffic within the VPC and to external networks. Incorrect or suboptimal routing (e.g., routing through an unnecessary NAT Gateway instead of a direct path) can introduce latency. Option B (VPC Flow Logs) can capture detailed information about network traffic, including source/destination IP, ports, and action (ACCEPT/REJECT). Analyzing these logs can reveal if traffic is being dropped or routed unexpectedly, contributing to latency. Option C (AWS Certificate Manager settings) are for SSL/TLS certificates and do not directly impact network latency. Option D (Amazon Route 53 record types) are for DNS resolution, which can affect initial connection setup but not ongoing network latency once a connection is established. Option E (Elastic Load Balancer listener rules) are for traffic distribution to targets and are not directly relevant if the issue is with a single EC2 instance''s network latency.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  62,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_75',
  'An administrator needs to ensure that an Elastic Load Balancer (ELB) gracefully stops sending new requests to a target instance that is being deregistered or failing health checks, allowing existing requests to complete. Which ELB feature should be configured? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Connection Draining","Sticky Sessions","Target Group Health Checks","Cross-Zone Load Balancing","Request Routing"]'::json,
  '{0,2}'::int[],
  'Option A (Connection Draining) is the feature that allows an ELB to stop sending new requests to a deregistering or unhealthy instance while keeping existing connections open for a specified duration, ensuring that ongoing requests can complete gracefully. Option C (Target Group Health Checks) are essential for identifying unhealthy instances and initiating the deregistration process, which then triggers connection draining. Without proper health checks, the ELB wouldn''t know when to stop sending traffic to a problematic instance. Option B (Sticky Sessions) ensures that requests from a user are always sent to the same target instance. Option D (Cross-Zone Load Balancing) distributes traffic evenly across all registered instances in all enabled Availability Zones. Option E (Request Routing) refers to how the ELB directs requests based on rules, not how it handles instance deregistration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  63,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_76',
  'A SysOps Administrator is investigating intermittent connectivity issues for an application hosted on Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer (ALB). Users report occasional timeouts and slow responses. The administrator suspects network-related problems. Which two logging and analysis services can provide valuable insights into the network traffic and load balancer behavior? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["Amazon CloudWatch Logs for EC2 instance system logs.","VPC Flow Logs for network traffic visibility within the VPC.","AWS CloudTrail for API call history related to networking.","Elastic Load Balancing (ELB) access logs for client request details.","AWS Config for configuration changes to network resources."]'::json,
  '{1,3}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) is VPC Flow Logs. VPC Flow Logs capture information about IP traffic going to and from network interfaces in your VPC. They can help identify if traffic is reaching the instances, if security groups or NACLs are dropping packets, or if there are unexpected traffic patterns, which is crucial for diagnosing intermittent connectivity issues. Option D (correct) is Elastic Load Balancing (ELB) access logs. ELB access logs capture detailed information about requests sent to your load balancer, including client IP addresses, request times, latency, and response codes. This data is invaluable for understanding client request patterns, identifying slow responses, and troubleshooting timeouts related to the ALB.
Option A (incorrect) is Amazon CloudWatch Logs for EC2 instance system logs. While EC2 system logs (e.g., `/var/log/messages`) can provide insights into instance-level issues, they don''t directly provide network traffic visibility within the VPC or load balancer behavior. The question specifically asks for network traffic and load balancer behavior. Option C (incorrect) is AWS CloudTrail. CloudTrail records API calls made to AWS services, which is useful for auditing and security, but it does not provide details about network traffic flow or load balancer requests. Option E (incorrect) is AWS Config. AWS Config records configuration changes to your AWS resources, which is useful for compliance and auditing, but it does not provide real-time or historical data on network traffic or load balancer access patterns.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  64,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_77',
  'A SysOps Administrator needs to analyze the network path between an Amazon EC2 instance in a private subnet and a database instance in another private subnet within the same VPC. The administrator suspects a misconfiguration in route tables or security groups. Which two AWS services or features are specifically designed to help analyze and troubleshoot network reachability in such a scenario? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["VPC Flow Logs to capture traffic between the instances.","AWS Reachability Analyzer to determine the network path.","Amazon CloudWatch metrics for network utilization.","AWS Config to review resource configuration history.","ELB access logs to check connection attempts."]'::json,
  '{0,1}'::int[],
  'Option A (correct) is VPC Flow Logs. VPC Flow Logs capture information about IP traffic going to and from network interfaces in your VPC. By analyzing flow logs, the administrator can see if traffic is reaching the destination, if it''s being accepted or rejected by security groups or NACLs, and identify the source of dropped packets. Option B (correct) is AWS Reachability Analyzer. Reachability Analyzer is a network diagnostics tool that enables you to analyze and debug network reachability between two resources in your VPCs. It provides a detailed path analysis, highlighting any blocking security groups, NACLs, or incorrect route table entries, which directly addresses the scenario of suspected misconfigurations.
Option C (incorrect) is Amazon CloudWatch metrics for network utilization. CloudWatch metrics provide performance data like network in/out bytes, but they don''t show the actual network path or identify specific blocking rules. Option D (incorrect) is AWS Config. AWS Config records configuration changes to your AWS resources, which can be useful for auditing, but it doesn''t actively analyze network reachability or troubleshoot live traffic flow. Option E (incorrect) is ELB access logs. ELB access logs are specific to Elastic Load Balancers and capture client request details. They are not relevant for troubleshooting direct connectivity between two EC2 instances within private subnets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  65,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_78',
  'A SysOps Administrator is investigating why an Amazon CloudFront distribution is serving outdated content to users despite recent updates to the origin. The administrator has confirmed that the origin server has the latest content. Which two areas should the administrator investigate to resolve the stale content issue? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["The Cache-Control headers configured on the origin server for the affected content.","The DNS records in Amazon Route 53 pointing to the CloudFront distribution.","Any active CloudFront invalidation requests for the content paths.","The security group rules on the origin server instances.","The CloudFront distribution''s origin domain name configuration."]'::json,
  '{0,2}'::int[],
  'Option A (correct) is the Cache-Control headers configured on the origin server. CloudFront respects `Cache-Control` headers (e.g., `max-age`, `s-maxage`, `no-cache`, `no-store`) from the origin. If these headers are set to a long duration, CloudFront will continue to serve cached content until the TTL expires, leading to stale content. Option C (correct) is any active CloudFront invalidation requests. If content has been updated on the origin, an invalidation request needs to be issued for the specific paths in CloudFront to force edge locations to fetch the new content. If no invalidation was performed, or it''s still in progress, users might see stale content.
Option B (incorrect) is the DNS records in Amazon Route 53. DNS records for CloudFront distributions typically point to the distribution''s domain name (e.g., d123.cloudfront.net). Changes to DNS records would affect how users *reach* the distribution, not the freshness of content *served by* the distribution from its origin. Option D (incorrect) is the security group rules on the origin server instances. Security group rules control network access to the origin. If they were misconfigured, CloudFront wouldn''t be able to reach the origin at all, resulting in errors, not stale content. Option E (incorrect) is the CloudFront distribution''s origin domain name configuration. If the origin domain name was incorrect, CloudFront would fail to retrieve content, leading to errors, not stale content from the correct origin.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  66,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_79',
  'A company is experiencing issues with its web application where users are intermittently disconnected from their sessions. The application uses an Application Load Balancer (ALB) in front of Amazon EC2 instances. The SysOps Administrator suspects that instances are being terminated or unregistered from the ALB while still processing active requests. Which two ELB features should the administrator investigate to address this issue? (Select TWO.)',
  'multiple',
  '["The target group health check configuration.","The connection draining (deregistration delay) setting on the target group.","The cross-zone load balancing setting on the ALB.","The sticky sessions (session affinity) configuration on the target group.","The access logs for the Application Load Balancer."]'::json,
  '{1,4}'::int[],
  'Option B (correct) is the connection draining (deregistration delay) setting on the target group. Connection draining ensures that the load balancer stops sending new requests to an instance that is deregistering or unhealthy, but keeps existing connections open for a specified duration to allow in-flight requests to complete. This directly addresses intermittent disconnections during instance deregistration. Option E (correct) is the access logs for the Application Load Balancer. ALB access logs provide detailed information about client requests, including the time, client IP, target IP, and response codes. Analyzing these logs can help identify patterns of disconnections, such as requests failing or being terminated when an instance is deregistering, and correlate them with instance lifecycle events.
Option A (incorrect) is the target group health check configuration. Health checks determine if an instance is healthy and can receive traffic. While important, misconfigured health checks would lead to instances being marked unhealthy and removed from rotation, not necessarily intermittent disconnections of *active* sessions during deregistration. Option C (incorrect) is the cross-zone load balancing setting. Cross-zone load balancing distributes traffic evenly across all registered instances in all enabled Availability Zones. While it affects traffic distribution, it doesn''t directly address the issue of active sessions being cut off during instance deregistration. Option D (incorrect) is the sticky sessions (session affinity) configuration. Sticky sessions ensure that requests from a specific client are always routed to the same target instance. While this can prevent session loss if an instance is still healthy, it doesn''t solve the problem of sessions being cut off when an instance is *deregistering* or *terminating*.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  67,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'hard'
