INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_22_m_22_1_91',
  'A large financial institution operates a critical application on Amazon EC2 instances, requiring comprehensive monitoring beyond default metrics to include memory utilization and disk space. The operations team needs to collect these custom metrics efficiently, ensuring high availability and minimal manual overhead for configuration management across a fleet of instances. Order the steps required to implement this enhanced monitoring solution using the CloudWatch agent.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new IAM role with permissions to publish CloudWatch metrics and attach it to the EC2 instances.","Install the CloudWatch agent on the EC2 instances and configure it to collect memory and disk space metrics.","Define a CloudWatch agent configuration file (`amazon-cloudwatch-agent.json`) specifying desired metrics and log collection.","Start the CloudWatch agent service on the EC2 instances to begin sending custom metrics to Amazon CloudWatch."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}'::int[],
  'To implement enhanced monitoring for custom metrics like memory and disk space on EC2 instances, the correct sequence of steps is crucial. Option A (correct) is the initial step: an IAM role with appropriate permissions (e.g., `CloudWatchAgentServerPolicy`) must be created and attached to the EC2 instances, allowing the CloudWatch agent to publish data. Option C (correct) follows, where a configuration file is defined to specify which metrics (e.g., `mem_used_percent`, `disk_used_percent`) and logs the agent should collect. Option B (correct) involves installing the CloudWatch agent on the instances, typically using AWS Systems Manager Run Command or a user data script, and then applying the configuration file. Finally, Option D (correct) is to start the CloudWatch agent service, which will then begin collecting and sending the specified custom metrics to Amazon CloudWatch. This ensures that the agent has the necessary permissions, knows what to collect, is installed, and is actively running.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain22_5',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_22_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_22_m_22_1_92',
  'A compliance-driven organization needs to centralize application logs from various Amazon EC2 instances into Amazon CloudWatch Logs. The logs must be retained for a specific period (e.g., 90 days for operational analysis, 365 days for audit) to meet regulatory requirements, and older logs should be automatically archived or deleted to manage costs. Order the steps to establish this logging solution with appropriate retention policies.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure the CloudWatch agent on EC2 instances to send application logs to the created CloudWatch Log Group.","Create a new CloudWatch Log Group for the application logs.","Set the desired retention policy (e.g., 90 days) for the CloudWatch Log Group.","Verify that logs are flowing into the Log Group and retention is correctly applied."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}'::int[],
  'Establishing a centralized logging solution with specific retention policies requires a logical order of operations. Option B (correct) is the first step: a CloudWatch Log Group must be created to serve as the destination for the application logs. Option C (correct) follows, where the appropriate retention policy (e.g., 90 days) is configured for this newly created Log Group. Without this, logs would be stored indefinitely, incurring unbounded storage costs, which is an anti-pattern (ap-1.1-002). Option A (correct) then involves configuring the CloudWatch agent on the EC2 instances to stream the application logs to this specific Log Group. Finally, Option D (correct) is to verify the entire setup, ensuring logs are successfully ingested and the retention policy is actively managing their lifecycle.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain22_5',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_22_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_22_m_22_1_93',
  'An e-commerce platform experiences occasional spikes in CPU utilization on its backend Amazon EC2 instances, which can lead to degraded customer experience. The operations team needs to be immediately notified via Amazon SNS when the average CPU utilization of a specific Auto Scaling group exceeds 80% for five consecutive minutes. Order the steps to configure a CloudWatch alarm for this scenario.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Amazon SNS topic to receive alarm notifications.","Define the CloudWatch alarm, specifying the metric (CPUUtilization), statistic (Average), period (5 minutes), and threshold (80%).","Configure the alarm action to send notifications to the created Amazon SNS topic.","Select the EC2 metric namespace and the specific Auto Scaling group for the alarm."]'::json,
  '{0,3,1,2}'::int[],
  'To set up a CloudWatch alarm for high CPU utilization, a logical sequence ensures all dependencies are met. Option A (correct) is the initial step: an Amazon SNS topic must be created first, as it will be the destination for the alarm notifications. Option D (correct) then involves selecting the correct metric namespace (AWS/EC2) and identifying the specific Auto Scaling group whose CPU utilization needs to be monitored. Option B (correct) is where the core alarm definition occurs, setting the metric, statistic, period, and the 80% threshold. Finally, Option C (correct) links the alarm to the SNS topic, configuring it to publish notifications when the alarm state changes to `ALARM`.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain22_5',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_22_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_22_m_22_1_94',
  'A development team has multiple individual CloudWatch alarms for an application''s health, such as high CPU, low disk space, and application errors. They are experiencing alert fatigue due to multiple alarms firing simultaneously for a single underlying issue. To reduce noise and provide a clearer indication of overall application health, they want to consolidate these into a single, high-level alarm. Order the steps to create a composite alarm for this purpose.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the alarm rule expression using `AND` or `OR` logic to combine the states of the individual alarms.","Create the individual CloudWatch alarms for CPU, disk space, and application errors if they don''t already exist.","Specify the actions to be taken when the composite alarm enters an `ALARM` state, such as sending an Amazon SNS notification.","Name the composite alarm and provide a description for clarity."]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}'::int[],
  'To effectively reduce alert noise and provide a consolidated view of application health, creating a composite alarm is the recommended approach, addressing anti-pattern ap-1.1-003. Option B (correct) is the foundational step: ensure all individual CloudWatch alarms (e.g., for CPU, disk, errors) are already created and functioning correctly, as the composite alarm will depend on their states. Option D (correct) then involves naming the new composite alarm and providing a clear description. Option A (correct) is the core of the composite alarm, where the alarm rule expression is defined using logical operators (`AND`, `OR`) to combine the states of the individual alarms. For example, `ALARM(CPUAlarm) AND ALARM(DiskAlarm)` would only trigger if both are in ALARM. Finally, Option C (correct) specifies the actions, such as sending an Amazon SNS notification, when the composite alarm itself transitions into an `ALARM` state.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain22_5',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_22_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_22_m_22_1_95',
  'An operations engineer needs to quickly troubleshoot an intermittent application error by analyzing historical log data stored in Amazon CloudWatch Logs. The engineer suspects a specific error message pattern and wants to identify its frequency and associated timestamps over the last hour across multiple log streams within a log group. Order the steps to efficiently query this log data using CloudWatch Logs Insights.',
  'ordering',
  '["Select the specific CloudWatch Log Group(s) containing the application logs.","Enter a CloudWatch Logs Insights query to filter for the error message pattern and extract relevant fields.","Specify the desired time range for the query (e.g., ''Last 1 hour'').","Run the query and analyze the results to identify the frequency and timestamps of the error."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}'::int[],
  'To efficiently troubleshoot application errors using CloudWatch Logs Insights, a specific sequence of actions is followed. Option A (correct) is the initial step: the engineer must first select the relevant CloudWatch Log Group(s) that contain the application logs to be analyzed. Option C (correct) then involves specifying the desired time range for the query, such as ''Last 1 hour'', to narrow down the data. Option B (correct) is where the actual query is constructed using CloudWatch Logs Insights syntax to filter for the specific error message pattern and extract any necessary fields. Finally, Option D (correct) is to execute the query and review the returned results, which will show the frequency and timestamps of the identified error messages.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain22_5',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_22_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_22_m_22_1_96',
  'A DevOps team is responsible for maintaining compliance for Amazon S3 buckets, specifically ensuring that all new and existing buckets have server-side encryption enabled. They need an automated solution to continuously monitor their AWS environment for non-compliant S3 buckets and report any violations. Order the steps to deploy an AWS Config rule to enforce this security posture.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an AWS Config rule, selecting the `s3-bucket-server-side-encryption-enabled` managed rule.","Enable AWS Config service recording for S3 buckets in the desired AWS Region(s).","Specify the scope of the rule to include all S3 buckets in the account.","Review the compliance dashboard and remediation actions for non-compliant S3 buckets."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}'::int[],
  'To deploy an AWS Config rule for monitoring S3 bucket encryption, a structured approach is required. Option B (correct) is the foundational step: AWS Config service recording must be enabled for S3 buckets in the relevant AWS Region(s) to track resource configuration changes. Option A (correct) then involves creating the AWS Config rule itself, specifically selecting the `s3-bucket-server-side-encryption-enabled` managed rule. Option C (correct) is to define the scope of the rule, ensuring it applies to all S3 buckets in the account. Finally, Option D (correct) is to review the AWS Config compliance dashboard to identify non-compliant buckets and plan any necessary remediation actions, such as enabling default encryption.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain22_5',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_22_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_22_m_22_1_97',
  'A operations manager requires a consolidated view of the health and performance of their critical application, which spans multiple AWS services including Amazon EC2, Amazon RDS, and AWS Lambda. They need a single pane of glass to quickly assess key metrics, alarms, and logs without navigating between different service consoles. Order the steps to create a comprehensive CloudWatch Dashboard for this purpose.',
  'ordering',
  '["Add widgets to the dashboard, selecting relevant metrics from EC2, RDS, and Lambda, and any associated CloudWatch alarms.","Create a new CloudWatch Dashboard in the Amazon CloudWatch console.","Arrange and resize the widgets on the dashboard for optimal visibility and readability.","Save the dashboard and share it with relevant team members."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}'::int[],
  'To create a comprehensive CloudWatch Dashboard for monitoring application health, a logical progression of steps is followed. Option B (correct) is the initial action: a new CloudWatch Dashboard must be created in the Amazon CloudWatch console. Option A (correct) then involves adding various widgets to this dashboard, pulling in key metrics from services like EC2, RDS, and Lambda, as well as displaying the states of relevant CloudWatch alarms. Option C (correct) is to organize and resize these widgets to ensure a clear and intuitive layout for quick assessment. Finally, Option D (correct) is to save the configured dashboard and share it with other team members who need access to this consolidated view.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain22_5',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_22_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_22_m_22_1_98',
  'An operations team wants to reduce alert fatigue by combining multiple related alarms into a single actionable notification. Order the steps to implement a composite alarm in Amazon CloudWatch.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the alarm rule using logical AND/OR operators.","Create the composite alarm, referencing the individual alarms and the rule.","Configure an Amazon SNS topic for the composite alarm''s notifications.","Create individual Amazon CloudWatch alarms for specific metrics."]'::json,
  '{3,0,1,2}'::int[],
  'Implementing a composite alarm starts with defining the foundational alarms it will monitor. Option D (Create individual Amazon CloudWatch alarms for specific metrics) is the first step, as composite alarms depend on the states of other alarms. Once the individual alarms exist, Option A (Define the alarm rule using logical AND/OR operators) specifies how their states will combine to determine the composite alarm''s state. Then, Option B (Create the composite alarm, referencing the individual alarms and the rule) brings these components together. Finally, Option C (Configure an Amazon SNS topic for the composite alarm''s notifications) ensures that alerts are sent when the composite alarm changes state, effectively reducing alert noise by providing a single, consolidated notification for related issues.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain22_5',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_22_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

