INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_23_m_23_1_75',
  'A rapidly growing online education platform experiences fluctuating traffic patterns throughout the day, with peak loads during evening hours. The platform''s web tier, hosted on Amazon EC2 instances, needs to automatically scale out and in based on demand to maintain optimal performance and control costs. The operations team decides to implement an EC2 Auto Scaling group with a Target Tracking scaling policy based on average CPU utilization. Order the following steps to configure this elastic scaling solution.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an EC2 Launch Template specifying the AMI, instance type, security groups, and user data for the web application.","Define an EC2 Auto Scaling group, linking it to the launch template and specifying the desired, minimum, and maximum capacity.","Configure a Target Tracking scaling policy for the Auto Scaling group, targeting an average CPU utilization of 50%.","Attach the Auto Scaling group to an existing Application Load Balancer to distribute incoming traffic."]'::json,
  '{0,1,3,2}'::int[],
  'Configuring EC2 Auto Scaling begins with defining the instance configuration. Option A (Create an EC2 Launch Template specifying the AMI, instance type, security groups, and user data for the web application) is the initial step, as it dictates how instances will be launched. Next, Option B (Define an EC2 Auto Scaling group, linking it to the launch template and specifying the desired, minimum, and maximum capacity) establishes the group that will manage the instances. Option D (Attach the Auto Scaling group to an existing Application Load Balancer to distribute incoming traffic) integrates the ASG with the load balancer, allowing it to receive traffic. Finally, Option C (Configure a Target Tracking scaling policy for the Auto Scaling group, targeting an average CPU utilization of 50%) is the crucial step to enable dynamic scaling based on a performance metric, which is generally preferred over simple scaling policies (ap-2.1-001) for smoother adjustments and less operational overhead.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain23_5',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_23_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_23_m_23_1_76',
  'A popular social media application experiences heavy read traffic to its Amazon RDS MySQL database, particularly during peak hours, leading to performance bottlenecks. To alleviate the load on the primary database instance and improve read throughput, the operations team decides to implement Amazon RDS Read Replicas. Order the following steps to effectively scale the read capacity of the database.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create one or more Amazon RDS Read Replicas from the primary MySQL database instance.","Modify the application code to direct read queries to the Read Replica endpoint.","Monitor the performance metrics of both the primary instance and the Read Replicas to ensure load distribution.","Ensure the primary Amazon RDS MySQL instance has automated backups enabled, as this is a prerequisite for Read Replicas."]'::json,
  '{3,0,1,2}'::int[],
  'Creating Amazon RDS Read Replicas has a prerequisite. Option D (Ensure the primary Amazon RDS MySQL instance has automated backups enabled, as this is a prerequisite for Read Replicas) is the necessary first step, as Read Replicas rely on the primary instance''s automated backups for replication. Next, Option A (Create one or more Amazon RDS Read Replicas from the primary MySQL database instance) provisions the read-only copies of the database. Option B (Modify the application code to direct read queries to the Read Replica endpoint) is crucial for the application to actually utilize the new read capacity. Finally, Option C (Monitor the performance metrics of both the primary instance and the Read Replicas to ensure load distribution) is essential for validating the effectiveness of the scaling solution and identifying any further bottlenecks.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain23_5',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_23_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_23_m_23_1_77',
  'A rapidly growing mobile gaming company uses Amazon DynamoDB for storing player data, game states, and leaderboards. The application experiences highly unpredictable traffic patterns, with sudden spikes during new game releases or promotional events. To ensure consistent performance without over-provisioning and to minimize operational overhead, the operations team decides to configure the DynamoDB tables to use On-Demand capacity mode. Order the following steps to implement this cost-effective and elastic capacity management strategy.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new Amazon DynamoDB table or modify an existing one.","Set the capacity mode for the DynamoDB table to On-Demand.","Monitor the DynamoDB table''s performance and cost metrics to ensure it meets application requirements.","Ensure the application is configured to handle potential throttling if traffic exceeds On-Demand burst capacity limits."]'::json,
  '{0,1,3,2}'::int[],
  'Configuring DynamoDB On-Demand capacity starts with the table itself. Option A (Create a new Amazon DynamoDB table or modify an existing one) is the initial step, as the capacity mode is set at the table level. Next, Option B (Set the capacity mode for the DynamoDB table to On-Demand) directly implements the desired scaling strategy. Option D (Ensure the application is configured to handle potential throttling if traffic exceeds On-Demand burst capacity limits) is a critical application-level consideration, as even On-Demand has limits and the application should be resilient. Finally, Option C (Monitor the DynamoDB table''s performance and cost metrics to ensure it meets application requirements) is essential for validating the effectiveness and cost-efficiency of the On-Demand configuration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain23_5',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_23_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_23_m_23_1_78',
  'A media streaming service needs to distribute incoming user requests across a fleet of Amazon EC2 instances that host its video encoding microservices. To ensure high availability and scalability, the operations team must deploy an Application Load Balancer (ALB) to efficiently route traffic and manage instance health. Order the following steps to configure an Application Load Balancer for this purpose.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Application Load Balancer (ALB) in the desired VPC and Availability Zones.","Define one or more target groups, specifying the protocol and port for the EC2 instances.","Configure ALB listeners to forward incoming client requests on specific ports to the appropriate target groups.","Register the Amazon EC2 instances with their respective target groups."]'::json,
  '{0,1,3,2}'::int[],
  'Setting up an Application Load Balancer begins with provisioning the load balancer itself. Option A (Create an Application Load Balancer (ALB) in the desired VPC and Availability Zones) is the initial step to deploy the ALB. Next, Option B (Define one or more target groups, specifying the protocol and port for the EC2 instances) creates logical groupings for the backend instances. Option D (Register the Amazon EC2 instances with their respective target groups) links the actual compute resources to these target groups. Finally, Option C (Configure ALB listeners to forward incoming client requests on specific ports to the appropriate target groups) defines how the ALB accepts and routes incoming traffic to the appropriate backend services.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain23_5',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_23_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_23_m_23_1_79',
  'A data processing company runs its batch processing jobs on an Amazon ECS service, which experiences varying workloads throughout the day. To optimize resource utilization and ensure timely job completion, the operations team needs to implement automatic scaling for the ECS service based on its CPU utilization. Order the following steps to configure AWS Application Auto Scaling for this Amazon ECS service.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define a scaling policy for the scalable target, specifying a target tracking policy based on average CPU utilization.","Ensure the Amazon ECS service is running and configured with a task definition.","Register the Amazon ECS service as a scalable target with AWS Application Auto Scaling.","Monitor the ECS service''s CPU utilization and scaling events in Amazon CloudWatch."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}'::int[],
  'To scale an Amazon ECS service, the service must first be operational. Option B (Ensure the Amazon ECS service is running and configured with a task definition) is the prerequisite step, as Application Auto Scaling operates on existing ECS services. Next, Option C (Register the Amazon ECS service as a scalable target with AWS Application Auto Scaling) makes the service eligible for automatic scaling. Option A (Define a scaling policy for the scalable target, specifying a target tracking policy based on average CPU utilization) then configures the actual scaling logic. Finally, Option D (Monitor the ECS service''s CPU utilization and scaling events in Amazon CloudWatch) is essential for observing the effectiveness of the auto-scaling policy and making any necessary adjustments.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain23_5',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_23_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_23_m_23_1_80',
  'A rapidly growing SaaS application uses an Amazon RDS MySQL database, and its storage utilization is increasing steadily. To prevent storage full issues and minimize operational overhead, the operations team needs to enable Amazon RDS Storage Auto Scaling. This feature should automatically expand the database storage capacity when needed, up to a defined maximum. Order the following steps to implement and manage RDS Storage Auto Scaling.',
  'ordering',
  '["Modify the Amazon RDS MySQL instance to enable Storage Auto Scaling and set a maximum storage threshold.","Monitor the Amazon RDS instance''s storage utilization metrics in Amazon CloudWatch to observe auto-scaling events.","Ensure the Amazon RDS instance is running on an engine version that supports Storage Auto Scaling.","Review the billing dashboard to understand the cost implications of increased storage capacity."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}'::int[],
  'Enabling Amazon RDS Storage Auto Scaling requires a compatible engine version. Option C (Ensure the Amazon RDS instance is running on an engine version that supports Storage Auto Scaling) is the necessary prerequisite. Next, Option A (Modify the Amazon RDS MySQL instance to enable Storage Auto Scaling and set a maximum storage threshold) directly configures the auto-scaling feature. Option B (Monitor the Amazon RDS instance''s storage utilization metrics in Amazon CloudWatch to observe auto-scaling events) is crucial for verifying that the feature is working as expected and scaling storage when needed. Finally, Option D (Review the billing dashboard to understand the cost implications of increased storage capacity) is an important operational consideration to manage costs associated with automatic expansion.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain23_5',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_23_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_23_m_23_1_81',
  'An analytics dashboard application frequently queries an Amazon RDS PostgreSQL database, leading to high database load and slow response times for users. To improve application performance and reduce the strain on the database, the operations team decides to implement an Amazon ElastiCache for Redis cluster as a caching layer for frequently accessed data. Order the following steps to integrate ElastiCache into the application architecture.',
  'ordering',
  '["Launch an Amazon ElastiCache for Redis cluster in the same VPC as the application''s EC2 instances.","Modify the application code to first check the ElastiCache cluster for data before querying the Amazon RDS database.","Ensure the security group associated with the ElastiCache cluster allows inbound traffic from the application''s EC2 instances.","Populate the ElastiCache cluster with frequently accessed data from the Amazon RDS database."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}'::int[],
  'Integrating Amazon ElastiCache begins with provisioning the cache cluster. Option A (Launch an Amazon ElastiCache for Redis cluster in the same VPC as the application''s EC2 instances) is the initial step to deploy the caching service. Next, Option C (Ensure the security group associated with the ElastiCache cluster allows inbound traffic from the application''s EC2 instances) is a critical networking step to enable communication between the application and the cache. Option B (Modify the application code to first check the ElastiCache cluster for data before querying the Amazon RDS database) is essential for the application to actually utilize the cache. Finally, Option D (Populate the ElastiCache cluster with frequently accessed data from the Amazon RDS database) ensures that the cache contains relevant data to serve requests efficiently.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain23_5',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_23_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_23_m_23_1_82',
  'A rapidly growing e-commerce application needs to be designed for end-to-end scalability and high availability. The architecture consists of a web tier, an application tier, and a database tier. The operations team must implement a solution that automatically scales compute resources based on demand and ensures the database can handle increasing read loads. Order the following steps to build a scalable and highly available multi-tier architecture using Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling, Application Load Balancing, and Amazon RDS Read Replicas.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure an Application Load Balancer (ALB) to distribute incoming web traffic to the web tier''s Auto Scaling group.","Deploy a Multi-AZ Amazon RDS database instance for the primary database.","Create an EC2 Auto Scaling group for the web and application tiers, configured across multiple Availability Zones with a target tracking policy.","Provision Amazon RDS Read Replicas from the primary database to offload read-heavy workloads."]'::json,
  '{1,3,2,0}'::int[],
  'Building a scalable multi-tier architecture starts with the foundational database layer. Option B (Deploy a Multi-AZ Amazon RDS database instance for the primary database) establishes a highly available and durable database. Next, Option D (Provision Amazon RDS Read Replicas from the primary database to offload read-heavy workloads) scales the database''s read capacity. Option C (Create an EC2 Auto Scaling group for the web and application tiers, configured across multiple Availability Zones with a target tracking policy) then provides scalable and highly available compute resources for the application. Finally, Option A (Configure an Application Load Balancer (ALB) to distribute incoming web traffic to the web tier''s Auto Scaling group) serves as the entry point, distributing traffic across the scalable web tier.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain23_5',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_23_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_23_m_23_1_83',
  'An administrator needs to configure an Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling group to automatically adjust capacity based on demand. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to set up a basic Auto Scaling group.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define a launch template that includes the Amazon Machine Image (AMI), instance type, and security groups.","Create an Elastic Load Balancer and attach it to the Auto Scaling group for distributing traffic.","Configure scaling policies (e.g., target tracking) to define how the group scales in and out.","Create an Auto Scaling group, specifying the launch template and desired capacity."]'::json,
  '{0,3,1,2}'::int[],
  'To set up an Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling group, the foundational components must be created in a logical sequence. Option A (Define a launch template) is the first step, as the launch template specifies the configuration for the EC2 instances that the Auto Scaling group will launch. Option D (Create an Auto Scaling group) comes next, as it uses the previously defined launch template to create the group. Option B (Create an Elastic Load Balancer and attach it) is typically done after the Auto Scaling group is created, allowing the ELB to distribute incoming traffic to the instances launched by the group. Finally, Option C (Configure scaling policies) defines how the Auto Scaling group will dynamically adjust its capacity based on metrics, which is applied to the already configured group and its associated ELB.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain23_5',
  NOW(),
  62,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_23_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

