INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_1_141',
  'A security team wants to centralize security findings from various AWS services and monitor compliance against industry best practices using AWS Security Hub. Order the steps to configure Security Hub for this purpose.',
  'ordering',
  '["Remediate critical security findings identified by Security Hub","Enable integrations with other AWS security services (e.g., GuardDuty, Inspector)","Enable AWS Security Hub in the console for the desired region","Review security standards (e.g., AWS Foundational Security Best Practices) and their controls"]'::json,
  '{2,1,3,0}'::int[],
  'Centralizing security findings with Security Hub starts with its activation. Option C (Enable AWS Security Hub in the console) is the initial step to activate the service. Option B (Enable integrations with other AWS security services) allows Security Hub to aggregate findings from other services. Option D (Review security standards and controls) helps the team understand the compliance posture. Option A (Remediate critical security findings) is the action taken based on the aggregated and analyzed findings.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  13,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_1_142',
  'A SysOps Administrator needs to enforce a strong password policy for all IAM users in their AWS account. Arrange the following steps to configure the IAM account password policy.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure the desired password policy requirements (e.g., minimum length, character types)","Save the changes to the account password policy","Navigate to the IAM console in the AWS Management Console","Select ''Account settings'' from the navigation pane"]'::json,
  '{2,3,0,1}'::int[],
  'Setting an IAM password policy follows a clear path in the console. Option C (Navigate to the IAM console) is the starting point to access IAM configurations. Option D (Select ''Account settings'' from the navigation pane) is where the password policy settings are located. Option A (Configure the desired password policy requirements) is where the specific rules for passwords are defined. Option B (Save the changes to the account password policy) finalizes the configuration, applying the new policy.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  14,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_1_143',
  'A security team wants to perform vulnerability scanning on their Amazon EC2 instances using Amazon Inspector. Order the steps to initiate and review a scan.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the scanning scope and target resources (e.g., EC2 instances by tags)","Remediate identified vulnerabilities based on Inspector findings","Review and analyze the generated findings in the Inspector console","Enable Amazon Inspector in the AWS console for the desired region"]'::json,
  '{3,0,2,1}'::int[],
  'To use Amazon Inspector, the service must first be enabled. Option D (Enable Amazon Inspector in the AWS console) is the prerequisite step. Option A (Define the scanning scope and target resources) specifies which resources Inspector should evaluate. Option C (Review and analyze the generated findings) is where the results of the scan are examined. Option B (Remediate identified vulnerabilities) is the action taken to address the security issues found.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  15,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_1_144',
  'A SysOps Administrator needs to deploy an AWS Config conformance pack to assess and enforce compliance with a set of best practices across multiple accounts. Arrange the steps to implement a conformance pack.',
  'ordering',
  '["Deploy the conformance pack to target accounts or Organizational Units (OUs)","Remediate non-compliant resources based on conformance pack findings","Monitor compliance status using the conformance pack dashboard in AWS Config","Define the conformance pack template (e.g., using a YAML file or a managed template)"]'::json,
  '{3,0,2,1}'::int[],
  'Implementing an AWS Config conformance pack involves defining, deploying, monitoring, and remediating. Option D (Define the conformance pack template) is the initial step where the desired compliance rules are specified. Option A (Deploy the conformance pack) applies these rules to the target environment. Option C (Monitor compliance status) is the ongoing process of checking resource adherence. Option B (Remediate non-compliant resources) is the corrective action taken when violations are detected.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  16,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_1_145',
  'A security team needs to establish a new, highly restrictive IAM policy to grant specific, minimal permissions to a new application role. This policy must then be attached to the role, and its effectiveness verified before deployment. Order the steps required to achieve this with the LEAST risk of over-permissioning.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new IAM policy document with the principle of least privilege, defining only the necessary actions and resources.","Attach the newly created IAM policy to the target application IAM role.","Use the IAM Policy Simulator to test the policy against expected actions and resources, adjusting as needed.","Review existing AWS CloudTrail logs to identify actual permissions used by similar applications to inform policy creation."]'::json,
  '{3,0,1,2}'::int[],
  'To ensure the LEAST risk of over-permissioning, it''s best to first understand actual usage patterns. Option D (Review existing AWS CloudTrail logs) provides valuable insights into the permissions truly needed by similar applications, informing a more precise policy. Option A (Create a new IAM policy document) is the next logical step, where the policy is crafted based on the gathered information, adhering to the principle of least privilege. Option B (Attach the newly created IAM policy) then associates this policy with the target role. Finally, Option C (Use the IAM Policy Simulator) is crucial for validating the policy''s effectiveness and ensuring it grants only the intended permissions without unintended side effects, before the role is put into production.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  17,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_1_146',
  'A company requires a preventive guardrail to ensure no Amazon EC2 instances can be launched outside of approved AWS Regions across all accounts in an AWS Organization. The security team must implement this using Service Control Policies (SCPs) with MINIMAL disruption to existing compliant operations. Order the steps to implement this SCP.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new SCP that explicitly denies the ''ec2:RunInstances'' action when the ''aws:RequestedRegion'' condition is not one of the approved regions.","Attach the SCP to the root of the AWS Organization or to a specific Organizational Unit (OU) containing the target accounts.","Test the SCP''s effect by attempting to launch an EC2 instance in a disallowed region from a test account within the targeted OU.","Ensure all existing EC2 instances in approved regions are compliant and document the approved regions."]'::json,
  '{3,0,2,1}'::int[],
  'Before implementing a preventive control, it''s crucial to understand the current state and define the scope. Option D (Ensure all existing EC2 instances in approved regions are compliant and document the approved regions) establishes the baseline. Next, Option A (Create a new SCP) defines the preventive guardrail itself. Option C (Test the SCP''s effect) is a critical step to validate that the SCP functions as intended and does not inadvertently block legitimate actions, which is essential for MINIMAL disruption. Finally, Option B (Attach the SCP to the root or OU) applies the policy broadly, knowing it has been tested.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  18,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_1_147',
  'A compliance team needs to continuously monitor whether all Amazon S3 buckets across their AWS environment have server-side encryption enabled. They decide to use AWS Config to detect non-compliant configurations. Order the steps to deploy an AWS Config rule for this specific compliance requirement.',
  'ordering',
  '["Select the ''s3-bucket-server-side-encryption-enabled'' managed rule from the AWS Config console.","Enable AWS Config recording for S3 buckets as a resource type.","Configure the rule''s parameters, such as the desired encryption type (e.g., SSE-S3, SSE-KMS).","Review the compliance dashboard and findings generated by the AWS Config rule."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}'::int[],
  'Before any rules can evaluate resources, AWS Config must be set up to record changes. Therefore, Option B (Enable AWS Config recording for S3 buckets) is the foundational first step. Once recording is active, Option A (Select the ''s3-bucket-server-side-encryption-enabled'' managed rule) identifies the specific compliance check. Next, Option C (Configure the rule''s parameters) allows customization of the rule, such as specifying the required encryption type. Finally, Option D (Review the compliance dashboard and findings) is where the team monitors the compliance status and identifies non-compliant S3 buckets.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  19,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_1_148',
  'A security operations center (SOC) needs to aggregate security findings from various AWS services like Amazon GuardDuty, Amazon Inspector, and AWS Config into a single, centralized dashboard for efficient monitoring and incident response. Order the steps to set up AWS Security Hub for this purpose, assuming it''s a new deployment.',
  'ordering',
  '["Enable AWS Security Hub in the desired AWS Region.","Enable the default security standards (e.g., AWS Foundational Security Best Practices, CIS AWS Foundations Benchmark).","Integrate other AWS security services (e.g., Amazon GuardDuty, Amazon Inspector) to send their findings to Security Hub.","Review the Security Hub dashboard for aggregated findings and compliance scores."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'The initial step is to activate the service itself, so Option A (Enable AWS Security Hub) is first. Once enabled, Option B (Enable the default security standards) provides a baseline for security posture assessment and generates initial findings. Next, Option C (Integrate other AWS security services) is crucial for centralizing findings from various sources into Security Hub. Finally, Option D (Review the Security Hub dashboard) allows the SOC team to monitor the aggregated security posture and respond to issues efficiently.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  20,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_1_149',
  'A company wants to enhance its threat detection capabilities for its AWS accounts to identify unusual and potentially malicious activity, such as cryptocurrency mining, unauthorized access, or compromised instances. They decide to activate Amazon GuardDuty. Order the steps to enable and initially configure Amazon GuardDuty.',
  'ordering',
  '["Review the generated findings in the GuardDuty console and investigate any high-severity alerts.","Navigate to the Amazon GuardDuty service in the AWS Management Console.","Enable GuardDuty for the current AWS account.","Configure trusted IP lists and threat intelligence sets, if applicable, to refine detection."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}'::int[],
  'The first step to activate Amazon GuardDuty is to locate the service, so Option B (Navigate to the Amazon GuardDuty service) is correct. Next, Option C (Enable GuardDuty for the current AWS account) activates the service, allowing it to begin monitoring. After activation, Option D (Configure trusted IP lists and threat intelligence sets) allows for customization to reduce false positives and enhance detection accuracy. Finally, Option A (Review the generated findings) is where the security team analyzes the alerts and takes appropriate action.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  21,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_1_150',
  'A development team has deployed several Amazon EC2 instances running custom applications and needs to regularly scan them for software vulnerabilities and unintended network exposure. They plan to use Amazon Inspector to automate these security assessments. Order the steps to configure Amazon Inspector for vulnerability scanning of these EC2 instances.',
  'ordering',
  '["Review the generated findings and detailed reports in the Amazon Inspector console.","Enable Amazon Inspector in the desired AWS Region.","Define an assessment target by tagging the EC2 instances to be scanned.","Create and configure an assessment template, specifying rules packages and duration."]'::json,
  '{1,2,3,0}'::int[],
  'The first step is to activate the service, so Option B (Enable Amazon Inspector) is necessary. Once Inspector is enabled, Option C (Define an assessment target) specifies which EC2 instances will be included in the scans, typically using tags. Next, Option D (Create and configure an assessment template) defines the rules and duration for the scan. Finally, Option A (Review the generated findings) is where the team analyzes the vulnerabilities and takes remediation actions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  22,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_1_151',
  'A company operates a centralized security auditing account that needs to periodically access resources in multiple production accounts to perform compliance checks and retrieve logs. The security team wants to establish cross-account access using IAM roles, adhering to the principle of least privilege. Order the steps to configure this cross-account access.',
  'ordering',
  '["In the production account, create an IAM role with a trust policy that allows the security auditing account to assume the role.","In the security auditing account, create an IAM user or role that will perform the cross-account access.","In the production account, attach an IAM policy to the newly created role, granting only the necessary permissions to access specific resources (e.g., S3 buckets, CloudWatch Logs).","From the security auditing account, test assuming the new role and verify access to the intended resources."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}'::int[],
  'The process begins in the account that will initiate the access, so Option B (In the security auditing account, create an IAM user or role) is the first step. Next, Option A (In the production account, create an IAM role with a trust policy) establishes the trust relationship, allowing the auditing account to assume the role. Following this, Option C (In the production account, attach an IAM policy) defines the specific permissions the assumed role will have, adhering to least privilege. Finally, Option D (From the security auditing account, test assuming the new role) verifies that the cross-account access works as expected and has the correct permissions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  23,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_1_152',
  'A company wants to ensure that no IAM user or role, even administrators, can ever elevate their privileges beyond a predefined set of permissions, regardless of the policies attached to them. They decide to implement an IAM permissions boundary. Order the steps to apply a permissions boundary to an IAM role.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new IAM policy that defines the maximum permissions allowed for any entity to which this boundary will be applied.","Attach the newly created permissions boundary policy to the target IAM role.","Verify that the effective permissions of the IAM role are now the intersection of its identity-based policies and the permissions boundary.","Test the IAM role''s permissions to ensure it can perform allowed actions but is blocked from disallowed actions by the boundary."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'To implement an IAM permissions boundary, the first step is to define the boundary itself. Option A (Create a new IAM policy) creates the policy that will act as the boundary. Next, Option B (Attach the newly created permissions boundary policy) associates this boundary with the target IAM role. After attachment, Option C (Verify that the effective permissions) confirms that the boundary is active and correctly restricting permissions. Finally, Option D (Test the IAM role''s permissions) ensures that the boundary functions as intended, preventing privilege escalation while allowing necessary actions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  24,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

