INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_126',
  'A company needs to encrypt an existing unencrypted Amazon EBS volume attached to an Amazon EC2 instance. Order the steps to achieve this without data loss.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new encrypted EBS volume from the encrypted snapshot.","Create a snapshot of the original unencrypted EBS volume.","Attach the new encrypted volume to the EC2 instance and detach the old volume.","Copy the snapshot, enabling encryption during the copy process."]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}'::int[],
  'To encrypt an existing unencrypted EBS volume, the first step is to create a snapshot of the original unencrypted EBS volume to preserve its data (Option B). Next, copy this snapshot, ensuring that you enable encryption during the copy process, which creates an encrypted version of the snapshot (Option D). Then, create a new encrypted EBS volume from this newly encrypted snapshot (Option A). Finally, attach the new encrypted volume to your EC2 instance and detach the old unencrypted volume, making sure to update any necessary configurations or mount points (Option C). This process ensures data at rest is encrypted.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  25,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_127',
  'A SysOps Administrator wants to ensure all new objects uploaded to an Amazon S3 bucket are encrypted by default using SSE-S3. Order the steps to configure default server-side encryption for an S3 bucket.',
  'ordering',
  '["Navigate to the S3 bucket''s ''Properties'' tab.","Select ''Amazon S3 key (SSE-S3)'' as the encryption type.","Locate the ''Default encryption'' section and click ''Edit''.","Click ''Save changes'' to apply the default encryption."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}'::int[],
  'To configure default server-side encryption for an S3 bucket using SSE-S3, the initial step is to navigate to the S3 bucket''s ''Properties'' tab (Option A). Next, locate the ''Default encryption'' section within the properties and click ''Edit'' (Option C). Then, select ''Amazon S3 key (SSE-S3)'' as the desired encryption type (Option B). Finally, click ''Save changes'' to apply this configuration, ensuring that all new objects uploaded to the bucket will be encrypted by default using S3-managed encryption keys (Option D). This enhances data security at rest.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  26,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_128',
  'A security team needs to create a new customer managed key (CMK) in AWS Key Management Service (KMS) for encrypting sensitive application data. Order the steps to create a symmetric CMK.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define key administrators who can manage the CMK.","Choose ''Symmetric'' as the key type.","Define key usage permissions for IAM users and roles.","Review the key policy and click ''Finish'' to create the key."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}'::int[],
  'To create a symmetric CMK in AWS KMS, the first step is to choose ''Symmetric'' as the key type, which is suitable for most encryption tasks (Option B). Next, define the key administrators, which are the IAM users or roles authorized to manage the CMK, such as enabling or disabling it (Option A). After that, define the key usage permissions, specifying which IAM users and roles can use the CMK for cryptographic operations (Option C). Finally, review the generated key policy, which summarizes all the defined permissions, and click ''Finish'' to create the CMK (Option D). This establishes a secure key for data encryption.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  27,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_129',
  'An application running on an Amazon EC2 instance needs to allow inbound HTTP traffic from specific IP addresses. Order the steps to configure a security group to permit this traffic.',
  'ordering',
  '["Specify ''HTTP'' as the protocol and ''80'' as the port range.","Select the security group associated with the EC2 instance.","Add an inbound rule to the security group.","Define the source IP addresses or CIDR block."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}'::int[],
  'To configure a security group to allow inbound HTTP traffic, the first step is to select the specific security group associated with the Amazon EC2 instance (Option B). Next, add a new inbound rule to this security group (Option C). Then, specify ''HTTP'' as the protocol and ''80'' as the port range for the incoming traffic (Option A). Finally, define the source IP addresses or CIDR block from which the HTTP traffic is allowed (Option D). This ensures that only authorized sources can communicate with the EC2 instance on the specified port.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  28,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_130',
  'A security team wants to activate Amazon GuardDuty to continuously monitor for malicious activity and unauthorized behavior in their AWS accounts. Order the steps to enable GuardDuty.',
  'ordering',
  '["Navigate to the Amazon GuardDuty console.","Review the overview of GuardDuty features and pricing.","Click the ''Enable GuardDuty'' button.","Begin monitoring security findings generated by GuardDuty."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'To enable Amazon GuardDuty, the first step is to navigate to the Amazon GuardDuty console (Option A). Next, review the overview of GuardDuty''s features and understand its pricing model (Option B). Then, click the prominent ''Enable GuardDuty'' button to activate the service (Option C). Once enabled, GuardDuty will immediately begin monitoring your AWS environment, and you can then start reviewing and acting on the security findings it generates (Option D). GuardDuty provides intelligent threat detection for your AWS accounts and workloads.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  29,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_131',
  'A SysOps Administrator needs to perform automated security assessments to identify vulnerabilities in Amazon EC2 instances. Order the steps to initiate an Amazon Inspector scan.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define an assessment target, specifying the EC2 instances to be scanned.","Enable Amazon Inspector in your AWS account.","Configure an assessment template, including rules packages and duration.","Run the assessment to initiate the vulnerability scan."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}'::int[],
  'To initiate an Amazon Inspector scan, the first step is to enable Amazon Inspector in your AWS account (Option B). Next, define an assessment target, which specifies the particular Amazon EC2 instances you want to include in the scan (Option A). After defining the target, configure an assessment template, where you select the desired rules packages (e.g., network reachability, host vulnerabilities) and set the duration of the assessment (Option C). Finally, run the assessment to initiate the vulnerability scan against the specified EC2 instances (Option D). Inspector helps identify security vulnerabilities and deviations from best practices.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  30,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_132',
  'A security team wants to centralize security findings from various AWS services for easier management and analysis. Order the steps to enable AWS Security Hub in an AWS account.',
  'ordering',
  '["Navigate to the AWS Security Hub console.","Review the overview of Security Hub''s capabilities and integrated standards.","Click the ''Enable Security Hub'' button.","Begin reviewing aggregated security findings and compliance status."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'To enable AWS Security Hub, the first step is to navigate to the AWS Security Hub console (Option A). Next, review the overview of Security Hub''s capabilities, such as its integration with various AWS security services and supported security standards (Option B). Then, click the ''Enable Security Hub'' button to activate the service in your account (Option C). Once enabled, Security Hub will automatically start aggregating security findings from other AWS services, allowing you to begin reviewing and managing your security posture from a central location (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  31,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_133',
  'A SysOps Administrator needs to prevent accidental public exposure of Amazon S3 buckets at the account level. Order the steps to configure S3 Block Public Access settings for an entire AWS account.',
  'ordering',
  '["Navigate to the S3 console and select ''Block Public Access (account settings)''.","Click ''Edit'' to modify the current settings.","Enable all four Block Public Access settings.","Click ''Save changes'' and confirm the action."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'To configure S3 Block Public Access settings at the account level, the first step is to navigate to the Amazon S3 console and select ''Block Public Access (account settings)'' from the left navigation pane (Option A). Next, click ''Edit'' to modify the current account-level settings (Option B). Then, enable all four Block Public Access settings, which include blocking public access to buckets and objects granted through new ACLs, any ACLs, new public bucket policies, and any public bucket policies (Option C). Finally, click ''Save changes'' and confirm the action to apply these preventive controls across all S3 buckets in the account (Option D). This is a critical security best practice.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  32,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_134',
  'A company requires all new objects uploaded to an Amazon S3 bucket to be encrypted at rest using server-side encryption. Order the steps to configure default server-side encryption for an S3 bucket.',
  'ordering',
  '["Save the changes to the bucket properties","Navigate to the ''Properties'' tab of the S3 bucket","Select the Amazon S3 bucket from the S3 console","Enable default encryption (SSE-S3 or SSE-KMS) and choose the desired key"]'::json,
  '{2,1,3,0}'::int[],
  'Configuring default server-side encryption for an S3 bucket is a straightforward process. Option C (Select the Amazon S3 bucket) is the first step to choose the target bucket. Option B (Navigate to the ''Properties'' tab) is where bucket-level settings are managed. Option D (Enable default encryption) is the core action to specify the encryption type. Option A (Save the changes to the bucket properties) finalizes the configuration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  33,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_135',
  'A SysOps Administrator needs to enable VPC Flow Logs for a specific Amazon VPC to capture network traffic metadata for security analysis and troubleshooting. Order the steps to configure VPC Flow Logs.',
  'ordering',
  '["Select the Amazon VPC for which to enable Flow Logs","Create a new Flow Log configuration","Choose a destination for the Flow Logs (e.g., Amazon S3 bucket or Amazon CloudWatch Logs)","Configure the log record format and retention settings"]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'Enabling VPC Flow Logs requires a sequence of configuration steps. Option A (Select the Amazon VPC) is the initial action to specify the network to monitor. Option B (Create a new Flow Log configuration) initiates the setup process. Option C (Choose a destination for the Flow Logs) is crucial for storing and accessing the log data. Option D (Configure the log record format and retention settings) allows for customization of the log content and lifecycle.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  34,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_136',
  'A company has a strict compliance requirement to encrypt all Amazon EBS volumes at rest using a Customer Managed Key (CMK) from AWS Key Management Service (KMS). Order the steps to ensure new EBS volumes are encrypted with a specific CMK.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an encrypted Amazon EBS volume using the specified CMK","Attach the encrypted volume to an Amazon EC2 instance","Enable EBS encryption by default for the region, specifying the CMK","Create a new Customer Managed Key (CMK) in AWS KMS"]'::json,
  '{3,2,0,1}'::int[],
  'To ensure EBS volumes are encrypted with a specific CMK, a logical order of operations is required. Option D (Create a new Customer Managed Key (CMK) in AWS KMS) is the first step to have a key available. Option C (Enable EBS encryption by default for the region, specifying the CMK) ensures that all newly created volumes in that region are encrypted with the chosen key. Option A (Create an encrypted Amazon EBS volume) is the action of provisioning the storage. Option B (Attach the encrypted volume to an Amazon EC2 instance) makes the storage available for use by a compute resource.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  35,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_137',
  'A SysOps Administrator needs to enforce encryption in transit for a web application hosted on Amazon EC2 instances behind an Application Load Balancer (ALB) using TLS/SSL. Order the steps to achieve this.',
  'ordering',
  '["Obtain an SSL/TLS certificate (e.g., from AWS Certificate Manager or a third-party CA)","Update DNS records to point to the Application Load Balancer''s DNS name","Configure the Application Load Balancer listener to use HTTPS and the certificate","Import or provision the certificate into AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)"]'::json,
  '{0,3,2,1}'::int[],
  'Enforcing TLS/SSL for a web application involves certificate management and load balancer configuration. Option A (Obtain an SSL/TLS certificate) is the initial requirement for secure communication. Option D (Import or provision the certificate into AWS Certificate Manager (ACM)) makes the certificate available for AWS services. Option C (Configure the Application Load Balancer listener) sets up the ALB to use HTTPS and the certificate. Option B (Update DNS records) directs traffic to the secure endpoint.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  36,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_138',
  'A security team wants to ensure that an Amazon S3 bucket containing sensitive data is never publicly accessible. Order the steps to configure S3 Block Public Access settings for the bucket.',
  'ordering',
  '["Select the Amazon S3 bucket from the S3 console","Navigate to the ''Permissions'' tab of the S3 bucket","Save the changes to the bucket settings","Enable the desired Block Public Access settings (e.g., Block all public access)"]'::json,
  '{0,1,3,2}'::int[],
  'Configuring S3 Block Public Access is a critical security measure. Option A (Select the Amazon S3 bucket) is the first step to target the specific bucket. Option B (Navigate to the ''Permissions'' tab) is where access control settings are managed. Option D (Enable the desired Block Public Access settings) is the core action to prevent public access. Option C (Save the changes to the bucket settings) applies the configuration.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  37,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_139',
  'A SysOps Administrator is tasked with setting up a new Amazon VPC and securing its network traffic using both Network Access Control Lists (NACLs) and Security Groups. Order the steps to implement this network security architecture.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure Network Access Control Lists (NACLs) for subnets","Create subnets within the Amazon VPC","Configure Security Groups for Amazon EC2 instances","Create the Amazon VPC with desired CIDR blocks"]'::json,
  '{3,1,0,2}'::int[],
  'Setting up VPC security requires a hierarchical approach. Option D (Create the Amazon VPC) is the foundational step for the entire network. Option B (Create subnets within the Amazon VPC) partitions the VPC into smaller networks. Option A (Configure Network Access Control Lists (NACLs)) provides stateless traffic filtering at the subnet level. Option C (Configure Security Groups) provides stateful traffic filtering at the instance level, which is typically done after NACLs for a layered defense.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  38,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_140',
  'A SysOps Administrator is investigating a network connectivity issue between an Amazon EC2 instance and an external service. They suspect a firewall rule is blocking traffic. Order the steps to use VPC Flow Logs to troubleshoot this issue.',
  'ordering',
  '["Replicate the network connectivity issue to generate relevant log entries","Analyze traffic patterns in the Flow Logs to identify blocked connections","Enable VPC Flow Logs for the relevant VPC or subnet, directing logs to CloudWatch Logs","Query and filter Flow Logs in Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights to pinpoint specific traffic"]'::json,
  '{2,0,3,1}'::int[],
  'Troubleshooting network issues with VPC Flow Logs involves enabling, generating, querying, and analyzing. Option C (Enable VPC Flow Logs) is the essential first step to capture network traffic metadata. Option A (Replicate the network connectivity issue) ensures that the problematic traffic is recorded in the logs. Option D (Query and filter Flow Logs in Amazon CloudWatch Logs Insights) allows for efficient searching of specific traffic patterns or blocked connections. Option B (Analyze traffic patterns in the Flow Logs) is the final step to interpret the data and identify the root cause of the connectivity issue.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  39,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_141',
  'A development team needs to encrypt sensitive data at rest for a new application. They plan to use AWS Key Management Service (AWS KMS) to create and manage a Customer Managed Key (CMK) for this purpose. Order the steps to create a new symmetric Customer Managed Key in AWS KMS.',
  'ordering',
  '["Define the key policy, specifying which IAM users and roles can administer and use the CMK.","Navigate to the AWS KMS service in the AWS Management Console.","Configure key rotation settings for the CMK.","Choose the key type (symmetric) and usage (encryption and decryption) for the new CMK."]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}'::int[],
  'The process of creating a CMK begins by accessing the service, so Option B (Navigate to the AWS KMS service) is the first step. Next, Option D (Choose the key type and usage) specifies the fundamental characteristics of the key. Following this, Option A (Define the key policy) is critical for controlling access to the key, determining who can manage and use it. Finally, Option C (Configure key rotation settings) ensures that the key material is periodically rotated for enhanced security.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_142',
  'A company stores critical customer data in an Amazon S3 bucket and has a strict compliance requirement to ensure all new objects uploaded to this bucket are encrypted at rest using a Customer Managed Key (CMK) from AWS KMS. Order the steps to configure the S3 bucket for default encryption with AWS KMS.',
  'ordering',
  '["Configure the S3 bucket''s default encryption settings to use ''SSE-KMS'' and specify the ARN of the created KMS CMK.","Create a new symmetric Customer Managed Key (CMK) in AWS KMS and define its key policy to allow S3 to use it.","Ensure the IAM role or user uploading objects has permissions to use the specified KMS CMK for encryption.","Upload a test object to the S3 bucket and verify that it is encrypted with the specified KMS CMK."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}'::int[],
  'To use KMS for S3 encryption, the KMS key must exist first. Option B (Create a new symmetric Customer Managed Key) is the initial step, ensuring the encryption key is available. Next, Option A (Configure the S3 bucket''s default encryption settings) tells S3 to use this specific KMS key for new objects. Following this, Option C (Ensure the IAM role or user uploading objects has permissions) is crucial for successful uploads, as the uploader needs permission to use the KMS key. Finally, Option D (Upload a test object) verifies the entire setup is working correctly.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_143',
  'A system administrator needs to encrypt an existing, unencrypted Amazon EBS volume that is currently attached to an Amazon EC2 instance. The application running on the instance requires minimal downtime during this process. Order the steps to encrypt the EBS volume with the LEAST impact on the running application.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a snapshot of the unencrypted EBS volume.","Create a new encrypted EBS volume from the encrypted snapshot.","Stop the EC2 instance, detach the unencrypted volume, attach the new encrypted volume, and restart the instance.","Copy the snapshot, enabling encryption during the copy process."]'::json,
  '{0,3,1,2}'::int[],
  'To encrypt an existing EBS volume with minimal downtime, the process involves snapshots. Option A (Create a snapshot) captures the data of the unencrypted volume. Next, Option D (Copy the snapshot, enabling encryption) creates an encrypted version of the data. Following this, Option B (Create a new encrypted EBS volume) provisions the new encrypted volume from the copied snapshot. Finally, Option C (Stop the EC2 instance, detach, attach, and restart) is the step that causes downtime, but it''s minimized as the encryption process happens offline.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_144',
  'A security engineer needs to enable comprehensive network traffic logging for a specific Amazon VPC to aid in security investigations, network troubleshooting, and compliance auditing. They decide to configure VPC Flow Logs to capture metadata about IP traffic. Order the steps to enable VPC Flow Logs for a VPC, sending logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs.',
  'ordering',
  '["Select the target VPC for which to enable Flow Logs.","Create a new IAM role with permissions to publish logs to Amazon CloudWatch Logs.","Configure the Flow Log to capture all traffic (accepted and rejected) and specify the IAM role and CloudWatch Log Group as the destination.","Verify that flow log records are being delivered to the specified Amazon CloudWatch Log Group."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}'::int[],
  'Before configuring VPC Flow Logs to send to CloudWatch Logs, the necessary permissions must exist. Option B (Create a new IAM role) ensures that VPC Flow Logs has the authorization to publish logs. Next, Option A (Select the target VPC) identifies the network for which logging will be enabled. Following this, Option C (Configure the Flow Log) sets up the logging parameters, including the destination and the type of traffic to capture. Finally, Option D (Verify that flow log records are being delivered) confirms that the setup is working correctly and logs are flowing.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_145',
  'A system administrator is deploying a new web application on an Amazon EC2 instance and needs to ensure that only HTTP (port 80) and HTTPS (port 443) traffic from the internet, and SSH (port 22) traffic from a specific IP range, can reach the instance. Order the steps to configure the Amazon EC2 security group for this instance.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new security group for the EC2 instance.","Add an inbound rule to allow HTTP (port 80) traffic from 0.0.0.0/0.","Associate the security group with the EC2 instance.","Add an inbound rule to allow SSH (port 22) traffic from the specified IP range."]'::json,
  '{0,1,3,2}'::int[],
  'The first step is to create the container for the rules, so Option A (Create a new security group) is correct. Next, Option B (Add an inbound rule for HTTP) allows web traffic. Following this, Option D (Add an inbound rule for SSH) configures administrative access. Finally, Option C (Associate the security group) applies these rules to the EC2 instance, making them effective.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_146',
  'A security engineer needs to implement stateless filtering for a subnet within a VPC to explicitly deny specific inbound and outbound IP addresses and ports, while allowing all other traffic. They decide to use a Network Access Control List (NACL). Order the steps to configure a NACL for this purpose.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a new Network Access Control List (NACL) in the VPC.","Associate the NACL with the target subnet.","Add inbound and outbound rules to explicitly deny the specific IP addresses and ports (lower rule numbers for deny).","Add inbound and outbound rules to allow all other necessary traffic (higher rule numbers for allow)."]'::json,
  '{0,2,3,1}'::int[],
  'The first step is to create the NACL itself, so Option A (Create a new Network Access Control List) is correct. Next, Option C (Add inbound and outbound rules to explicitly deny) establishes the specific blocking rules, which should have lower rule numbers to take precedence. Following this, Option D (Add inbound and outbound rules to allow all other necessary traffic) ensures legitimate traffic is permitted, using higher rule numbers. Finally, Option B (Associate the NACL with the target subnet) applies these rules to the network traffic flowing in and out of the subnet.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_147',
  'A company has a compliance mandate to ensure that no Amazon S3 buckets in their AWS account are publicly accessible, even if an administrator mistakenly configures a bucket policy or ACL to allow public access. They need to enforce this at the account level. Order the steps to implement S3 Block Public Access settings for the entire AWS account.',
  'ordering',
  '["Navigate to the S3 console and select ''Block Public Access (account settings)''.","Enable all four ''Block Public Access'' settings at the account level (BlockPublicAcls, IgnorePublicAcls, BlockPublicPolicy, RestrictPublicBuckets).","Review existing S3 buckets for any public access configurations that will be affected by the new settings.","Test access to a previously public S3 bucket or attempt to make a bucket public to verify the settings are enforced."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}'::int[],
  'Before implementing account-level public access blocks, it''s critical to understand the potential impact. Option C (Review existing S3 buckets) identifies any current public access configurations that will be affected. Next, Option A (Navigate to the S3 console) is the entry point for configuring these settings. Following this, Option B (Enable all four ''Block Public Access'' settings) applies the comprehensive account-level protection. Finally, Option D (Test access to a previously public S3 bucket) verifies that the settings are correctly enforced and prevent public access.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_148',
  'A database administrator needs to enable encryption at rest for an existing Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL DB instance that was initially deployed without encryption. The application connected to this database requires minimal disruption. Order the steps to encrypt the RDS DB instance with the LEAST impact on the running application.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a manual snapshot of the unencrypted RDS DB instance.","Restore a new DB instance from the encrypted snapshot, specifying an AWS KMS key for encryption.","Modify the application''s connection string to point to the new, encrypted RDS DB instance.","Copy the manual snapshot, enabling encryption during the copy process."]'::json,
  '{0,3,1,2}'::int[],
  'To encrypt an existing RDS instance, a snapshot-based approach is required. Option A (Create a manual snapshot) captures the current state of the unencrypted database. Next, Option D (Copy the manual snapshot, enabling encryption) creates an encrypted version of the snapshot. Following this, Option B (Restore a new DB instance from the encrypted snapshot) provisions the new encrypted database. Finally, Option C (Modify the application''s connection string) updates the application to use the new, secure database, completing the migration with minimal downtime during the actual switch.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_25_m_25_2_149',
  'A web application team needs to enforce TLS 1.2 as the minimum security policy for all client connections to their Application Load Balancer (ALB) to meet strict compliance requirements. They have already obtained an SSL/TLS certificate. Order the steps to configure the ALB to enforce this TLS security policy.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an HTTPS listener on the Application Load Balancer.","Upload the SSL/TLS certificate to AWS Certificate Manager (ACM).","Configure the HTTPS listener to use the uploaded certificate and select a security policy that enforces TLS 1.2 (e.g., ''ELBSecurityPolicy-TLS13-1-2-2021-06'').","Test client connections to the ALB to ensure only TLS 1.2 and higher connections are accepted."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}'::int[],
  'Before configuring the ALB listener, the certificate must be available in AWS. Option B (Upload the SSL/TLS certificate to AWS Certificate Manager) makes the certificate usable by the ALB. Next, Option A (Create an HTTPS listener) sets up the secure entry point on the ALB. Following this, Option C (Configure the HTTPS listener to use the uploaded certificate and select a security policy) applies the certificate and, crucially, enforces the desired TLS 1.2 security policy. Finally, Option D (Test client connections) verifies that the policy is correctly enforced, rejecting older TLS versions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain25_5',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_25_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

