) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_102',
  'A company needs to establish a secure, dedicated network connection between its on-premises data center and its AWS VPC. Order the steps to set up an AWS Direct Connect connection.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a Direct Connect connection in the AWS Direct Connect console, specifying a Direct Connect location.","Work with the Direct Connect partner or network provider to establish the physical cross-connect.","Create a Virtual Interface (VIF) for the Direct Connect connection, specifying the VPC to connect to.","Configure routing on your on-premises router to advertise local networks to AWS and learn VPC routes."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'The process begins by creating a Direct Connect connection in the AWS Direct Connect console (Option A), selecting a Direct Connect location. Next, work with the Direct Connect partner or network provider to establish the physical cross-connect (Option B) at the chosen location. Once the physical connection is active, create a Virtual Interface (VIF) (Option C), specifying whether it''s private, public, or transit, and associating it with the target VPC. Finally, configure routing on your on-premises router (Option D) to exchange routes with AWS over the VIF, completing the connectivity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  40,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_103',
  'A SysOps Administrator is configuring a Site-to-Site VPN connection between an on-premises network and an AWS VPC. Order the steps to establish this secure connection.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a Customer Gateway in AWS, providing the public IP address of the on-premises VPN device.","Create a Virtual Private Gateway (VPG) in AWS and attach it to the VPC.","Create a Site-to-Site VPN connection, linking the VPG and the Customer Gateway.","Configure the on-premises VPN device using the configuration file downloaded from AWS."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}'::int[],
  'First, create a Virtual Private Gateway (VPG) in AWS and attach it to the VPC (Option B); this is the AWS side of the VPN connection. Next, create a Customer Gateway (Option A), which represents the on-premises VPN device, providing its public IP address. Then, create the Site-to-Site VPN connection (Option C), linking the VPG and the Customer Gateway. Finally, download the configuration file from AWS and use it to configure the on-premises VPN device (Option D) to establish the VPN tunnels.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  41,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_104',
  'A SysOps Administrator needs to implement a multi-NIC configuration for an EC2 instance to separate management traffic from application traffic. Order the steps to set up this configuration.',
  'ordering',
  '["Launch an EC2 instance with its primary Elastic Network Interface (ENI) in the application subnet.","Create a new Elastic Network Interface (ENI) in the management subnet.","Attach the newly created ENI to the running EC2 instance.","Configure the operating system on the EC2 instance to recognize and utilize both network interfaces for their respective traffic types."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'First, launch an EC2 instance with its primary Elastic Network Interface (ENI) in the application subnet (Option A), which handles the main application traffic. Next, create a new Elastic Network Interface (ENI) specifically for management traffic in the management subnet (Option B). Then, attach this newly created ENI to the running EC2 instance (Option C). Finally, configure the operating system on the EC2 instance (Option D) to recognize and utilize both network interfaces, assigning appropriate IP addresses and routing rules for management and application traffic separation.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  42,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_105',
  'A large enterprise is migrating an on-premises application to AWS and requires a secure, private network connection between their existing data center and a new Amazon VPC. The solution must provide consistent bandwidth and low latency for critical data transfers. Arrange the following steps in the correct order to establish an AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a Customer Gateway in AWS, specifying the on-premises VPN device''s public IP address.","Configure the routing for the Virtual Private Gateway to propagate routes to the VPC route tables.","Create a Virtual Private Gateway and attach it to the target Amazon VPC.","Create a Site-to-Site VPN connection, linking the Virtual Private Gateway and the Customer Gateway."]'::json,
  '{2,0,3,1}'::int[],
  'To establish an AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection, the correct sequence of operations is crucial. Option C (Create a Virtual Private Gateway and attach it to the target Amazon VPC) is the initial step, as the Virtual Private Gateway acts as the VPN concentrator on the AWS side and must be associated with the VPC. Option A (Create a Customer Gateway in AWS, specifying the on-premises VPN device''s public IP address) follows, representing the on-premises VPN device to AWS. Option D (Create a Site-to-Site VPN connection, linking the Virtual Private Gateway and the Customer Gateway) is then performed to establish the logical connection between the two gateways. Finally, Option B (Configure the routing for the Virtual Private Gateway to propagate routes to the VPC route tables) ensures that traffic can correctly flow between the VPC and the on-premises network through the newly established VPN connection.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  43,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_106',
  'An organization needs to allow EC2 instances in private subnets within their Amazon VPC to initiate outbound connections to the internet for software updates and external API calls, without exposing them directly to inbound internet traffic. The solution must be highly available and managed by AWS. Order the steps to correctly implement a NAT Gateway for this requirement.',
  'ordering',
  '["Update the route tables associated with the private subnets to route internet-bound traffic through the NAT Gateway.","Create a NAT Gateway in a public subnet and associate an Elastic IP address with it.","Ensure the public subnet''s route table has a default route to an Internet Gateway.","Verify that security groups and Network ACLs allow outbound traffic from private instances to the NAT Gateway and from the NAT Gateway to the internet."]'::json,
  '{1,2,0,3}'::int[],
  'Implementing a NAT Gateway requires a specific order of operations to ensure proper functionality. Option B (Create a NAT Gateway in a public subnet and associate an Elastic IP address with it) is the first step, as the NAT Gateway itself must be provisioned in a public subnet with a public IP. Option C (Ensure the public subnet''s route table has a default route to an Internet Gateway) is necessary next, as the NAT Gateway relies on the public subnet''s ability to reach the internet. Option A (Update the route tables associated with the private subnets to route internet-bound traffic through the NAT Gateway) then directs traffic from private instances to the NAT Gateway. Finally, Option D (Verify that security groups and Network ACLs allow outbound traffic from private instances to the NAT Gateway and from the NAT Gateway to the internet) is a critical validation step to ensure network access controls do not block the intended traffic flow.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  44,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_107',
  'A development team has two separate Amazon VPCs in the same AWS Region, one for development and one for testing. They need to establish direct, private network connectivity between specific subnets in these two VPCs for data exchange, without routing traffic through the public internet. Order the steps to correctly configure VPC peering between these two VPCs.',
  'ordering',
  '["Accept the peering connection request from the requester VPC in the accepter VPC.","Update the route tables in both VPCs'' subnets to include routes to the peered VPC''s CIDR block via the peering connection.","Create a VPC peering connection request from the development VPC to the testing VPC.","Ensure that the security groups and Network ACLs in both VPCs allow traffic between the peered subnets."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}'::int[],
  'To establish VPC peering, a precise sequence of actions is required. Option C (Create a VPC peering connection request from the development VPC to the testing VPC) is the initial action, originating the request from one VPC. Option A (Accept the peering connection request from the requester VPC in the accepter VPC) must then be performed in the target VPC to establish the peering link. Following this, Option B (Update the route tables in both VPCs'' subnets to include routes to the peered VPC''s CIDR block via the peering connection) is essential to direct traffic between the VPCs through the peering connection. Finally, Option D (Ensure that the security groups and Network ACLs in both VPCs allow traffic between the peered subnets) is a critical step to ensure that network access controls do not inadvertently block communication over the established peering connection.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  45,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_108',
  'A company hosts a data processing application in a private subnet within an Amazon VPC. This application needs to securely and privately access Amazon S3 buckets to retrieve and store large datasets, ensuring all traffic remains within the AWS network and does not traverse the internet. Order the steps to configure a Gateway VPC Endpoint for Amazon S3.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a Gateway VPC Endpoint for Amazon S3 within the VPC.","Attach an endpoint policy to the Gateway VPC Endpoint to control S3 access.","Update the route tables associated with the private subnets to include a route for S3 traffic via the Gateway VPC Endpoint.","Verify that security groups and Network ACLs on the EC2 instances allow outbound HTTPS traffic to Amazon S3."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'Configuring a Gateway VPC Endpoint for Amazon S3 involves a specific order of operations. Option A (Create a Gateway VPC Endpoint for Amazon S3 within the VPC) is the first step, as the endpoint resource must be provisioned. Option B (Attach an endpoint policy to the Gateway VPC Endpoint to control S3 access) is next, defining which S3 actions and resources are allowed through this endpoint. Option C (Update the route tables associated with the private subnets to include a route for S3 traffic via the Gateway VPC Endpoint) is crucial to direct traffic from instances in private subnets to the endpoint. Finally, Option D (Verify that security groups and Network ACLs on the EC2 instances allow outbound HTTPS traffic to Amazon S3) is a necessary check to ensure that network access controls do not block communication to S3 via the endpoint.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  46,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_109',
  'A solutions architect is designing a highly available web application in a single AWS Region. The application requires public-facing web servers and private backend database servers, ensuring fault tolerance across multiple Availability Zones. Order the steps to correctly set up the foundational networking for this application within an Amazon VPC.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create public subnets and private subnets across multiple Availability Zones within the VPC.","Configure route tables for public subnets to route internet-bound traffic through an Internet Gateway.","Create an Amazon VPC with a suitable CIDR block.","Configure route tables for private subnets to route internet-bound traffic through a NAT Gateway in a public subnet."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}'::int[],
  'Setting up foundational networking for a highly available application in an Amazon VPC follows a logical progression. Option C (Create an Amazon VPC with a suitable CIDR block) is the essential first step, as all other network components reside within the VPC. Option A (Create public subnets and private subnets across multiple Availability Zones within the VPC) is next, segmenting the network for different tiers of the application and ensuring high availability. Option B (Configure route tables for public subnets to route internet-bound traffic through an Internet Gateway) is then necessary to allow public-facing resources to communicate with the internet. Finally, Option D (Configure route tables for private subnets to route internet-bound traffic through a NAT Gateway in a public subnet) enables private resources to initiate outbound internet connections securely.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  47,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_110',
  'A large organization operates numerous Amazon VPCs across several departments and requires a scalable, centralized solution for inter-VPC connectivity. They need to avoid complex, non-transitive peering connections and manage routing centrally. Order the steps to implement an AWS Transit Gateway for this hub-and-spoke network architecture.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create Transit Gateway attachments for each VPC that needs to connect to the Transit Gateway.","Create an AWS Transit Gateway in the desired AWS Region.","Configure Transit Gateway route tables to define how traffic is routed between the attached VPCs.","Update the route tables in each attached VPC to direct traffic destined for other VPCs through the Transit Gateway attachment."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}'::int[],
  'Implementing an AWS Transit Gateway for a hub-and-spoke architecture requires a specific order. Option B (Create an AWS Transit Gateway in the desired AWS Region) is the foundational step, as the Transit Gateway itself must be provisioned first. Option A (Create Transit Gateway attachments for each VPC that needs to connect to the Transit Gateway) follows, linking individual VPCs to the central Transit Gateway. Option C (Configure Transit Gateway route tables to define how traffic is routed between the attached VPCs) is then performed to establish the routing logic within the Transit Gateway. Finally, Option D (Update the route tables in each attached VPC to direct traffic destined for other VPCs through the Transit Gateway attachment) ensures that traffic from the VPCs correctly utilizes the Transit Gateway for inter-VPC communication.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  48,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_111',
  'A system administrator needs to ensure that a critical EC2 instance, hosting a public-facing application, retains the same public IP address even if the instance is stopped and restarted or replaced. This is essential for maintaining consistent external access and DNS records. Order the steps to correctly associate an Elastic IP address with an EC2 instance.',
  'ordering',
  '["Allocate a new Elastic IP address in the Amazon VPC console.","Associate the allocated Elastic IP address with the running EC2 instance.","Verify that the EC2 instance''s network interface now uses the Elastic IP address.","Ensure the EC2 instance is running and in the correct VPC."]'::json,
  '{0,3,1,2}'::int[],
  'Associating an Elastic IP address with an EC2 instance follows a specific order. Option A (Allocate a new Elastic IP address in the Amazon VPC console) is the first step, as the EIP resource must be provisioned. Option D (Ensure the EC2 instance is running and in the correct VPC) is a prerequisite to ensure the target for the EIP is ready and accessible. Option B (Associate the allocated Elastic IP address with the running EC2 instance) then links the EIP to the instance. Finally, Option C (Verify that the EC2 instance''s network interface now uses the Elastic IP address) is a crucial validation step to confirm the association was successful and the instance is reachable via the static public IP.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  49,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_112',
  'A global media company requires a dedicated, high-throughput, and low-latency network connection between their on-premises broadcast center and AWS for real-time content ingestion and distribution. They need a solution that bypasses the public internet. Order the steps to establish an AWS Direct Connect connection.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a Virtual Private Gateway (VGW) and attach it to the target Amazon VPC.","Create a Direct Connect connection in the AWS console, selecting a Direct Connect location and port speed.","Configure a Virtual Interface (VIF) on the Direct Connect connection, linking it to the VGW for private connectivity.","Work with an AWS Direct Connect Partner or network provider to establish the physical cross-connect at the Direct Connect location."]'::json,
  '{1,3,0,2}'::int[],
  'Establishing an AWS Direct Connect connection is a multi-step process involving both AWS console actions and physical network setup. Option B (Create a Direct Connect connection in the AWS console, selecting a Direct Connect location and port speed) is the initial AWS-side action to request the connection. Option D (Work with an AWS Direct Connect Partner or network provider to establish the physical cross-connect at the Direct Connect location) is the crucial physical step that must occur before logical configuration can be completed. Option A (Create a Virtual Private Gateway (VGW) and attach it to the target Amazon VPC) is then necessary to provide the AWS endpoint for the private connection within the VPC. Finally, Option C (Configure a Virtual Interface (VIF) on the Direct Connect connection, linking it to the VGW for private connectivity) establishes the logical connection over the physical link to the VPC.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  50,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_1_113',
  'A company is deploying a new multi-tier application in an Amazon VPC, consisting of web servers in public subnets and database servers in private subnets. They need to ensure that the web servers can communicate with the database servers, and that both tiers are protected by appropriate network access controls. Order the steps to correctly configure network access controls for this application.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create Security Groups for the web servers, allowing inbound traffic from the internet on necessary ports (e.g., 80, 443) and outbound to the database Security Group.","Create Security Groups for the database servers, allowing inbound traffic only from the web server Security Group on the database port (e.g., 3306) and outbound to necessary services.","Configure Network ACLs for the public subnets, allowing inbound and outbound traffic on web ports and ephemeral ports.","Configure Network ACLs for the private subnets, allowing inbound and outbound traffic on database ports and ephemeral ports from the web subnets."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'Configuring network access controls for a multi-tier application involves a layered approach. Option A (Create Security Groups for the web servers, allowing inbound traffic from the internet on necessary ports (e.g., 80, 443) and outbound to the database Security Group) is the first step for instance-level protection of the public tier. Option B (Create Security Groups for the database servers, allowing inbound traffic only from the web server Security Group on the database port (e.g., 3306) and outbound to necessary services) follows, securing the private tier and restricting access to only the web tier. Option C (Configure Network ACLs for the public subnets, allowing inbound and outbound traffic on web ports and ephemeral ports) then adds a stateless subnet-level firewall for the public tier. Finally, Option D (Configure Network ACLs for the private subnets, allowing inbound and outbound traffic on database ports and ephemeral ports from the web subnets) completes the subnet-level protection for the private tier, remembering the stateless nature of NACLs for return traffic.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  51,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_1',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_26_3
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_82',
  'Order the steps to enable Amazon VPC Flow Logs for a VPC.',
  'ordering',
  '["Navigate to the Amazon VPC console.","Select the VPC for which you want to enable Flow Logs.","Choose ''Flow Logs'' tab and click ''Create flow log''.","Specify the destination (e.g., Amazon S3 bucket or Amazon CloudWatch Logs) and traffic type to log."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'To enable Amazon VPC Flow Logs, first navigate to the Amazon VPC console (Option A). Then, select the specific VPC for which you intend to enable Flow Logs (Option B). Within the VPC details, go to the ''Flow Logs'' tab and click ''Create flow log'' (Option C). Finally, specify the destination for the logs (either an Amazon S3 bucket or Amazon CloudWatch Logs) and choose the type of traffic (e.g., All, Accept, Reject) to be logged (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  52,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_83',
  'Order the basic troubleshooting steps for an Amazon EC2 instance unable to connect to the internet.',
  'ordering',
  '["Check the instance''s Security Group outbound rules.","Verify the subnet''s route table has a default route to an Internet Gateway or NAT Gateway.","Inspect the Network ACL (NACL) associated with the subnet for outbound rules.","Confirm the EC2 instance has a public IP address or is in a private subnet with a NAT Gateway route."]'::json,
  '{3,1,0,2}'::int[],
  'When an EC2 instance cannot connect to the internet, start by confirming if it has a public IP or if it''s in a private subnet correctly routed through a NAT Gateway (Option D). Next, verify the subnet''s route table has a default route (0.0.0.0/0) pointing to an Internet Gateway (for public subnets) or a NAT Gateway (for private subnets) (Option B). Then, check the instance''s Security Group outbound rules (Option A) to ensure traffic is allowed. Finally, inspect the Network ACL (NACL) associated with the subnet for outbound rules (Option C), remembering NACLs are stateless and require explicit allow rules for return traffic.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  53,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_84',
  'Order the steps to analyze network path connectivity using AWS Reachability Analyzer.',
  'ordering',
  '["Navigate to the Amazon VPC console and select ''Reachability Analyzer''.","Specify the source and destination components (e.g., EC2 instance to EC2 instance).","Run the analysis and review the results.","Interpret the findings to identify any blocking network configurations."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'To analyze network path connectivity using AWS Reachability Analyzer, first navigate to the Amazon VPC console and select ''Reachability Analyzer'' (Option A). Next, specify the source and destination network components for the path you want to analyze, such as an EC2 instance to another EC2 instance or a VPC endpoint (Option B). Then, run the analysis and review the detailed results provided by the tool (Option C). The final step is to interpret these findings (Option D) to pinpoint any security group, NACL, or route table configurations that might be blocking the intended network traffic.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  54,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_85',
  'Order the sequence of network access control evaluation for inbound traffic to an EC2 instance.',
  'ordering',
  '["Network ACL (NACL) inbound rules are evaluated.","Security Group inbound rules are evaluated.","Traffic reaches the EC2 instance if allowed by both.","Traffic arrives at the subnet containing the EC2 instance."]'::json,
  '{3,0,1,2}'::int[],
  'For inbound traffic to an EC2 instance, the sequence of network access control evaluation begins when traffic arrives at the subnet containing the EC2 instance (Option D). First, the Network ACL (NACL) inbound rules associated with that subnet are evaluated (Option A). If the NACL allows the traffic, then the Security Group inbound rules associated with the specific EC2 instance are evaluated (Option B). Only if both the NACL and the Security Group explicitly allow the traffic will it successfully reach the EC2 instance (Option C).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  55,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_86',
  'Order the steps to check for common issues preventing an Amazon CloudFront distribution from serving content.',
  'ordering',
  '["Verify the origin server (e.g., S3 bucket, EC2 instance) is accessible and serving content.","Check the CloudFront distribution''s status and ensure it is ''Deployed''.","Examine CloudFront access logs for error codes (e.g., 4xx, 5xx).","Review the distribution''s cache behaviors and origin settings for misconfigurations."]'::json,
  '{1,0,3,2}'::int[],
  'When troubleshooting CloudFront content delivery, first check the distribution''s status to ensure it is ''Deployed'' (Option B) and not in a ''InProgress'' state. Next, verify that the origin server (e.g., Amazon S3 bucket, EC2 instance) is healthy, accessible, and correctly serving content directly (Option A). Then, review the distribution''s cache behaviors and origin settings (Option D) for any misconfigurations, such as incorrect paths or protocol policies. Finally, examine CloudFront access logs (Option C) for specific HTTP error codes (like 4xx or 5xx) which can pinpoint the exact nature of the problem.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  56,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_87',
  'Order the steps to enable access logs for an Elastic Load Balancing (ELB) Application Load Balancer.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Amazon S3 bucket to store the access logs.","Navigate to the EC2 console and select ''Load Balancers''.","Select the target Application Load Balancer and go to the ''Attributes'' tab.","Enable ''Access logs'' and specify the S3 bucket and an optional prefix."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'To enable access logs for an Application Load Balancer, first create an Amazon S3 bucket (Option A) that will serve as the destination for the logs. Next, navigate to the EC2 console and select ''Load Balancers'' (Option B). Then, select the specific Application Load Balancer for which you want to enable logging and go to its ''Attributes'' tab (Option C). Finally, enable the ''Access logs'' feature, specify the S3 bucket you created, and optionally provide a prefix for the log files (Option D).',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  57,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_88',
  'Order the steps to identify if an Amazon EC2 instance is experiencing issues due to its Network ACL configuration.',
  'ordering',
  '["Identify the subnet where the EC2 instance resides.","Check the Network ACL (NACL) associated with that subnet.","Review both inbound and outbound rules in the NACL for explicit DENY rules or missing ALLOW rules for expected traffic.","Compare the NACL rules with the Security Group rules to understand the combined effect."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'To identify if an EC2 instance has NACL-related issues, first identify the subnet where the EC2 instance is located (Option A). Next, check the Network ACL (NACL) that is associated with that specific subnet (Option B). Then, carefully review both the inbound and outbound rules within the NACL (Option C) for any explicit DENY rules that might be blocking traffic, or missing ALLOW rules, remembering that NACLs are stateless. Finally, compare the NACL rules with the Security Group rules (Option D) to understand their combined effect, as both must permit traffic for it to flow.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  58,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_89',
  'Order the steps to verify if an Amazon Route 53 DNS record is resolving correctly.',
  'ordering',
  '["Use a DNS lookup tool (e.g., `dig` or `nslookup`) to query the domain name.","Identify the expected IP address or CNAME target for the record.","Compare the returned DNS resolution with the expected target.","Check the record''s configuration in the Amazon Route 53 console if there''s a discrepancy."]'::json,
  '{1,0,2,3}'::int[],
  'To verify if an Amazon Route 53 DNS record is resolving correctly, first identify the expected IP address or CNAME target that the record should point to (Option B). Next, use a DNS lookup tool like `dig` or `nslookup` (Option A) from an external network to query the domain name. Then, compare the IP address or CNAME returned by the DNS query with your expected target (Option C). If there''s a discrepancy, the final step is to check the record''s configuration directly in the Amazon Route 53 console (Option D) for any misconfigurations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  59,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'easy'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_90',
  'An administrator needs to enable and analyze network traffic flow within a VPC to troubleshoot connectivity issues. Order the steps to configure and review VPC Flow Logs.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create a Flow Log for the VPC, subnet, or Elastic Network Interface (ENI), specifying a destination like Amazon CloudWatch Logs or Amazon S3.","Analyze the collected flow log data in CloudWatch Logs Insights or by querying S3 objects.","Define the log record format and choose the traffic type (e.g., All, Accept, Reject) to capture.","Ensure appropriate IAM permissions are granted for Flow Logs to publish to the chosen destination."]'::json,
  '{0,2,3,1}'::int[],
  'To configure VPC Flow Logs, first, create a Flow Log for the desired resource (VPC, subnet, or ENI), specifying a destination such as Amazon CloudWatch Logs or Amazon S3 (Option A). Next, define the log record format and choose the traffic type (Option C) to capture (e.g., All, Accept, or Reject). Ensure that appropriate IAM permissions are granted (Option D) for Flow Logs to publish to the chosen destination. Finally, analyze the collected flow log data (Option B) using tools like CloudWatch Logs Insights or by querying S3 objects to troubleshoot network traffic.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  60,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_91',
  'An EC2 instance is unable to receive inbound traffic on a specific port. Order the steps a SysOps Administrator should take to troubleshoot the security group configuration.',
  'ordering',
  '["Check the EC2 instance''s security groups to ensure an inbound rule allows traffic on the required port from the correct source IP address or CIDR block.","Verify that the Network ACL (NACL) associated with the instance''s subnet has an inbound rule allowing the traffic and an outbound rule for return traffic.","Confirm that the application on the EC2 instance is listening on the expected port.","Use `telnet` or `netcat` from a client to test connectivity to the instance on the specific port."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'The most direct cause for inbound traffic issues is often the security group. First, check the EC2 instance''s security groups (Option A) to ensure an inbound rule allows traffic on the required port from the correct source. Next, verify the Network ACL (NACL) (Option B) associated with the instance''s subnet, ensuring both inbound and outbound rules permit the traffic, as NACLs are stateless. Then, confirm that the application on the EC2 instance is actually listening on the expected port (Option C). Finally, use `telnet` or `netcat` (Option D) from a client to test connectivity, providing real-time feedback on the port''s reachability.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  61,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_92',
  'An EC2 instance is experiencing issues connecting to external services, indicating a potential outbound connectivity problem. Order the steps to troubleshoot the Network ACL (NACL) configuration.',
  'ordering',
  '["Identify the Network ACL (NACL) associated with the subnet where the EC2 instance resides.","Verify that the NACL has an outbound rule allowing traffic on the required destination port (e.g., 443 for HTTPS) to the external service''s IP range.","Verify that the NACL has an inbound rule allowing the ephemeral ports (1024-65535) for the return traffic from the external service.","Check the route table of the subnet to ensure outbound traffic is directed to the Internet Gateway or NAT Gateway."]'::json,
  '{0,1,3,2}'::int[],
  'To troubleshoot outbound connectivity issues related to NACLs, first, identify the Network ACL (NACL) associated with the instance''s subnet (Option A). Next, verify that the NACL has an explicit outbound rule allowing traffic on the required destination port to the external service (Option B). Before checking return traffic, ensure the subnet''s route table is correctly configured (Option D) to direct outbound traffic to the Internet Gateway or NAT Gateway. Finally, verify that the NACL also has an inbound rule allowing the ephemeral ports (Option C) for the return traffic from the external service, as NACLs are stateless and require explicit rules for both directions.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  62,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_93',
  'A SysOps Administrator needs to analyze the network path between an EC2 instance and an Amazon S3 bucket to diagnose connectivity issues. Order the steps to use AWS Reachability Analyzer for this purpose.',
  'ordering',
  '["Specify the source (e.g., EC2 instance ENI) and destination (e.g., S3 bucket endpoint) for the network path analysis.","Review the analysis results to identify any blocking components such as security groups, Network ACLs, or route table entries.","Initiate the path analysis in the AWS Reachability Analyzer console.","Remediate any identified network configuration issues based on the analysis results."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}'::int[],
  'To use AWS Reachability Analyzer, first, specify the source (e.g., an EC2 instance''s Elastic Network Interface) and the destination (e.g., an Amazon S3 bucket endpoint) for the network path analysis (Option A). Next, initiate the path analysis in the AWS Reachability Analyzer console (Option C). After the analysis completes, review the results (Option B) to identify any blocking components like security groups, Network ACLs, or route table entries. Finally, remediate any identified network configuration issues (Option D) to restore or enable connectivity.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  63,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_94',
  'An application behind an Elastic Load Balancer (ELB) is experiencing intermittent connection failures. Order the steps to use ELB access logs to investigate the issue.',
  'ordering',
  '["Enable access logs for the Elastic Load Balancer, specifying an Amazon S3 bucket as the destination.","Analyze the access logs in Amazon S3 to identify patterns such as high HTTP error codes (e.g., 5xx), high latency, or connection timeouts.","Configure an S3 bucket policy that grants the ELB permission to write logs to the bucket.","Use Amazon Athena or another query tool to efficiently search and filter the large volume of log data."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}'::int[],
  'Before enabling access logs, configure an S3 bucket policy (Option C) that grants the ELB permission to write logs to the bucket. Then, enable access logs for the Elastic Load Balancer (Option A), specifying this S3 bucket as the destination. Once logs are being collected, analyze the access logs in Amazon S3 (Option B) to identify patterns such as high HTTP error codes, high latency, or connection timeouts. For large volumes of data, use Amazon Athena or another query tool (Option D) to efficiently search and filter the log data, making analysis more manageable.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  64,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_95',
  'An EC2 instance in a private subnet cannot reach the internet for critical updates. Order the steps to diagnose and resolve this outbound connectivity issue.',
  'ordering',
  '["Check the private subnet''s route table to ensure a default route (0.0.0.0/0) points to a NAT Gateway or NAT instance.","Verify that the NAT Gateway or NAT instance is running and has a public IP address (Elastic IP) associated with it.","Examine the security groups and Network ACLs associated with the EC2 instance and its subnet to ensure outbound traffic is allowed.","Confirm that the public subnet where the NAT Gateway resides has a route to the Internet Gateway."]'::json,
  '{0,1,3,2}'::int[],
  'The first step is to check the private subnet''s route table (Option A) to confirm that a default route (0.0.0.0/0) exists and correctly points to a NAT Gateway or NAT instance. Next, verify that the NAT Gateway or NAT instance itself is running and has a public IP address (Elastic IP) associated (Option B). Crucially, confirm that the public subnet where the NAT Gateway resides has a route to the Internet Gateway (Option D), allowing the NAT Gateway to reach the internet. Finally, examine the security groups and Network ACLs (Option C) for the EC2 instance and its subnet to ensure outbound traffic is explicitly allowed, as these can block traffic even if routing is correct.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  65,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_96',
  'A CloudFront distribution is serving stale content despite the origin having the latest version. Order the steps to troubleshoot and resolve this caching issue.',
  'ordering',
  '["Verify that the origin (e.g., Amazon S3 bucket) contains the updated content.","Check the CloudFront distribution''s cache behaviors and cache policy settings (e.g., TTLs, forwarded headers, query strings) to ensure they are not caching for too long.","Create an invalidation for the specific paths of the stale content in the CloudFront console.","Test the content delivery after invalidation to confirm the latest version is served."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'First, verify that the origin (e.g., Amazon S3 bucket) contains the updated content (Option A) to ensure the source is correct. Next, check the CloudFront distribution''s cache behaviors and cache policy settings (Option B) to ensure that TTLs (Time To Live) or other caching parameters are not configured to cache content for an excessively long period. If the cache settings are correct but content is still stale, create an invalidation for the specific paths of the stale content (Option C) to force CloudFront to fetch the new version from the origin. Finally, test the content delivery (Option D) to confirm that the latest version is now being served.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  66,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_97',
  'Two EC2 instances in different subnets within the same VPC are unable to communicate. Order the steps to diagnose the network connectivity issue.',
  'ordering',
  '["Check the security groups associated with both EC2 instances to ensure they allow inbound and outbound traffic on the necessary ports and protocols between each other''s IP addresses or security group IDs.","Verify the Network ACLs (NACLs) associated with both subnets, ensuring both inbound and outbound rules permit the traffic flow between the subnets, considering ephemeral ports for return traffic.","Examine the route tables of both subnets to confirm that routes exist for traffic to reach the other subnet''s CIDR block.","Use `ping` or `traceroute` from one instance to the other to test basic network reachability."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}'::int[],
  'When instances in different subnets cannot communicate, the first step is to examine the route tables of both subnets (Option C) to ensure that routes exist for traffic to reach the other subnet''s CIDR block. Next, check the security groups associated with both EC2 instances (Option A) to ensure they allow the necessary inbound and outbound traffic between them. Then, verify the Network ACLs (NACLs) (Option B) for both subnets, ensuring that both inbound and outbound rules permit the traffic, remembering that NACLs are stateless. Finally, use `ping` or `traceroute` (Option D) from one instance to the other to test basic network reachability and path, which can help pinpoint where traffic is being dropped.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  67,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_98',
  'A SysOps Administrator is troubleshooting a Site-to-Site VPN connection that is intermittently failing to establish tunnels. Order the steps to diagnose and resolve common VPN connectivity issues.',
  'ordering',
  '["Verify the Customer Gateway device''s public IP address configured in AWS matches the actual public IP of the on-premises VPN device.","Check the on-premises VPN device''s logs for errors related to tunnel establishment, authentication, or encryption mismatches.","Review the Virtual Private Gateway (VPG) and Customer Gateway configurations in AWS for correct pre-shared keys, IPsec parameters, and routing.","Ensure network connectivity (e.g., ping) exists between the on-premises VPN device''s public IP and the AWS VPG''s public IP."]'::json,
  '{0,3,2,1}'::int[],
  'First, verify that the Customer Gateway device''s public IP address configured in AWS matches the actual public IP of the on-premises VPN device (Option A), as a mismatch will prevent tunnel initiation. Next, ensure basic network connectivity (e.g., ping) exists between the on-premises VPN device''s public IP and the AWS VPG''s public IP (Option D) to rule out basic routing or firewall issues. Then, review the Virtual Private Gateway (VPG) and Customer Gateway configurations in AWS (Option C) for correct pre-shared keys, IPsec parameters, and routing settings. Finally, check the on-premises VPN device''s logs (Option B) for specific errors related to tunnel establishment, authentication, or encryption mismatches, which provide detailed insights into the failure.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  68,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_99',
  'A SysOps Administrator needs to troubleshoot why an EC2 instance is unreachable via SSH, even though its public IP is assigned. Order the steps to systematically investigate the issue.',
  'ordering',
  '["Check the security group attached to the EC2 instance to ensure inbound SSH (port 22) traffic is allowed from your source IP address.","Verify the Network ACL (NACL) associated with the instance''s subnet has inbound rule for port 22 and outbound rule for ephemeral ports (1024-65535).","Confirm the instance''s route table allows traffic to and from the internet via an Internet Gateway.","Use AWS Systems Manager Session Manager to attempt a connection to the instance, bypassing network-level issues."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'The most common reason for SSH unreachability is security group configuration. First, check the security group attached to the EC2 instance (Option A) to ensure inbound SSH (port 22) traffic is allowed from your source IP. Next, verify the Network ACL (NACL) (Option B) associated with the instance''s subnet, ensuring both inbound (port 22) and outbound (ephemeral ports) rules permit the SSH traffic, as NACLs are stateless. Then, confirm the instance''s route table (Option C) allows traffic to and from the internet via an Internet Gateway. Finally, use AWS Systems Manager Session Manager (Option D) as a last resort to attempt a connection, as it can often bypass network-level issues and provide direct access for further diagnostics.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  69,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_100',
  'A SysOps Administrator is investigating why an Elastic Load Balancer (ELB) is marking backend EC2 instances as unhealthy. Order the steps to troubleshoot the ELB health check configuration.',
  'ordering',
  '["Check the ELB''s health check configuration, including protocol, port, path, healthy threshold, unhealthy threshold, and timeout settings.","Verify that the application on the backend EC2 instances is running and listening on the port specified in the health check.","Examine the security groups of the ELB and the backend EC2 instances to ensure health check traffic is allowed.","Review CloudWatch metrics for the ELB and EC2 instances (e.g., HealthyHostCount, UnHealthyHostCount, CPU utilization) for anomalies."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}'::int[],
  'The first step is to check the ELB''s health check configuration (Option A), ensuring that the protocol, port, path, thresholds, and timeout settings are correctly defined and match the application''s expected behavior. Next, examine the security groups of both the ELB and the backend EC2 instances (Option C) to ensure that health check traffic is allowed to pass between them. Then, verify that the application on the backend EC2 instances is running and listening on the port specified in the health check (Option B). Finally, review CloudWatch metrics (Option D) for the ELB and EC2 instances to identify any trends or anomalies in host health or resource utilization that might correlate with the unhealthy status.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  70,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'medium'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_101',
  'A critical web server running on an Amazon EC2 instance is suddenly unreachable from the internet. The operations team needs to quickly identify the root cause of this connectivity loss. Order the steps to diagnose why an EC2 instance cannot be reached from the internet.',
  'ordering',
  '["Check the Security Groups associated with the EC2 instance to ensure inbound traffic on the required ports (e.g., 80, 443, 22) is allowed from the internet.","Verify the Network ACLs associated with the subnet hosting the EC2 instance, ensuring both inbound and outbound rules permit the necessary traffic.","Examine the route table of the EC2 instance''s subnet to confirm a default route to an Internet Gateway exists.","Confirm the EC2 instance has a public IP address or is associated with an Elastic IP address, and that the Internet Gateway is attached to the VPC."]'::json,
  '{3,2,0,1}'::int[],
  'Diagnosing why an EC2 instance is unreachable from the internet requires checking network components in a logical order. Option D (Confirm the EC2 instance has a public IP address or is associated with an Elastic IP address, and that the Internet Gateway is attached to the VPC) is the foundational check, as without these, internet access is impossible. Option C (Examine the route table of the EC2 instance''s subnet to confirm a default route to an Internet Gateway exists) follows, ensuring traffic can be routed out of the VPC. Option A (Check the Security Groups associated with the EC2 instance to ensure inbound traffic on the required ports (e.g., 80, 443, 22) is allowed from the internet) addresses the instance-level firewall. Finally, Option B (Verify the Network ACLs associated with the subnet hosting the EC2 instance, ensuring both inbound and outbound rules permit the necessary traffic) checks the subnet-level firewall, paying close attention to the stateless nature of NACLs and ephemeral ports.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  71,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_102',
  'A security team needs to monitor and analyze all IP traffic flowing in and out of their Amazon VPCs to detect anomalies, identify unauthorized communication, and troubleshoot connectivity issues. They require detailed logs for forensic analysis. Order the steps to correctly enable and analyze VPC Flow Logs for network traffic visibility.',
  'ordering',
  '["Create an Amazon S3 bucket or an Amazon CloudWatch Logs log group to store the flow logs.","Create a Flow Log for the VPC, subnet, or Elastic Network Interface (ENI), specifying the destination (S3 or CloudWatch Logs) and the log format.","Ensure the IAM role used by Flow Logs has the necessary permissions to publish logs to the chosen destination.","Analyze the collected flow logs using Amazon Athena (for S3) or CloudWatch Logs Insights (for CloudWatch Logs) to gain insights into network traffic patterns."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}'::int[],
  'Enabling and analyzing VPC Flow Logs involves a specific sequence. Option A (Create an Amazon S3 bucket or an Amazon CloudWatch Logs log group to store the flow logs) is the initial step, as a destination for the logs must exist. Option C (Ensure the IAM role used by Flow Logs has the necessary permissions to publish logs to the chosen destination) is crucial for granting Flow Logs the ability to write to the destination. Option B (Create a Flow Log for the VPC, subnet, or Elastic Network Interface (ENI), specifying the destination (S3 or CloudWatch Logs) and the log format) then configures the actual logging. Finally, Option D (Analyze the collected flow logs using Amazon Athena (for S3) or CloudWatch Logs Insights (for CloudWatch Logs) to gain insights into network traffic patterns) is the step for deriving value from the logs.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  72,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_103',
  'An operations engineer needs to verify the network path between an EC2 instance in a private subnet and an Amazon S3 bucket''s Gateway VPC Endpoint. They want to confirm that traffic is indeed flowing privately and not attempting to reach S3 over the internet. Order the steps to use AWS Reachability Analyzer for connectivity path analysis.',
  'ordering',
  '["Navigate to the VPC console and select ''Reachability Analyzer''.","Create a new path analysis, specifying the source (EC2 instance ENI) and destination (S3 Gateway VPC Endpoint).","Run the analysis and review the results to confirm reachability and the network path taken.","Identify any blocking components (e.g., security groups, NACLs, route tables) if the path is unreachable."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'Using AWS Reachability Analyzer to confirm network path analysis follows a direct sequence. Option A (Navigate to the VPC console and select ''Reachability Analyzer'') is the initial access step. Option B (Create a new path analysis, specifying the source (EC2 instance ENI) and destination (S3 Gateway VPC Endpoint)) defines the specific network path to be analyzed. Option C (Run the analysis and review the results to confirm reachability and the network path taken) executes the analysis and provides the outcome. Finally, Option D (Identify any blocking components (e.g., security groups, NACLs, route tables) if the path is unreachable) is the troubleshooting step based on the analysis results.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  73,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_104',
  'A web application served by an Elastic Load Balancer (ELB) is experiencing intermittent 5xx errors and dropped connections. Users report slow loading times and occasional timeouts. The operations team needs to investigate the health and performance of the backend instances and the ELB itself. Order the steps to troubleshoot an ELB experiencing connection issues.',
  'ordering',
  '["Check the Amazon CloudWatch metrics for the ELB (e.g., HealthyHostCount, HTTPCode_Target_5XX_Count, Latency) and its target groups.","Review the ELB access logs for detailed information on requests, responses, and errors.","Examine the health checks configured for the target group and verify the health status of registered instances.","Inspect the security groups of the ELB and the backend instances to ensure traffic is allowed on the correct ports."]'::json,
  '{0,2,3,1}'::int[],
  'Troubleshooting an Elastic Load Balancer experiencing connection issues requires a systematic approach. Option A (Check the Amazon CloudWatch metrics for the ELB (e.g., HealthyHostCount, HTTPCode_Target_5XX_Count, Latency) and its target groups) is the initial step to get an overview of the ELB''s health and performance. Option C (Examine the health checks configured for the target group and verify the health status of registered instances) follows to determine if backend instances are failing health checks. Option D (Inspect the security groups of the ELB and the backend instances to ensure traffic is allowed on the correct ports) is crucial for ensuring network connectivity. Finally, Option B (Review the ELB access logs for detailed information on requests, responses, and errors) provides granular data for deeper analysis after initial checks.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  74,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_105',
  'A global content delivery network (CDN) using Amazon CloudFront is reported to be serving outdated content to users despite updates being made at the origin. The operations team needs to diagnose and resolve this caching issue. Order the steps to identify and resolve issues with a CloudFront distribution not serving the latest content.',
  'ordering',
  '["Verify that the origin content (e.g., Amazon S3 bucket) has been updated with the latest version of the files.","Check the CloudFront distribution''s cache behaviors to ensure the TTL (Time To Live) settings are appropriate and not excessively long.","Create an invalidation request for the specific paths of the outdated content in the CloudFront console.","Monitor the invalidation status and then test accessing the content to confirm the latest version is being served."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'Resolving CloudFront caching issues requires a methodical approach. Option A (Verify that the origin content (e.g., Amazon S3 bucket) has been updated with the latest version of the files) is the first logical step, ensuring the source content is indeed current. Option B (Check the CloudFront distribution''s cache behaviors to ensure the TTL (Time To Live) settings are appropriate and not excessively long) follows, as long TTLs can cause stale content. Option C (Create an invalidation request for the specific paths of the outdated content in the CloudFront console) is the direct action to force CloudFront to fetch new content. Finally, Option D (Monitor the invalidation status and then test accessing the content to confirm the latest version is being served) is the crucial validation step.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  75,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_106',
  'Two Amazon EC2 instances, `Instance A` and `Instance B`, are located in different subnets within the same Amazon VPC. An application on `Instance A` cannot establish a connection to a service running on `Instance B`. The operations team needs to troubleshoot this inter-instance connectivity issue. Order the steps to systematically diagnose the problem.',
  'ordering',
  '["Check the Security Groups attached to both `Instance A` and `Instance B` to ensure they allow inbound and outbound traffic on the necessary ports and protocols between each other.","Examine the Network ACLs associated with the subnets of both `Instance A` and `Instance B` to ensure they permit both inbound and outbound traffic on the required ports, including ephemeral ports for return traffic.","Verify the route tables associated with the subnets of `Instance A` and `Instance B` to confirm that routes exist for traffic to reach each other''s subnets within the VPC.","Use `ping` or `telnet` from `Instance A` to `Instance B` to test basic network reachability and port accessibility."]'::json,
  '{2,0,1,3}'::int[],
  'Troubleshooting connectivity between two EC2 instances in different subnets within the same VPC requires a structured approach. Option C (Verify the route tables associated with the subnets of `Instance A` and `Instance B` to confirm that routes exist for traffic to reach each other''s subnets within the VPC) is the initial step, ensuring the network path is correctly defined. Option A (Check the Security Groups attached to both `Instance A` and `Instance B` to ensure they allow inbound and outbound traffic on the necessary ports and protocols between each other) follows, addressing instance-level firewall rules. Option B (Examine the Network ACLs associated with the subnets of both `Instance A` and `Instance B` to ensure they permit both inbound and outbound traffic on the required ports, including ephemeral ports for return traffic) is critical for subnet-level firewall rules, especially considering NACLs are stateless. Finally, Option D (Use `ping` or `telnet` from `Instance A` to `Instance B` to test basic network reachability and port accessibility) is a practical diagnostic test after verifying configurations.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  76,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_107',
  'A hybrid cloud environment relies on an AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection to link an on-premises data center with an Amazon VPC. The operations team receives alerts that the VPN tunnel is down, impacting critical business applications. Order the steps to systematically diagnose why a Site-to-Site VPN connection is down.',
  'ordering',
  '["Check the status of the VPN tunnels in the AWS Management Console for the Site-to-Site VPN connection.","Verify the configuration of the Customer Gateway device on-premises, ensuring IP addresses, pre-shared keys, and tunnel settings match the AWS configuration.","Review the Virtual Private Gateway (VGW) configuration in AWS, ensuring it''s attached to the correct VPC and its route propagation is enabled.","Examine the on-premises firewall and network devices to ensure they are not blocking UDP ports 500 and 4500 (IKE and IPSec NAT traversal) to and from the AWS VPN endpoints."]'::json,
  '{0,2,1,3}'::int[],
  'Diagnosing a down AWS Site-to-Site VPN connection requires checking both AWS and on-premises components. Option A (Check the status of the VPN tunnels in the AWS Management Console for the Site-to-Site VPN connection) is the immediate first step to confirm the reported status. Option C (Review the Virtual Private Gateway (VGW) configuration in AWS, ensuring it''s attached to the correct VPC and its route propagation is enabled) follows, verifying the AWS side of the connection. Option B (Verify the configuration of the Customer Gateway device on-premises, ensuring IP addresses, pre-shared keys, and tunnel settings match the AWS configuration) is crucial for checking the on-premises VPN device''s settings against AWS''s. Finally, Option D (Examine the on-premises firewall and network devices to ensure they are not blocking UDP ports 500 and 4500 (IKE and IPSec NAT traversal) to and from the AWS VPN endpoints) addresses common firewall-related issues that can prevent tunnel establishment.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  77,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_26_m_26_3_108',
  'An application deployed in an Amazon VPC is experiencing intermittent failures when trying to resolve internal domain names for other services within the same VPC. The operations team suspects a DNS resolution issue. Order the steps to systematically troubleshoot DNS resolution issues for an application hosted in a VPC.',
  'ordering',
  '["Verify that the VPC has DNS resolution and DNS hostnames enabled in its attributes.","Check the EC2 instance''s `/etc/resolv.conf` (Linux) or network adapter settings (Windows) to ensure it''s configured to use the VPC''s DNS resolver (the .2 IP address of the VPC CIDR).","Confirm that the hosted zone in Amazon Route 53 contains the correct record sets for the internal domain names.","Examine security groups and Network ACLs to ensure they allow outbound DNS queries (UDP/TCP port 53) from the instance to the VPC DNS resolver and inbound responses."]'::json,
  '{0,1,2,3}'::int[],
  'Troubleshooting DNS resolution issues in a VPC requires a methodical check of DNS-related configurations. Option A (Verify that the VPC has DNS resolution and DNS hostnames enabled in its attributes) is the foundational check, as these settings are prerequisites for DNS to function within the VPC. Option B (Check the EC2 instance''s `/etc/resolv.conf` (Linux) or network adapter settings (Windows) to ensure it''s configured to use the VPC''s DNS resolver (the .2 IP address of the VPC CIDR)) follows, ensuring the instance is pointing to the correct DNS server. Option C (Confirm that the hosted zone in Amazon Route 53 contains the correct record sets for the internal domain names) is crucial for verifying the actual DNS records. Finally, Option D (Examine security groups and Network ACLs to ensure they allow outbound DNS queries (UDP/TCP port 53) from the instance to the VPC DNS resolver and inbound responses) addresses potential firewall blocks preventing DNS traffic.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain26_5',
  NOW(),
  78,
  NULL,
  NULL,
  'm_26_3',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

