  '{"A":"Elastic Load Balancing Health Checks","B":"Amazon RDS Multi-AZ","C":"Amazon EFS","D":"Route 53 Failover Routing Policy"}'::json,
  NULL,
  'Elastic Load Balancing Health Checks (left item 0) are crucial for detecting unhealthy instances in a target group and automatically routing traffic away from them, ensuring application availability. Amazon RDS Multi-AZ (left item 1) provides synchronous replication of your database to a standby instance in a different Availability Zone, enabling automatic failover and enhancing database resilience. Amazon EFS (left item 2) offers shared, highly available, and durable file storage that can be accessed concurrently by EC2 instances across multiple Availability Zones. Route 53 Failover Routing Policy (left item 3), when combined with health checks, automatically redirects user traffic to a healthy alternative endpoint in case of a primary endpoint failure, ensuring continuous service availability.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain23_5',
  NOW(),
  39,
  '{"left":["Elastic Load Balancing Health Checks","Amazon RDS Multi-AZ","Amazon EFS","Route 53 Failover Routing Policy"],"right":["Detects unhealthy backend instances","Provides synchronous database replication","Offers shared, highly available file storage","Redirects traffic to healthy endpoints"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2,3],"right":[0,1,2,3]}'::jsonb,
  'm_23_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_23_m_23_2_97',
  'A global e-commerce platform requires a highly available DNS solution with automatic failover capabilities. Match the Route 53 feature to its role in achieving this resilience.',
  'matching',
  '{"A":"Route 53 Health Checks","B":"Route 53 Failover Routing Policy","C":"Route 53 Latency-based Routing","D":"Route 53 Weighted Routing Policy"}'::json,
  NULL,
  'Route 53 Health Checks (left item 0) are essential for monitoring the health and availability of endpoints, such as EC2 instances or application servers. Route 53 Failover Routing Policy (left item 1) is used to automatically switch DNS queries from an unhealthy primary endpoint to a healthy secondary endpoint, ensuring continuous availability. Route 53 Latency-based Routing (left item 2) routes user requests to the AWS Region that provides the lowest latency for the user, improving performance. Route 53 Weighted Routing Policy (left item 3) allows you to distribute traffic to multiple resources in proportions that you specify, useful for A/B testing or gradual rollouts.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain23_5',
  NOW(),
  40,
  '{"left":["Route 53 Health Checks","Route 53 Failover Routing Policy","Route 53 Latency-based Routing","Route 53 Weighted Routing Policy"],"right":["Monitors endpoint availability","Switches to secondary endpoint on failure","Routes users to lowest latency region","Distributes traffic based on specified weights"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2,3],"right":[0,1,2,3]}'::jsonb,
  'm_23_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_23_m_23_2_98',
  'A development team is deploying a new microservices application that requires shared storage and high availability across Availability Zones. Match the storage service to its characteristic that supports this requirement.',
  'matching',
  '{"A":"Amazon EFS","B":"Amazon S3","C":"Amazon EBS","D":"Amazon RDS"}'::json,
  NULL,
  'Amazon EFS (left item 0) provides a simple, scalable, elastic file system for Linux-based workloads for use with AWS Cloud services and on-premises resources, supporting shared access across multiple EC2 instances in different Availability Zones. Amazon S3 (left item 1) is object storage known for its high durability and availability, but it''s not a shared file system for EC2 instances. Amazon EBS (left item 2) provides block storage volumes for use with EC2 instances, typically attached to a single instance in a single AZ, though snapshots can be copied. Amazon RDS (left item 3) is a managed relational database service, not a general-purpose shared file system.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain23_5',
  NOW(),
  41,
  '{"left":["Amazon EFS","Amazon S3","Amazon EBS","Amazon RDS"],"right":["Shared file system across AZs","Object storage with high durability","Block storage for single EC2 instance","Managed relational database"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2,3],"right":[0,1,2,3]}'::jsonb,
  'm_23_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_23_m_23_2_99',
  'A critical application needs to maintain high availability and resilience against Availability Zone failures. Match the AWS service deployment pattern to its primary mechanism for achieving this.',
  'matching',
  '{"A":"EC2 Auto Scaling Group across AZs","B":"Amazon RDS Multi-AZ Deployment","C":"Amazon ElastiCache Multi-AZ","D":"Amazon EFS"}'::json,
  NULL,
  'An EC2 Auto Scaling Group across AZs (left item 0) distributes instances across multiple Availability Zones, ensuring that if one AZ becomes unavailable, the application remains operational. Amazon RDS Multi-AZ Deployment (left item 1) provides synchronous replication of your database to a standby instance in a different Availability Zone, enabling automatic failover and enhancing database resilience. Amazon ElastiCache Multi-AZ (left item 2) with automatic failover ensures that if a primary node fails, a replica is promoted to primary, maintaining cache availability. Amazon EFS (left item 3) provides a shared file system that is accessible from EC2 instances across multiple Availability Zones, offering high availability for file-based workloads.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain23_5',
  NOW(),
  42,
  '{"left":["EC2 Auto Scaling Group across AZs","Amazon RDS Multi-AZ Deployment","Amazon ElastiCache Multi-AZ","Amazon EFS"],"right":["Distributes instances for fault tolerance","Synchronous standby replica for failover","Automatic node replacement and failover","Shared file system accessible from multiple AZs"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2,3],"right":[0,1,2,3]}'::jsonb,
  'm_23_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_23_m_23_2_100',
  'A company is building a highly available web application using EC2 instances. Match the Elastic Load Balancing feature to its contribution in ensuring application resilience and performance.',
  'matching',
  '{"A":"Cross-Zone Load Balancing","B":"Health Checks","C":"Sticky Sessions","D":"Connection Draining"}'::json,
  NULL,
  'Cross-Zone Load Balancing (left item 0) ensures that traffic is distributed evenly across all registered instances in all enabled Availability Zones, improving fault tolerance and resource utilization. Health Checks (left item 1) are critical for Elastic Load Balancing to monitor the health of registered targets and automatically route traffic only to healthy instances. Sticky Sessions (left item 2), also known as session affinity, ensure that requests from a user during a session are routed to the same target instance, which can be important for applications that store session state locally. Connection Draining (left item 3) allows an Elastic Load Balancer to complete in-flight requests from a deregistering or unhealthy instance before it is fully removed, preventing abrupt disconnections for users.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain23_5',
  NOW(),
  43,
  '{"left":["Cross-Zone Load Balancing","Health Checks","Sticky Sessions","Connection Draining"],"right":["Distributes traffic evenly across AZs","Monitors target instance availability","Routes user to same instance for session","Allows requests to complete before deregistration"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2,3],"right":[0,1,2,3]}'::jsonb,
  'm_23_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_23_m_23_2_101',
  'A company is designing a highly available architecture for its application that uses a shared file system. Match the AWS service to its characteristic that ensures resilience across Availability Zones.',
  'matching',
  '{"A":"Amazon EFS","B":"Amazon S3","C":"Amazon EBS","D":"Amazon RDS Multi-AZ"}'::json,
  NULL,
  'Amazon EFS (left item 0) automatically replicates data across multiple Availability Zones within a region, providing high availability and durability for shared file systems. Amazon S3 (left item 1) is object storage that inherently provides high durability by storing data redundantly across multiple devices in multiple Availability Zones. Amazon EBS (left item 2) volumes are zonal resources; they are tied to a single Availability Zone and require snapshots for cross-AZ or cross-region disaster recovery. Amazon RDS Multi-AZ (left item 3) provides a highly available relational database by synchronously replicating data to a standby instance in a different Availability Zone.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain23_5',
  NOW(),
  44,
  '{"left":["Amazon EFS","Amazon S3","Amazon EBS","Amazon RDS Multi-AZ"],"right":["Automatically replicates data across multiple AZs","Object storage with high durability across AZs","Single-AZ block storage, requires snapshots for DR","Database with synchronous replication across AZs"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2,3],"right":[0,1,2,3]}'::jsonb,
  'm_23_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_23_m_23_2_102',
  'A critical application needs to be resilient to regional outages. Match the AWS service feature to its role in a multi-region disaster recovery strategy.',
  'matching',
  '{"A":"S3 Cross-Region Replication","B":"Route 53 Geoproximity Routing","C":"DynamoDB Global Tables","D":"AWS Backup Cross-Region Copy"}'::json,
  NULL,
  'S3 Cross-Region Replication (left item 0) asynchronously copies objects between S3 buckets in different AWS Regions, providing a robust solution for data durability and disaster recovery. Route 53 Geoproximity Routing (left item 1) allows you to route traffic to resources based on the geographic location of your users and your resources, and optionally shift traffic from resources in one location to resources in another. DynamoDB Global Tables (left item 2) provide a fully managed, multi-region, multi-master database, enabling active-active replication for high availability and low-latency access globally. AWS Backup Cross-Region Copy (left item 3) allows you to copy backups to a secondary AWS Region, enhancing disaster recovery capabilities for various services managed by AWS Backup.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain23_5',
  NOW(),
  45,
  '{"left":["S3 Cross-Region Replication","Route 53 Geoproximity Routing","DynamoDB Global Tables","AWS Backup Cross-Region Copy"],"right":["Asynchronous object data replication","Routes traffic based on resource location","Multi-region active-active database","Copies backups to a secondary region"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2,3],"right":[0,1,2,3]}'::jsonb,
  'm_23_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

INSERT INTO public.question (id, text, type, options, correct_answer, explanation, created_at, quiz_id, modified_at, index, pairs, matches, module_id, difficulty)
VALUES (
  'q_23_m_23_2_103',
  'A company is building a new application and needs to ensure its infrastructure can automatically recover from common failures. Match the AWS service feature to its role in automated recovery.',
  'matching',
  '{"A":"EC2 Auto Scaling Health Checks","B":"Elastic Load Balancing Health Checks","C":"Route 53 Health Checks","D":"RDS Multi-AZ Automatic Failover"}'::json,
  NULL,
  'EC2 Auto Scaling Health Checks (left item 0) monitor the health of instances within an Auto Scaling group and automatically terminate and replace unhealthy instances. Elastic Load Balancing Health Checks (left item 1) monitor the health of registered targets and ensure that traffic is only routed to healthy instances. Route 53 Health Checks (left item 2), when associated with routing policies, can update DNS records to direct traffic away from unhealthy endpoints. RDS Multi-AZ Automatic Failover (left item 3) automatically switches database operations to a healthy standby replica in a different Availability Zone in the event of a primary database failure.',
  NOW(),
  'quiz_domain23_5',
  NOW(),
  46,
  '{"left":["EC2 Auto Scaling Health Checks","Elastic Load Balancing Health Checks","Route 53 Health Checks","RDS Multi-AZ Automatic Failover"],"right":["Replaces unhealthy EC2 instances","Routes traffic away from unhealthy targets","Updates DNS records for unhealthy endpoints","Switches to standby database replica"]}'::json,
  '{"left":[0,1,2,3],"right":[0,1,2,3]}'::jsonb,
  'm_23_2',
  'hard'
) ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;

